      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>TIT-BITS. A TERRIBLE ORDEAL.</head>
        <p>TIT-BITS. A TERRIBLE ORDEAL.</p>
        <p>An officer of the French army, during the reign of Napoleon, having incurred the suspicion or resent- ment of the emperor, thought it expedient to abandon his country, and take refuge in one of the Austrian provinces, and here he became advised of and initiated into a society, the object of whose formation was to hurl to the ground the Colossus whose arm smote and governed the whole continent of Europe with a scepter of iron. One day a letter was brought to him containing the usual signs and passwords of the society, and requiring him to repair, on the following night, to a secluded spot In a forest, where he would meet some of his associates. He went, but he found nobody. The orders were repeated four times. The officer sought the appointed place with no better success than the first. 0 , the firth ntght of his appearance at the rendewous, after Waiting some time, be was on the point of returning, when loud cries suddenly arrested his attention.</p>
        <p>Drawing his sword, he hastened to the spot whence they seemed to proceed, and was fired upon by three msn, who, on seeing that he remained unwounded, instantly took to flight ; but at his feet. lay a bleeding corpse, in which, by the feeble light ef the moon, he in vain sought for tokens of return- ing animation. He was yet bending over the dead man, when a detachment of chasseurs, summoned epparently by the noise of the pietats that had been discharged at himself, came up suddenly and arrested him as the assassin. He was loaded with ehains, tried the next day, and condemned to die for his supposed crime. His execution was ordered to take place at midnight. Surrounded by the miMsters ef justice, he was led at a slow pace, by tile light of torches, and the funeral tolling of bells, to n vast square, in the corner of which was a scaffold environed by horsemen. Beyond these wae a numerous group of spectators who muttered imputiently, and at intervals sent forth a cry of abnorrence.</p>
        <p>The victim mounted the scaffold, and Ws sentence was read, and the first act of the tragedy was or the po nt of fulfillment, when an cffier let fall a word of hope. An edict bad just been promulgated by the government offering a pardon and life to any oonderaned criminal who khould discloses the mem- bers and secret tokens of a particular association, the existence of which the Frenchman to whom these words were addressed had lately become a aware of, and of which he had become a member. He was questioned, but he denied all knowledge. Teey urged him to confess, with promises of ad- ditional reward. His only reply was a demand for immediate death—and his initiation was completed. All that p t:sed was a terrible trial of fidelity. Those who surroundeded him were members of the society, and evary incident that has been described, from the SUM, II1')129 to the last moment of exoected death, was only a step in the progress of the fearful ordeal by .which society sought to determine the trustworthiness of the neopbyte.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>MINISTER AND HIS SAUSAGES.</head>
        <p>THE MINISTER AND HIS SAUSAGES.</p>
        <p>A minister in one of our orthodox churches, while on his way to preach a funeral sermon in the country, called to see one of his members, an old widow lady, who lived near the road he was travel- ling. The old lady had just been making sausages, and she felt proud of them—they were so plump, round, and sweet. Of course, she insisted on her minister taking some of the lines home to his family. He objected on account of his not having his port- manteau along with him. Tae objection was over- ruled, and the old lady, after wrapping them in a rag, carefully placed a handle in either pocket of the preacher's capacious great coat. Thus equipped, he started for the funeral. While attending the solemn ceremonies of the grave, some hungry dogs scented the sausages, and were not long in flack:lag them to the pockets of the good man's overcoat. Of course this was a great annoyance, and he was several times under the necessity of kicking these whelps away. The obsequies of the grave completed, the minister and congregation re-passed to the church, whei e the funeral discourse was to be preached. After the service was finished, the minister halted to make some remarks to his congregation, when a brother who wished to have an appointment given out ascended the steps of the pulpit, and gave the minister's coat a hitch to got his attention. The divine, thinking it a dog having desiens upon his pocket, raised his foot, gave a sudden kick, and ' sent the gODd brother sprawling down the steps "You will excuse me, brethren and sisters," said the minister confusedly, and without looking at the work he had just done," for I could not avoid it- -1 have sausages in my pockets, and that dog has been trying to grab them ever since he came upon the premises 1"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>BENEVOLENT SINGER.</head>
        <p>A BENEVOLENT SINGER.</p>
        <p>The principti singer of the great theatre at Lyons one day observed a poor woman, with her four children, begging in the street. her decent and respectable appearance, in the midst of extreme poverty, interested the kind-hearted vocalist. He desired the poor woman to follow him into the Palace Ballour, where, placing himself in a corner, with his back to the wall, his head covered with his handkerchief, and hl a hat at his feet, he began to sing his most favourite owra airs. The beauty of his voice drew a crowd round; the idea of some mystery stimulated the bystanders, and five franc pieces fell in showers into the hat. When the si/ ger, who tad tt,119, in toe goodness of his hesrt, trim s.eFme,d himl into a street-micstrel, thought he had got enough, he teok up the hat, emptied its contents into the apron ot the poor woman, who stood motionless with sm.izement and happiness, and &amp;appeared amo• g the crowd. His talent, however, betrayed him, though his face was concealed; the stiry spreail, and the next evening, when he appeared on the stage, shouts of applause from all parts of the house proved that a good action is never thrown away.</p>
        <p>THE 119th Psalm, that meditation which, with sweet mouotony, strikss ever the golden string deep buried in the human heart, a string implying by its strange susceptibilities the reality of a music not of this world, yet harmonising all worlds in one I There is no poetry, there is little rhyilica, there is no in- tellectual insight, there is no comprehensive philosophy in the gentle life that yearns and pleads through those undying words Bus there is not one verse which does not tell of a man to whom tie Infinite Power was a living presence and a con- stant inspiration.—Mystery ot Matter. THE mere lapse of years is not life. To eat and drink and sleep—to be exta esd to darkness and the light—to pass round in the mill of habit, and turn thought into aa implement of trade—this is not life. The of mirth which vibratos through the heart; the tears which threaten the dry wastes within ; the rtio,ic which brings childhood back; the prayer that e.ills the future near; the doubt that mskts us meditate; the death that startles us with mystery; the hardship which forces us to struggle ; the anxiety that, ends in trait—are the true nourishment of onr natural being.</p>
        <p>Tuxu li a touching story told of an illiterate faint named Panbos. Being unable to read, be -came to some one to he taught a psalm. Baying learnt the verso, "I said I will take heed to my ways that I offend not with my tongue," he went away saying. that WAS enough if it were practically acquired. Men asked six months after why he had not come to learn anottair psAco, he answered that he had not yet been able to master what he had learned already. Sams; one has said that boasting of what you will au is Ra unwise as to advertise your prosperity. It your plan* are good ones, some one else will catch them up and be in thu field in time to divide the advantage with you. If they are not good, you may be certain no one will yrAnt out the errors in them, so that you cabno!. possibly gain aught by your contrnr.nutativeue.os. The men who listen well, and are not in hose to impart their own secrets, are ./..a Jana= who uenerallv get :dons.; in the world.</p>
        <p>TEOsE that are carping and finding fault with the small failings of others generally do so to distract their attention from recognising greater ones in themselves. The moat in their neighbour's eye is plainly visible, though their vision is distorted with the beam in their own.</p>
        <p>IT is a great mistake to suppose that the best work of the world is done by people of great strength and great opportunities. It is unquestionably an advantage to have both these things, hut neither of them is a necessity to the man who has the spirit and the pluck to achieve great results. Some of the greatest work of our time has been done by men of physical feebleness.</p>
      </div>
