      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>" CAPITAL " CHAT.</head>
        <p>" CAPITAL " CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Hulce of Newcastle's speciality in amateur photegraphy is to secure portraits of rare wild animal* in their native surroundings. He is travelling in quest ot these with Gambier Bolton, a member of the Royal Geographical Society, and well-known as one of the most expert amateur photographers of animals in the world. Toe two i.rroveeded from the World's Fair to California, where one of their chief objects is to photograph the big sea lions on the cliffs,</p>
        <p>Cornelius Vanderbilt, one of the American million- aire., is having fitted Op the most palatial train ever built, and cost is not to be lonsidered. There will be eight cars and an engine that is expected to beat all records, it being Ir. Vanderbilt's intention to make this train the fastest in the world. It will carry to Chicago, besides the Vanderbilt family, a score of members of the English nobility.</p>
        <p>F. 8. Jackson, the Cambridge captain, was born at Chapel Allerton, Yorkshire, on 21st, Nov., 1870. Ile is the son of the late Secretary tor Ireland, and one of the finest all round cricketers of the present day. A geod story is t'old of Jackson, jun. It was in the first year that he obtained his Blue and immediately after the inter-Tarsity ,inatcli, in which he bore a conspicuous part, ~The youngster was being congratulatea all round on his tine per- formance, when he replied, "-Yes, I am pleased that I did so well—not so much fur my own sake you know, but because it will give the Guv'itor such a lift." No doubt; the " G uit'uor " has got a lift since these words were spoken,but it is doubtful whether ,it was due to the cricketing eureer of the young :Cantab.</p>
        <p>As far as I can gather,the naval men in the House or Commons are disposeu to acquit Admiral Mark- ham of any blame for the collision between the Camt,erdown and the Victoria. In their opinion he went as tar as he could have been expected to do in the way of disobedience to orders when he indi- cated his hesitation to his chief. -After the second Biannl he WaS bound to assume that Admiral Tryon had some 'Special 'motive in -View, and to do as be was ordered. Suppose he had rtfused and the manceuvre had been effected safely lie would have been liable to be court-marshalled for disobedience. As to the action of AdmiralTryon liimself,no naval man can explain it except upon the assumption that for the moment his judgment Iced absolutely failed him. He had been on the sick list, and pos- sibly his illness may have.effected his brain. Some other explanation may. be forthcoming when the formal inquiry is entereilbpon,bUtin the face of.t he statements already made no one appears to be able to assign any possible rnotiveLfor the orders given. Admiral Tryon is known to have been fend of bold Illst.ceuvres, but it is not considered that the move- ment ordered actually could in the rational judg- ment of any experienced sailor have appeared pos- sible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>I laSli WIT.</head>
        <p>I laSli WIT.</p>
        <p>There are two kinds of Irish 'wit ; the intentional and the unintentienal. 01 this latter sort there is an absolutely, limitless supply, afforded by the redundancy ot the metaphor and illustration common to the national mind, and productive ol a le4irdities and uCI t;oles de- 'ligliti ul to study. 'Like the peroration, for instance of a clergyman describing the joyful death of the Chris- tian : . " My brethren, he leaps into the very arms of death, and makes his hollow jaws ring with eternal hallelujahs " And the destiny of the wicked : " When death, with its quiver full of arrows, mows them down with the besom of destruction." A poor-law guardiau in the North of Ireland very lately surpassed these flowers of rhetoric when he addressed his audience : " Mr. Chairmen, and Gentlemen of the Board !—The eyes ot Europe are upon us. The apple of discord has been flung in our midst, and if it be not nipped in the bud, it will borst into a conflagration, which will de- luge the world T. " At all times the richness of Irish phraseology borders on the grotesque. An English squire would be amazed at receiving the good wishes expressed once to our knowledge to an Irish one: "Long lire to your honour for ever, and long tress you reign in splendour -" or a petition addressed to the writer's father, Couched in these words: "As I am shortly going to make my exit from this Vale of Tears, I hope your honour will send me two-and-sixpence by return of post." Thr. queer ideas which enter the fertile brains of the fliberni- ans are stall times sufficiently astonishing. A school of poor children having read in their chapter in the Bible the denunciations against hypocrites who "Strain at a gnat and swallow a camel,' were afterwards examined by the benevolent patroness, Lady E—,as to their recol- lectious of the chapter, '6 What, hi particular VMS the sin of the Pharisees, children ? " said the 'atty., A iting camels, my lady," was the prompt reply.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>FATAL ACCIDENT TO AN EXCURSION</head>
        <p>FATAL ACCIDENT TO AN EXCURSION</p>
        <p>TRAIN.</p>
        <p>A fearful railway accident occurred on the joint line of the London and INorth-Western and Lancashire and Yoikshire Companies at Poulton-le-Fylde on Saturday night. The North Western Excursion train train Stock- port left Blackpool on its homeward journey about eleven o'clock. It consisted of four carriages, containing about fifty passengers. On rounding the, curve at r:oulton junction the engine ran off the rails, and after tearing along the permanent way rolled over upon its side: The first carriage was flung upon the engine, the second stood on end against a coal wagon in the siding, the third was completely smashed, and the fourth carriage was also broken. Cornelius Ridgway, the driver, was pitched Li; on his head, and picked up dead. James Marl;, a Wigan joiner, anti James Beall.. a youth of Wigan, two passengers, were also killed. the death or another passenger, Charles Gee, of Wigan was reported later. No fewer than thirty passengers were injured, and among these were many members of the St. Mary's Catholic choir from Wigan and several persons from Mar.- cheater. The line was blocked for some hours, and a number of excursion trains were delayed, nne running • to Kidderminster not quitting Blackpool till nearly three o'clock on Sunday morning.</p>
        <p>'area lives only, it has been ascertained, were lost in the railway disaster near Blackpool on Saturday night. The report that Charles Gee, of Wigan, was among the killed was fortunately incorrect. The news of the accident caused a great sensation not only in Blackpool but also throughout Lancashire. Ches lii re, and Yorkshire, and the condition of the lino at Poulton was the subject of much comment. The officials of the Loadon and. North-Western and of the Lancashire and Yorkshire Railway Companies expressed their sympathy with the sufferers on Monday, aid alsO took the opportunity to deny the statement that the wrecked carriages were set on fire hp collision with the pctroleuin tanks. They asserted that some of the broken woodwork was made into a bonfire in order to assist the work of rescue. Most of the injured passengers are making progress to- wards recovery. The inquest was opened at Poulton on Monday. Annie Marsh, one of the passengers, whose husband was killed in the accident, gave a thrilling account of the disaster. She ,said- the- train went along the curve at great speed, and it youth remarked, " It we run at this rate we shall soon Lest Wigan---,my word, we aro going." The carriage rocked, then turned round like a screw, and over thee went. : Her head was forced through the carriage window by a broken panel. Finally she struggled through the wreckage: to the roof of the carriage and escaped. Sarah Ann Ridgway, the driver's widow, said her husband bad been a temperate man for many years. He had asked her to go on the trip to Blackpool and -take her :little boy, but afterwards he said they had better stay at home as "it would be all going and coming." The inquest was adjourned till • Friday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>TBE RAMSGATE MURDER?</head>
        <p>TBE RAMSGATE MURDER?</p>
        <p>The Ramsgate magistrates on Wednesday, made up their minds to commit William Noel for trial on the charge of wife mutder, The police have made pro- longed and extensive searches in the hope of discovering the pistol with which the murder was committed, but they have been unsuccessful. The witnesses for the defence includecipersons who were present in the Sun- day school with Noel on the day of the murder, and they state that he conducted the work of his class with his usual calmness and cheerfulness. The police theory is. of course, that this was exactly the conduct that might he expected from a man alto had committed a cruel and deliberate murder and had endeavoured with much sue- e(-.,a to make it appear that the crime was the work of a housebreaker. The coroner's jury, it may be remem- bered, were content to rPi urn an open verdict, but the magistrates—who have had the accused before them for sixteen days or more—were satisfied that the evidence established a case in which he ought to be called on to answer.</p>
