      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>EXAMINER. SATURDA Y, JULY 8, 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. SATURDA Y, JULY 8, 1893.</p>
        <p>• The Complexion. Vegetarianism will receive a powerful stimulus if the statement be correct that the best preservativas of a lady's complexion are oatmeal knd oranges. To the former is perhaps attributable the clear and healthy tint of skin so common in Scotland, and to the latter the peculiar softness of comPlexion enjoyed by the ladies in Spain and Italy. Sinco the discovery was made many English women not only use oatmeal in the water with which they bathe their faces, but live almost entirely upon oranges._ , _</p>
        <p>Tomatoes With Mushrooms. " Choose sir smooth totreatoes, at the blossom end of which make a small hole large enough to hold a dice- shaped piece of butter which has seen well dipped in pepper, salt, aticl. grated - nutmeg. Pl4ce theta: in a buttered tin, and on every one pat a cup-shaped mush- room soused in heated butter. Set them in a hot oven Ito cook, while you prepare six thin slices of ham. Roll and fasten each one :with a small skewer, and grill them qulchiy over a clear fire. Place them aside to drain :while poaching six new laid eggs. Arrange the toma- toes and mushrooms inthe centre of a dish in the same manner as they were cooked, on both sides lay the ham, and at each end the cm."</p>
        <p>When making a " crwy corner" in piir parlour be sure to' make it cosy. Sometimes they are nothing but !narrow, stiff-backed,' badly uphollstere4 :benches, not :half so coMfortahle !tit -.191d-faillioneel wooden :" settle" and Much worse situated in relation to the fire. The divan should he !Ong enough for reclining, nd broad enough for sitting or lounging, and it is a good plan to have overhead a shelf for one's favourite books and the monthly magazine*. A few various- coloured cushioni and a; rug aro altthe " looSe articles " required. Dainty little "antnnacassara- ftre colt of p'ace here ; they shoula be reserved for whit the anctioi ees Call " stand chairs," by which, perhaps, thoy itwan chairs that nobibdy ever likes to sit upon.</p>
        <p>Orank,e Tapioca. Wash three tablesp ifirifols'ef tapioca, cover with cold ;water and soak over night. In the Morino.; heat one , pint of milk in a dentble'boiler, ar.l4 the tapioca, a pinch !of salt, and boil twenty minutes. To the well-beaten olk oistwo eggs add half a teaspoonful of granulated sugar. and one teaspoonful of 'cornfbur diss,JlV•xl in a little cold Milk, and boiffor 'five tnioutes. Then pour Silto a pudding dish, make a inerinque of the whites of eggs, two tablespoonfuls of powered sugar, flavour with orange extract, and in a moderate oven brown slightly Pare, slice thinly, and remove the scuds of six or eight large, sweet oranges ; lay in the bottom of a glass h -;md sift powdered stuarover it and between each layi r." , When the pudding is cAI, run a wet'kuife around to loosen the edge, lay it over the fruit, and serve.</p>
        <p>DARK WOOD. A TALE OF LONEMOOR. BY MRS. HARRIETT LEWIS, Author of "The Sundered Hearts," "The Bailiff's Scheme," "The Double Life," dm.</p>
        <p>CHAPTEII XLV. THE BALL.</p>
        <p>Miss Norreys was the very ideal of a hostess. She comprehended the tastes of her various guests as by intuition, and silently gratified them. For Mrs. Kenright, Stout and indolent, were the most ,luxurious chairs, the ,lightest fins, the gossiping Court newspapers; for the ' young people were archery parties, croquet, long rambles, rides and drives, excursions to places of interest, and oppor- tunities for flirtation unlimited.</p>
        <p>Wednesday of the following week had been appointed for the excursion to Dunholm Castle. Invitations had been previously issued for a dinner party on Tuesday, the day previous to that appointed for the excursion, to be followed by a 'ball on the same evening, to be given by Miss :Norreys at teechmont in honour of her guests. Upon the appointed evening the most notable of the country people were entertained at dinner. Among them was Lord Darkwood, who appeared in unusually high spirits. Miss Norreys appeared to regard him with favour, and he believed that she had fathomed his desire to marry her and was not averse to bib suit. The dinner was a success. So also was the ball, which came on later. Gwen and the Lady Georgina had been invited, but the marquis refused to allow his daughter to attend, and Gwen was forced to remain at home also. The best county peeple were in attendance, many having come down from Lon- don purposely for the occasion. Miss Narteys was riding upon the top wave of popularity, and several new suitors, to tue great jealousy of Lord Dark- wood; flocked to her standard.</p>
        <p>Oki General Norreys, her late father, had come of a high family, and his wife had been the daughter of a peer. Society, therefore, received Miss Norreys with open arms. Her fortune was reputed to be fabulous: She was marvellously beautiful and well-bred. She might aspire to any dignity short of royalty, Lord Darkwood said to himself in alarm. No time was to be lost in securing to him- self this'great prize. He would have paid her especial attention that evening, with,a view to linking her name with his, and pied acing the impression that she was betrothed to him, but "Miss Norreys was not be entrapped into a net so exceedingly apparent. She avoided him ,throughout the evening with such.adroitness and cleverness Vat he did not sus- pect that she avoided him. The Duke of Rosstyne claimed a large shire,of her attention, and seemed to have fallen. in love with her. His, grace was a -widower, elderly, still handsome, and well worthy the love as well as the ambition of Mies Norreys. Lord Darkwood was in agony. What if the duke should. snatch up this glittering prize from befere his eyes? His high spirits evaporated. His fat face took on an expression of misery which Miss Norreys cold not avoid noticing. Miss Norreys promenaded with a noble earl, danced repeatedly with other gentlemen, and also promenaded with Lord Chilton, who did not dance. There was something extremely friendly in the relations of Lord Chilton to his hostess, the mar- quis noticed with increasing gloom. They seemed upon confidential terms. Why, she must be as old as the viscount, and he had the look of a boy beside bet.</p>
        <p>, . "Could she intend to throw hersolf away upon that young viscount ?" Lord Darkwood asked himself, savagely. " Wemen are fools!" the mar- quis muttered. "A woman at twenty-five is older than a man at that age. She might be a duchess or a marchioness if she chose, and yet she smiles on that boy. The county is running after her. She will be the rage. She will make a sensatfon when presented to Her Majesty. She suits me in every way—gratifies my pride, satiafieg my love, and I will have her. Why should I wait for far- ther acquaintance? The prompt wooing is usually the most successful. I'll ask her to-night to marry me. I cannot much longer endure this suspense." He had made up his mind to propose to her with- out further delay. Now for the opportunity. He ,out for it long and vainly. She was engaged every moment before supper, and the Duke of Rosstyne led her out to the banqueting room. Lord Darkwood offered his arm to Mrs. Kenright, and was supremely miserable throughout the period she devoted to the refection. As to him, he ate nothing, but he drank a good deal .to fortify his courage, and finally conducted his charge back to her chair in the dancing-room, and wandered aside, watching eagerly for the oppor- tunity he craved. It was somewhere about two o'clock when the opportunity was at last afforded him. The Duke of Roastyne had been superseded in his attend- ance by another gentleman, and Miss Norreys had paused with him at the head of the room at a convenient point to overlook the dancers. Lord Dark wood made his way to her swiftly. "Will you promenade with me, Mies Norreys?" he asked, before she could turn from him. Miss Norreys graciously assented. Taking his arm, she strolled down the grand apartment, blazing with lights and jewels and radiant toilettes, herself the fairest woman there. She was dressed in a cream-tinted silk of superb quality, and made up with point lace into a toilette of exquisite beauty and elegance. Diamonds flashed from her heir, neck, and arms, and jewels which Lord Darkwood, deeply as he was in love, and anxious as be was about his fate, mentally appraised at immense value. fie led her towards the conservatory. It was brilliant with gaslights. The air was heavy with sweetness. The long avenues of bloom and vivid colour were nearly deserted, the ravish- ing strains of a Strauss waltz calling the guests to the ballroom.</p>
        <p>Lord Darkwood conducted her to an avenue embowered in orange trees in all the glory of waxen flower and golden fruitage. They were alone. His opportunity was come. He would "stake all upon the hazard of a die," and ask her to marry him. And Miss Norreys comprehended what was corning, and a subtle tremor shook her slender frame. Would she accept him? Ah, she did not know herself.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAREST NELLIE, There appears to me to be plenty of variety ha our day dresses, for different styles are being shown almost daily by our trading firms. The hands of contrasting material with which our skirts have been hooped for the last two or three months are rapidly disappearing, and one of the I most charming trimmings is an 18-inch flounce of a lighter material than the gown itself, set round the skirt in a, vankyked fashion and headed with a' tiny ruche. Another method is to repeat the frills at the hem round the hip; but this re- quires a sylph-like figure and a long waist. Hem- stitched tucks are very dainty-looking on muslin!, and such-like delicate materials. For short figures, one of the most successful styles of skirt trimmings i‘c that of outlining each seam with moile or satin -ribbon, finished about four inches from the skirt hem by rosette-like bows.</p>
        <p>A very charming blouse, and one that can be used either with a tea gown or with an out-door costume is known as the 6chu blouse. It is made of French cambric crossed over the figure to tie at the back and has full frills falling softly round the throat.</p>
        <p>A very pretty gown may be made of shot whip. ,cord in some attractive colour, with ficelle lace and narrow ribbon velvet, or even moire ribbon. Whipcord being usually of double width, and also capable of being used either way, cuts to more ad- vantage than silk, and six to six yards and a•half will be ample for this model, with a medium train. The method of cutting is intended for the lining, and the material is cut exactly to this for the back and curved back gores. On t:le back, at the ex- tension, fiat pleats are folded to meet in the centre. After the front lining is cut, a coloured or white cotton, according as the lining is dark or light in colour, should be run down in the position of the broken line on the diagram. This defines the edge of the material, and the front is let in. In the material only one bosom dart 13 cut, 513 the front line may be kept intact, and then fastened down over the bosom gore nearest the centre, which is sewn in the lining only. The seam under the arm must be carried off by gradnakdegrees, or if sewn to a poinio too ,alpirply, there will be an ugly pucker over the hip. Some decided novelties in the way of sailor hats have made their appearance lately. The very newest—l really cannot say the prettiest—style, has a black gland crown and a brim of coarse tan straw, the trimming consisting of a folded band of coloured satin ribbon round the crown, and a rosette of the same, 'together with an upright black quill•feather over each temple. Other sailor hats are covered with suit le kid, but far nicer and less eccontrio are those of Panama straw, with rather wide brims, low crowns, depressed in the centre, and a simple trimming of plain or shaded velvet ribbon, with a flat bow at the side.</p>
        <p>A charming and seasonable confection is of white fancy chip lined with gathered Hese and turned up at one side to dieplay a group of w.bite ostrich tips, in the centre of which are effectively placed two black wiugs. The fishwife dress for little girls from five to 10 years old is exceedingly pretty and, what is quite as important, most serviceable. It is made of a mixture of flue navy-blue serge (flannel or linen can be substituted if preferred), mixed with red and white striped galatea, and has a double skirt, the under one being of serge and the upper t,f galatea, while the short turned-up tunic above is of serge. The blouse bodice—ateo of serge—has a sailor collar, and opens at the throat to show a vest of the galatea, which is also used for the deep gauntlet cuffs. The two skirts are really kilted flounces on a lining foundation, the galatea being merely tacked in position so that It may be easily removed for washing. A pretty cross-over blouse can be made of white embroidery muslin, with lower sleeves, collar, sad vest of white embroidery One yard of this embroidery will be auffloient. The muslin is very wide, therefore you will find not more than two and a-half yards will be required. It will not bee very costly little garment, but Is so effective that ib may be worn on the smartest occasion. The back is cut on the cross, and drawn into the waist with reeving•string. The sleeves are lined with a coat. shaped lining throughout, and a pleating of stiff muslin must be gathered into the top of the sleeve.</p>
        <p>A yachting costume in white Irish flax, arranged with sailor biouse looks very tasty. The bodice should have a double collar of white and blue of the regulation navy cut, and a vest of flex. embroidered with a club burgee on the front. To a skirt of moderato fulness are fixed two bands of blue flax, on which are laid narrow lines of white linen braid, making an eff&lt;ctive trimming. The jacket, of naval shape, should he of blue flex matching the trimming of the gown, and fastened with anchor buttons. A sailor hat of coarse straw with a band of blue and white ril,bon to trim it, csomplet.ts a costume which is eminently suitable' for deck wear. Yours sincerely, JENNIE.</p>
        <p>St e4k awl Pigeon PM. Lay in a dish two pigeone, on a well-beaten steak (allowing a quarter of a pounct of steak to each bird), with breasts upward, covering ea zh with a thin slie ol bacon. Sprinkle with one teaspoonful salt, quarter of a teaspoonful of pepper, chopped parsley, halt clove of shallot. Fill up crevices with sliced hard-boiled eggs; pour in one teacupful of water, cover with piste, bring- ! ing birth.' feet up through a hole hi the middle.</p>
