      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>Sill I. CANAL.</head>
        <p>Sill I. CANAL.</p>
        <p>PROWESSIT NEAR WA RRINGTO N.</p>
        <p>From the deo' ks at. Latch ford to the. cant Bever bridge there is very little work be trig done on the hip, Cabal:, Tiny Construction., of - the locks,, the hydraulic pressure house, and the bridges on this section aro now completed. The siles of thr;.cut-.. ring are in many places still undergoing the streag- t 'letting process by being lined with pieces of rock. There will, at course, he a Considerable amount of work to do at the present railway station' at Latoll-'1 Cord, in a short timeswhen the L. it N.-W. Rail Way. Company band over that 'minor their Warrington to Steck port line. Near to the swing bridge at Twenty step Bridge, a score of navvies are to be seen lay- , ing the concrete foundations for the jock gates, r.lirough which vessels from the Old Quay Canal wit outer_ the Ship Canal. The approaches to the swing bridge at this point,andthe mason work at the entrances of the bridge are now almost completed. The setts on he bridge for vehicular traffic are now laid, and tlital parapets are completed. It is expected that thehrichpi will be in use before long, and the one in use at present pulled dawn. Looking down the cutting from Twenty Step 12ridge, a steam navvy and about a hundred eavviee are to be seen at work widening the canal,and it will be some time before this part is completed. ,Just beyond too, Paine's Bridge,t her e is in course of erection another !tydraulic pressure house, which ma to work two swing oridges. The work has been sublet. to Messrs. Neild, contractors, Manchester, and the work is now going on spare. A number of navvies were to work at the cul- vert near the river diversion, and two hand pumps are being worked to keep it free frain water, but the work of filing the river up at this point opposite River-road, his not, yet been commenced. A dredger is still at work widening the approach of the diversion at the Walton end. The continued spell of fine weather is greatly facil- itating the work of the contractors, and they are taking advantage of it, and should no tinforseen disaster occur, it is very proba hie that large vessels will be plying aloug its course early in 1894, pas: docklesa.,Warringt . . . . .</p>
        <p>We extract the following from an article dzi the canal in "The Manchester Weekly Times" of Friday last : " Thelwall is a charming rural village, but one possess- ing a far 'reaching and somewhat etiequered history. It is recorded that about the yesr 620, Edward the Elder, one of the best and wisest of the Saxon kings, built a town here, and fenced it about with stakes and etumps cut from the trees as a protection against any maraud- ing enemy, and it is from this circumstance the name is derived, "then" being the SaXOR word for stakes and wall," whicii has survived to the present day, having much the 3811:10 meaning then as it has now—a high fence encompassing a place. In those far-off days the Mersey was wider and deeper than it is now, and it is said that Thelwall was then a port for shjps, though doubtless they would he of light 'draught, but large enough, it would seem, te carry the piratical hordes who came fighting under the banner of the Black Raven from across the Northern Seas. Beyond Thetwall the eye ranges over a richly pastoral country to the lofty and beautifully wooded eminence of Hill Cliff, a favourite' resort of the good people of Warriugton, and beyond that again to the Overton Hills. overlooking Fretinhain and the broad grassy marsh which stretches away to the shore of the Mersey estuary. Again mounting our steam horse we speed along, and presently reach Latchford, where some of the heaviest and most. important work in connection with the undertakb g has had to be carried out; for, in :Addition io the Latchford Locks. which are of the same din ensions as those at Mode Wheel, Balton, and -Mem. there 13 the Warrington and Stockport (L. ! and N. W.) deviation railway, the Knutsford Road open- ing bridge, the Latchfnrd high-level road bridee and the Northwich Road opening bridge, ta say nothing of the docks, which the Warrington Corporation insisted upon having constructed before they would . withdraw their opposition to one af the Com- pany's Bills. Latchford, though geographically in Cheshire, is included within the limits of the municipal and parliamentary borough of Warrington. The name is doubtless derived from the ancient ford which existed in the time of Roinan occupation, and was then of consicerable importance, inasmuch as the passage over the river at this point was the entrance to a new province. The ford was at sonic distance from the present bridge and is believed to have been at a place called the Vi 4sti. After the Conquest, the monopoly of the ford was given by. Randle Blundeville, Earl of Chester, to the Bovdees, who were Lards of Latchford, and they appear to have established a ferry, to which they had exclusive rights, as evidenced by the fact that about die year 1250 they gave the monks of Statilawe the privilege of passage without any obstruction what- ever. This 'terry was in turn superseded by it bridge, which is known to have beers in existence as early as the year 1396, when it is mentioned in an inquisition that Thomas Boyden held Latchford, "Cu' passagio "pent's de Weryngton "—that is, with the passage of the bridge of Warrington, and we learn from aim ancient 545. that a toil of a halfpenny was demanded from every man with a horse, end for every horse with a load passing over the bridge, and as farthing from every man without a horse We don't know the exact date when the bride was first erected, but it had fallen into decay or been taken down before the time of Henry VII., for when that Sovereign made his famous progress into Lancashire and visited Latham and Kuowsley, iii complimeet to his stepfather. the Earl of Dertn ; built a substantial bridge across the river . . fer the King's more convenient . passage, and by' this munificent act conierred a benefit .n the two palatine counties the value of which it is nut easy to estimate. On the approach of the " Scotch rebels,'" headed by the Pretender, in 1745, the panic-stricken inhabitenrs cut down the middle arch to prevent the p 'siege aCrosS, anti in this state it remained until 1747, when it was re- built at the expense ot Parliament, a watchtiouse laming placed in the middle. This bridge in tie fell into a state of decay, and in 'lBl2 it Wa4 taken (lowa aol a wooden bridge resting un stone piers substituted for it at the joint cost of the Ceunty of Cnester amid.tliehmunai. red of West Derby, Laimishire. As a timber briclize it did credit to the architect, but there were some, indle- stionmi of the institlioiencv of the material employed;arid. inlB3B it gave place to the present stone strut:tens, which was erected from a design which bad been Pre-. pared by Mr. Harrison, then deceased, the architect or the well-known lirosvener Bridge over the Dee at (The.- ter, the cost being £6,000, half of which was bone by the County of Chester, and the remaining half by the hundred of West Derby. THE ARBITRATION.</p>
        <p>The arbitration relative to the claims of the North- Western and Great Western Railway Companies against the Alanchester Ship Canal Company was resu:ntat on Monday in London before Lord Ualfour of Burleigh. Mr. Moulton, Q.C., opened the case for the Siiip Company. He said the claims ainounting to £420,000, were the largest the tailaay companies could coast-, ariu there should be many oleduction, even cri acclunt of the advantages the new lines provided. II iniutended that the gradients of UM deviation lines were tict exces- sive, that very little bank enginet,assistarwe would be required, and that lavish sums were demanded • for really unnecessary- engine help in working the traffic. THE TRANtSFER OV RAILWAYS.</p>
        <p>... It is stated that the agreement for the transfer of rail- way lines from the London and North-Western and Great Western Companies to the ship Canal Company was completed On Tuesday. The agreement provides for the transfer of the lines in the week which begins on the 9th inst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>CHAPTER XLVI. A TELEGRAM.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XLVI. A TELEGRAM.</p>
        <p>Not too suddenly would Lord Darkwood broach the subject of his love and hopes to his brilliant hostess. He was upon the brink of a proposal, and he would not risk refusal by too great abrupt- ness; and yet he felt that his destiny must be settled that night. He could not bear the tortures of jealousy. He loved this beautiful East India heiress as he had loved once many years ago, but bad never expected to love again. The fires of passion, which he had deemed extinguished within him, had flamed up anew, and more fiercely than ever. What was the love of his early manhood to this consuming passion that occupied him sleeping and waking? Love, ambition, all the leading characteristics of his being, were engaged in this affair. With Miss Norreys for his wife, he would not exchange places, if he could, with any man in England. He felt as if he could pour out bis Confession in a wild out- burst befitting a youthful Romeo; but his discretion restrain&amp; him. I .</p>
        <p>He led her up and down the scented avenue, between the rows of orange trees iu tubs and her hand rested lightly upon his arm. The white, waxen blossoms, cups of odour, gleaming amid glossy green leaves, the gold globes of fruit, the clustering gaslights, enchanted the senses; and Miss Norreys, in her cream-tinted silk, her pale olive face radiant as a star, her dusky hair perfumed and silky, her eyes glowing, added to the strange, intoxicating charm. Lord Dark wood spoke of the dinner and the ball, and complimented his hostess upon their success. •He was thinking how best to broach the subject in hand, when the lady made a movement to withdraw her hand from his arm, and her bracelet caught Upon the lappel of his coat. The marquis gallantly attempted to disengage the trinket.</p>
        <p>It consisted of a great limpid diamond star, com- posed of large and magnificent jewels, mounted upon a slender band'of gold that encircled the fair arm, which was gloved to the elbow. Lord Dark- wood succeeded in loosening the bracelet from its hold upon him, and in so doing, unclasped it. It fell into his hand, and his glance -perceived, upon the inside of the t old band, a small and eicquisitely engraved monogram. said, reading it. "I beg your pardon, Miss Norreys ; But I have always been curious to know your Christian name. It is not a common one, I know, else it would be very unsuited to you. 'S' might stand for Sarah, which means princess:" _</p>
        <p>"My name is Sicily," said Miss Norreys, quietly. "It is an old family name." "Sicily Norreys,, ' exclaimed Lord Darkwood. "A beautiful name, and one that suits you." He replaced the bracelet on her arm but held her</p>
        <p>hand in his firm clasp. The hour and moment had come. -He could not longer suppress the words that thronged in a fevered train to his lips. "Miss Norreys, Sicily," he exclaimed, his low tones freighted with passionate emotion, "do not start from me: Listen to me. I have not known you many weeks, but I .have known you long enough to put my heart into your keeping. I love you, Miss Norreys, with all my soul. I had thought to tell you this in different words and tones, but the truth will not wait to be clothed in flowers of rhetoric. I love you, Sicily. Will you be my wife 1"</p>
        <p>'The word were spokon—the plunge was taken. He was frightened at his own temerity, lest he had spoken too soon, and yet he drew a sigh of relief that the dreaded declaration was over. , ,</p>
        <p>He could not see her face. At his words ,he sensitive skin had become stained with-vivid scar- let ; but the blood swiftly receded, leaving her white as marble. Into the dusky eyes came a wild, pained expression, followed closely by a swift keen exultation. Had she some revenge to wreak upon him, as she had once said in her self-cornmuningsl And, if so, how would she execute that revenge Would she refuse him with scorn, or would she accept him as her husband'?</p>
        <p>Her purpose was not made immediately manifest. Possibly she did not even know it herself. Withdrawing her hand from his hold, she sank into a seat under an orange tree. He sat down beside her, greatly agitated. " Have I been too abrupt ?", he asked. "Pot days the words have been at my tongue's end, and I restrained them lest I should injure my own cause by my precipitancy. I offer you the devotion of a heart that, in spite of my years i fresh still. You might marry a man of higher rank, Sicily; but you cannot marry one who would be so completely your slave as I would be."</p>
        <p>The lady was silent, and her silence thrilled her suitor with a great hope. She must be considering his offer. If she intended to refuse him, would she not signify such intention without allowing him tc go further ? • "In worldly position, I am not unworthy your notice, Miss Norreys," he exclaimed "I am a mar quis, of one of the oldest families in England, a.r 'Burke's Peerage' Would inform you. The Dark. woods trace their origin back to the days and tc one of the followers of William the Norman. My income is forty thousand a year, and I am taking measures that will resul tin its being nearlydoubled.' He referred to the pressure he was bringing to bear upon his tenants. In family and fortune, there. fore, I have nothing to be ashamed of. Will you not accept both as your own He waited in breathless suspense for her answer. It•'eame.in aloW, clear voice; but it was neither an aisent nor dissent. • •</p>
        <p>"1 hardly. know what to say, Lord Darkwood,' she declared. "You do me great honour in asking me to become your wife. Believe me I appreciate it; but I am hardly prepared to reply to you." "You do not refuse me then ?" he cried..</p>
        <p>"And I do not accept you," she answered, steadily. "At least not yet. lam not indifferent to your proposal. Upon some accounts I would like to accept your proposal." He muttered words of rapture. "But there are obstacles---"</p>
        <p>" I will remove them;" he cried, ardently. "If you object to my daughter, I will send her to a French convent. She need never annoy you, Sicily If the obstacle is in myself, name it, and I will do whatever You wish." Still she hesitated. He grew nearly wild with anxiety. He was tortured by her hesitancy and doubt, and she knew it, and was secretly pleased. At.last she relieved his impatience by saying— " Let me be frank with you, Lord Darkwood. I have known you but a few weeks. I know you as honoured and respected in the county but I know nothing whatever of your past life, Perhaps you would say that does not concern me. Yet, even if I loved a man never so well, I would not become his wife until lknew his private character, his past which had helped to form that character or to exemplify . it. A man may know a woman's life from its commencement. These friends of mine from India have known me and my family, I might say, always. They will tell you all about Me from the time I left the schoolroom. Have I not the right to know you equally well as you can know me!!</p>
        <p>"Why certainly," stammered the marquis, deci- dedly emliarrassea. There were deeds in his life he would not dare to uncover to those pure eyes. What could she mean? Women were queer, he thought, and East India heiresses, it appeared, were likely to excel other women in eccentricity. What could his past life matter to her? "Do you fear, Miss Norreys, that, like the villians in nbvels, I may have a surreptitious wife who will make an untimely appearance, to the great scandal of the community ? " He tried to speak jocularly" but it was a failure.</p>
        <p>"I do. ask Your confidence, Lord Darkwood. I only (lake to intimate that I could not confide my happiness and life in a man of whom I only know that he is titled, wealthy, an agreeable draw- ing-room companion, and an honoured member of society." • "But is not that nearly all you can know of any man ?" asked the marquis. "Yet I find your demand extremely reasonable." He Brooke falsely there. "I am preparad to tell you all about myself with the utmost frankness, since your desire to hear it implies that you are not decided to refuse me. I ant overjoyed at your manifestation of inter- est in me. I have not been Lord Darkwood long, as you doubtless know. Less than a year ago I was Captain Tollish of the army, stationed in Malta, encumbered with debts, yet still a gentleman, and held in honour by my comrades. The death of my cousin, to whom I was heir, changed all that, mak- ing me a peer of the realm." "I have heard all that:"</p>
        <p>"Shall I begin at the beginning ?" he questioned. " My grandfather, Lord Chateris, had two bons. The elder succeeded to the title and estates. The youngest , whose patrimony was not inconsiderable, but whose expenditure was large, married an heiress Miss Tollish, and, in defiance of the wishes of his heiress, changed his name to hers. I was the issue of the marriag,a. My uncle made a suitable alliance—he married the daughter of a duke - and one son was the result of marriage. That son, my cousin, Lord Edward Chateris, was the late mar- quis, whom I succeeded. He never married. You nowlnow my origin, and how I came to be Lord Darkwood. Shall I make confession of boyish scrapes, of peccadilloes great and small, of dissipa- tictn, and squauderings of wealth and formation of debts—'</p>
        <p>She put up her hand in a quick gesture. "These are nothing to me," she said. "I do no wish to hear of them. Do not 'humiliate yourself and me by dragging to the light the faults and follies of your youth, Lord Darkwood. I have noth- ing to do with your dissipations. tut is there no great wrong in your past that, if known, would deter any pure woman from becoming your wile 1" Her dusky eyes were lifted and their sulendour dazzled him. He looked down, and his counten- ance changed. What could she mean? A "great wrong" in his past—ay, there was? Were her words merely chance words, or did she guess the crimes that lay like graves in his past ? It was with an effort that he recovered himself. Of course she could suspect nothing ; he was mad to think it. He wiped his forehead hastily with his handkerchief and forced a quiveriug sort of smile.</p>
        <p>"There is no wrong in thy pa. st," he asseverate&amp; "I never wronged a human being." " The man I marry must be stainless," said Miss Norreys, proudly. "I do not ask a heart that has never known love. That would be folly. But I do ark for a heart not worn out by frequent fancies, evanescent passions aroused by every pretty face —a heart that can be true and steadfast to me, its capabilities of affection still strong—" "I can offer you that !" he interrupted, eagerly: "I adore you, Sicily. nave not been a man to fall frequently in love., I never loved but one woman until I knew you—" . .</p>
        <p>"And she was your wife, the mother of your child? No. I married my wife because she--" He was about to say frankly, "Because she had money; but he bethought himself in time, and said, Because she liked ihe, and her father was my friend and follow officer. , I never loved her." "But you have loved?" "Once—once only. A frail girl, midway in her teens, a little wilful creature, as pretty as a vision. It was many years ago, and she died.- soon after- wards. Ydn remind me of her now and then, Miss Norreyrip although she could no more be compared to you than the wild rose can be compared to the royal garden rose. • She died—and I regretted her She WAS my only love—"</p>
        <p>" Was she your wife ?" His features quivered an instr,nt. "No,".los_answered. "She was not."</p>
        <p>"She died unmaitied ?"</p>
        <p>He bowed assent, his eyes still downcast. "She was never married," he said, his face exhibi- tatan emotion which deed his iron will to con- " Let us not speak of her. Miss NorreYs.".</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>MUNICIPAL VACANCY AT NVIDNO-</head>
        <p>THE MUNICIPAL VACANCY AT NVIDNO-</p>
        <p>tie feared that she would probe the matter farther, would ask the name of that girl who hail died . Il young, and he would not for worlds be forced to tell her the story of Clare Markham and her awful fate, so he added desperately: "Her name is not , fit for your pure lips, Miss Norreys. She sleeps in a dishonoured grave. So let her rest I was sill" A less in the matter. I loved her—that was all. Y°ll will not let that girl come between us?" . . _,</p>
        <p>, "You never harmed her r, "Never l" Miss Norreys' face, drooping and half hidden from him blazed with a scorn that 'was terrible. Her red lips curled • her eyes flashed with a light- ning that might have withered him had he but seen it. - "I am tempted to marry you Lord Darkwood," she Said. face rffvu h oiera She was thinking of revenge. " Will you not promise?" -he demanded in an was wsitohfthe a her scorn sweetest and mhautsriecd, of hfro f. her ecstacy, forgetting his recent discomfort in the glow produced by that assurance. "My heart is fresh and young. I lay it at your feet. Become ray wife, Sicily.. I will make your life a very for . taste of heaven !" He wooed her with passionate protestations °f affection ; she listened, with her brain in a whirl. Should she marry him, or should she refuse him The strain of the Strauss waltz floated to their call, the fragrance around them steeped their senses a deep-delight. In the , far aisles of the conser- vatory a few pairs of lovers wandered. 'They. WeTV alone—the time was short—she would be Missed. Tr hat was to be her answer?</p>
        <p>He waited in an agony of suspense. - ;" Sicily,' he whispered, " Dunholm Castle waits for a mistress. Will you not be Marchioness Darkwood ?" • His grandeurs were so sweet to him, he fanejea they would tench her also. If she would not marrY hint for love, would not his.possessions tempt her. • She surveyed him with her splendid eyes, the short, stout figure, the fat, oily, sinister visage' all aquiver now with eagerness, and a deep disgso seized her. Could she marry him .even, for the consummation of her revenge? She was about to end the scene by an abrupt refusal of him, when a servant came silently don"" the aisle of bloom, and approached her with yellow envelope upon a salver. It was' seen it a glance that the envelope c°l3" tamed a telegram. "The message was brought over from Shrews' bury 'before midnight,' madam," explained •t}'!„ servant, "anti it yas sent up to Naya to be for you. But Nun grew nervous. lest there should be' important news-in it, and she asked the butler. to find you out and give it to you, The butler idsv discovered as you was in here ma'am, and sent, we. here, immediate."</p>
        <p>• He presented the salver with a low bow. Miss Norreys took up the envelope and dismio:ca . the hearer. "Curse the luck !" thought the marquis. " has prevented her answer. Why didn't that itli°" wait five minutes longer?". Miss Npreys tore open the message, drawing Iter'r a gaslight so that its light fell over her shoultio: upon the "paper. The message was brief, 1)"' startling. • It read as follows : AJACCIO, CORSICA, May 5. "To MISS NORREYS, Beechmont, ShrowsburY' Shropshire. The person was not drowned. Wal rescued by Corsicans, nursed through illness, left for home six months ago. "\V. G. Bansiv•</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
        <p>Monday was the icst, day for receiving n/123 tions for the vacancy on the Whines Town caused by the bankruptcy of Mr. Fitzpatrick, ..4 htid represented Waterloo Ward. Five noininati?,.. were received on behalf Of Hugh O'Donnell, ll'', - nes-road, baker and grocer, Tile fir 4 held the sitil‘a natures of the Rev. James Clarke as proposer, 8 Mr. Thomas Drury as seconder, and the followiegna;,, seating burgesses :—Messrs James Duffy, J. F. Eel 3 C. Keeneghan, J. Mahon, J. Sherratt, M. Pierce, 0. Muldoon, and M. Abbott. On the second, he was Pry), posed by Mr. D. E. Lowry, seconded by Mr. McClll. and assented to by Messrs. E. Griffiths, J. Ward, T.,.6hird F. Wallder, Hanlon, Edwards, and Maguire. Tllen--, proposer was Mr. J. - Williamson, and Mr. S. Seconded, the assentors being' Messrs. M. 4".' Fallon, B. Keogh, J. Camson, M. Boyle A. Ksvanjo, P. E. Carroll, and Mary Ann McConville. Vs f uliar was signed by the Revs. James Clarke and Joseph mer, and the following assenting burgesses—M"'., Kinsella, J. Brannon; T. Quinn, P. Kinsella. J. J°"'". Levin, J. McCormack, and J. Riley ; and the the Bev. J. Clarke and Mr. T. Drury, and asses. La by Messrs. I'. Duffy, M. A bbni t, P. Heel'l'd Mulloy, M. Burke. J. Campbell, F. Cuban, al/ Donoughuo. Charles Hemingway, 39, Church-street, Wicineshisr cribed as a builder, was proposed by Mr. Jsmes wed tø man, and seconded by Mr. T. Atherton, and at.sell„,t by Messrs. Messrs. R. Hicks, J. E. Boydell, T. Gerrard, E. ley, William Bray, James Kiddie, W. G. L. IWO- W. Jones and E. Steinart •</p>
        <p>The election is fixed for Monday next. -</p>
        <p>, • • J. de' WHOLESALE THEFT OF s ROPES Al RUNCORN. • 1, fair At Runcorn Petty fessiou., On Mandan Mr. C. Reynolds and Major flazleburst, Ingleaffeld and Thomaii Banner, of • the Queen of the Mersey, were charged, with stealing about slcwt. of ropes valued the property of the .111inchester Ship canal Cdeo, patty, on or shout June Ist, and Joseph Saun marine store dealer, Runcorn, was charged with re'fe.r• sameiNnegwtmhean etthe trm binowinogf Aies ittsorsb. Grundy esrtuolnedny partiodperxtyer:3,,,lT: prosecuted on behalf of the Ship Canal CoinpanYwr' Knowles (Widnes), represented Saunders, and ""'„:/!.--" Wynne Humphreys defended Ingles.field and Rahie , James Crank, Back Brunswick-A., stated that "Ulf' employed by Joseph Faundeis up to the 21at . About the 31st of May lie was instructed by Kiuhderlsoo take a barrow near the Ship Canal works, and tw° oae would bring him some ropes. Witness went, but no came. On the following morning Inglesfield arid Ban'o,. came to the shop. and brought a quantity of rope lor duced. The rope weighed 21cwt. Witness asked Irt he field why he did not come the previous night, an d,' ! replied, "Didn't you see the captain come on 0,0,5„10. !ti The next day witness saw the defendants stanu'liDlg Church-st., and watching Inspector .Cooper in at Saunders' window. Saunders said think the inspt cter smells anything? " ~ in Llth of June witn is ceuveyed a quantity of f°lot, a cart to the marine stores f Thomas n.'"`joila Runcorn, on the instructions of Saunders* „De, Meadowcroft, agent for the Ship Canal Corn,ich'is identified the rope produced by a red thread vo',;:;, worked through all the ropes of the companY•"'" the Chadwick, superintendent "of the Rope Works 57jOn ' canal, sp*ke to finding the rope produced at ! and Banner, Stores.--P.C. , being formally charged Inglesneld pleaded gull . ho the other two defendants not guilty. tbAt w nei nggialoi ce 'nbe:yrhbeetneaeriei,,etb3sn.chargedtowf stolen—Mr. provedl,age. stolenksiin)al