      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>CONTENTMENT.</head>
        <p>CONTENTMENT.</p>
        <p>&amp; Frenchman once saiTlTo—hi—s wife, " My dear, culti- 'rate contentment ; perhaps you may have to live upon ir ft happened that the lady did have to exercise her utmost philosophy to remain contealed under cir- cumstances of great poverty and privation. But then she enjoyed good health. Talk to a man suffering from Stout about contentment, and ten to one he will knock YOU down with his crutch. Try to assure the rich man who suffers the tortures of purgatory from dyspepsia, orS disordered liver, that he possesses every element of contentment, and you will be guilty of a cruel wrong, is there is no contentment without health, Mr. Thomas Corfield, of Bwlchgwyn, near Wrexham, tersely explains the cause of his discontent as follows : Following a severe attack of influenza I was taken With excruciating pains, something like acute Thema- under both shoulder blades and sharp stitches in lings which made breathing very painful,snd prevented rP. .1 also suffered from pains over the eyes, in my tnPlea and on both sides of my head. My appet'te liss fickle, food always caused flatulency; my bowels :ter erratic, and Movements usually accompanied both Pain. Improvement was perceptible from the first tie of tVarner's Safe Cure. In three months I was able to get out, and my health is now thoroughly re- covered,</p>
        <p>this. I want to discuss this question as between busi- ness men. If it is in the power of the Watch Commit- tee to make a condition not legally bindine— Councillor fleeeore : Then you have no right to make it. Counciller ROBERTS (continuing) : To make a condi- tion, a reservation, on the part of the licensees, with regard to the power of selling drink on these premises, the position would be this : That at the end of the year, if the licensees had broken the .blication into which they had voluntarily entered with the Watch Committee, the Watch Committee could absolutely refuse the license. It was done in other places, and he submitted with all res- pect that if the licensees would notaccept the licenses on those conditions, the conditions that they were willing to accept at the hands of the magistrates, the Couucil would be justified in refusing it. After sow further interruption, Councillor Roberts contended that neither the Court Theatre nor the Thee.- j tre Royal was suitable for the sale of drink. A more unsuitable place than the Theatre Royal could not have been found if they had searched Warrington all over. If j they grant licenses to those places, why net grant it tol the Empire,in Sankey-St.? His contention was that with every license which is granted tor the sale of drink, it followed that they were -adding a temptation to the young people of this town that they ought not to do, and it was on their behalf he spoke. He moved his. amendment. Councillor DIXON seconded the amendment. Alderman CHANDLEY said he happened to be in the chair at the time the license was granted, and It might be information for some, at least, of the members to know what took place. The lessees applied for a license for the Court Theatre. We feeling that V1,;(3 wished to certain extent, as far as we were able, to guard the morals of the public, made it a condition for them in this way. " Well," we said, " now if we grant you this license, will you undertake not to apply for an excise license for the sale of drink ? " They said, " We cannot accept that." The Committee felt that they ought not to deprive the town of a place of a musement,respectably conducted, with no complaint from the police and no in- stance of misbehaviour having ever come to the know- ledge of the Watch Committee. They accordingly granted the license to the lessees on the same conditions as they had it before. Councillor FLEMING speaking as a member of the Watch Committee said that when the license was granted to the Royal Court Theatre last year he objected to it. Later on it came before another set of gentlemen and was granted. As to the license to the Theatre Royal he objected to that as he did not think it was lit to be licbrised j at Ell. They might have arranged to grant them al license with a reservation that they were not to sell intoxicating drinks. Alderman Stereoe said the Watch Committee when- ever it granted these licenses had always given appli- cants to understand that they entirely disapproved of intoxicating liquors being sold. (Hear, hear.) And that if they ever did Fell intoxicating liq nors, it would go very much against them on renewing their applica- tion another time. (Hear, hear.) Alderman HARRISON defended the action of the Watch Committee. Councillor BROWNS: supported the amendment. It was a shame to grant to these men these licenses with- out the reservation, and he would tell them why. It was well-known to the four Aldermen at the top of the teble that for years and yeare the late Mr. Sheridan and Mr. William Johnson (assecretary of the Public Hall Cu) applied for the respective renewals of their licenses,and they never once raised a single murmur against the rid- ing of Mr.ll.l3.White to the effect that a reservation can be put on a license. Mr. Snape toolc over the Theatre Royal from the mortgagees, and the reservation was put on in that case. He was instructed to apply on behalf of Mr. Snape to the Watch Committee. The Town Clerk was present as well as Alderman Harrison. and they refused the license to me unless the reservation was put in. Councillor RORERTS : That appears to have been for- gotten. Councillor Bnowee added that it was a shame that a drink license should be granted from a publican's pint of view. There was Mr. eieor,ge Wright (Royal Curt Hotel) who had been mulcted into paying S:5O a year for the right to sell drink there (at the Royal Court Theatre.) After several interruptions, Councillor lieowrix con- tended that the Empire Theatre was just as much en- titled to be allowed to sell drink as the other places. Councillor Stem GREEN :.I would like to ask Alder- man Chandley, Harrison and Sutton what they mean ? They say that if the licenses do something which I can- not at present understand, "it will be seriously con- sidered next year." What do they mean ? Alde.man HARRISON: Permit drunkenness. The MAYOR: H the place is not properly con- ducted. Councillor SHAW GREEN : Are they the only things they will consider ? The MAYOR: Yes. Councillor SHAW GREEN : Not the suitability of the premises? The MAYOR: That is a question I cannot answer. COMICiiIOr SHAW GREEN : It seems to me that there has been a deliberate intention to give a license whether or not, because not many months ago you refused the license, but because the theatre is in other hands you grant it. The amendment of Councillor Roberts was then put, with the following result : For, Councillors Roberts, Green, Browne, Beaman, Fleming, Dixon, Bonnet!, Wilkinson, Plurnoton, 9. Against, Aldermen Harrison, Sutton, Chandley ; Coun- cillors Hutchinson, Hepherd, Heaton, Tinsley, Dr. Cannel!, Hill, Muir, Shaw, 11. The MAYOR : The amendment is lost. Councillor Sews, next moved as an amendment that j the resolution licensing the Theatre Royal, Scotland. road, be referred back to the Committee. He contended that the place was simply a Theatre of Varieties. Councillor Reowiee seconded the amendment, and said he understood that when the license was granted there were only four members of the committee present—two voted for it, and one against it, and one did not vote. Alderman HARRISON said that when he voted in favour of granting a license to the Theatre Royal in Scotland-road, he did net know that its character or nature had been changed in any degree. He thought it was a bona fide theatre. Now he heard from Mr. Shaw that it is a sort of theatre of varieties. If he had known that lie would not have voted for the license, and he would never vote for it again unless there was a coil- pl'ote alteration made in the structure. Councillor ROBERTS: I think we are improving very much. lam delighted to welcome the support of, Mr. Shaw and Mr. Harrison in this measure. As far as they go I am pleased to recognise their partial conversion. (Laughter.) Councillor SHAW: I don't understand what you mean by Conversion ! Councillor ROBERTS: I sin complimenting you upon the admirable manner in which you have moved the amendment. I could not have done it better myself, and that is saying a great deal: (Renewed laughter.) Alderman SUTTON said he would have no objection to the resolution goiag back to the committee for further consideration. The amendment was carried without opposition, and the minute in question was, therefore, referred back to the committee.</p>
        <p>STREET IMPROVEMENT CO3IMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON proposed the adoption of the minutes of this Committee, which were given in last week's Examiner. Councillor Motu seconded the motion. Councillor SHAW asked if the Committee could tell hi n when the lamps which had been promised for Dallam-lane were going to be erected. He had been told that the lamps were now in hand. It seemed to him that the hand which the lamps were in was either very long or very deep. (Laughter.) Councillor Mum : I am in a position to tell Councillor Shaw that the lamps are in hand. (Laughter.) Alderman CHANDLEr drew attention to a resolution in- structing the Town Clerk to give notice to all tenants of property other than cottage premises, which the Cor- poration had purchased for street improvement pur- poses to enable the Corporation to terminate the tenancies by a three months notice. He did not think this was a sufficiently long notice. After a short discussion, in which it was stated that the resolution was a formal one and that when the pro- perty was required an ample notics would be given the tenants, it was agreed to extend the notice to six</p>
        <p>, months. Mr. R. 'l'. FAIRCLOIIOH asked if it would not be a wise thing to require plans for the construction of streets just as they required plans for the construction of build- ings. They would remember that between Fennell-st., and the Old Church there was formerly no street and the Corporation had been obliged to purchase prmerty to construct a street. It seemed to him that it would be better to have plans of streets submitted before they were constructed, so that the Corporation could arrange where passages for new roads should be left. They could buy the land and not allow property to be erected first, then find that a street was required and be com- pelled to purchase and demolish the property. The TOWN CLERK explained that the• Corporation had no power to interfere with the construction of streets so long as they were made in accordance with the bye-laws as to width &amp;c. Councillor FATIICLOUGH asked when the Committee were going to make a charge upon those spears opposite the Lion Hotel in Bridge-street. He alluded to the rail- ings before the Hotel. He said Messrs. Greeijahl Whitley and Cc., always allowed consideration for the Corporation and he thought Messrs. Walker would do the same if they were approached. The latter were al- lowed to hare the railway lines running through the street in Pallam.lane, and they ought to show some consideration for the ratepayers in return. (Hear, hear.) Councillor BFAMAN suggested te the Committee that I they should erect a lamp in a passage between Rewsev- road and Edgeworth-sit. There were about twelve or 20 houses in the street, and there was no lamp there. Councillor SMETUUnST suggested that as the back of Wycliffe Chapel was being paved, a new lamp should be erected. Alderman SUTTON, in replying to the suggestions, said that there was a large ntmsber of lamps required in the town, but the committee were doing their best to meet all requirements. Respecting Councillor Fairclough's</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>monthly meeting of this Council was held at the Town Hall on Tuesday evening. The Mayor (Alderman Greenall) presided ; and there were also Present Aldermen Holmes, Chandley, Ilarrison, BUtton ; Councillors Fleming, Itobtrts, Bolton, ISr"rrie, R. T. Fairclough, Green, Cannel], Hill, 21ethurst, Beaman, Muir, Shaw, Bonnell, Willrin- Plurapton, Tinsley, Ileaton, flepherd, Dixon, ;.tilotxxl Slessrs. Whittle (town clerk) ; Longdin rough treasurer). surveyor); and Fairhurst (borough</p>
        <p>lb THE GUARDIANS AND THE CORPORATION. To Cutint read the following, extract (tor warded -01(1‘:Ir• J. C. button) trom the proceedings of the Board uardians at the last meeting :—" The Chairman said a t this being the last occasion of the Board meeting at the TOWn Hall he desired to acknowledge the kindness .1:41 icourte l sy of the Mayor and Corporation of Warring- -cln n granting the use of the hall for their Board meet- angs durla ing the period of quarantine at the Workhouse, cilad„ moved ' That the the best thanks of the Guar- be—half she given to the Mayor and Corporation in that .' The motion having been duly seconded and cluarrid unaaimously, the Clerk was instructed to corn- Same to the Thwn Clerk." DRINK AT LOCAL THEATRES. AN ANIMATED DEBATE.</p>
        <p>ti Tube minutes of the Watch Committee contained recoils- .o granting of a theatrical license to ,leessrs. thr, 'am Johnsen and Joseph Potter for the theatre Al. Scotland-road, and renewing the license for the 'eoYal Court Theatre. teThe alAeOlt moved, and Alderman HARRISON seconded "e coefirmation of the minutes of the committee. Councillor ROBERTS moved as an amendment that the Minutes of the iVatele Committee be c,refirined with the ee4le.7.Ptim of the resolutions of the Bth and 22nd June t"'"llg to the Theatre Royal, Scotland-road. and the re Royal Court Theatre, Rylands-street. First of all with , h gate] to the resolution proposing to renew the license te°!,eluinded the members of the Council that previous ,e' Lue tittle whetl the power to grant these theatrical renses devolved upon the Corporation, these premises tellcie been licensed for the performance of stage aneis, lot a number of years by the inagistratese We." ue believed tie was correct in stating that tit, eeeellse was granted by the magistrates on the implied „euerstanding and reservation that there should be no airioiia w tox M icating liquors sold on the premises. His COPD. as—that seeing that these premises had answered th e Purposes of the public for so many years without ' El.th necessity tor the salerof drink upon them, he could understand why the Watch Committee had. as it *Were, genc out et its way to confer an unreserved license 'aoll the licensees of these premises. Perhaps he might told that any reservation was really ultra circa, and )I.outetlilthrit;t be enforced. He was content to accept that, new perfectly well as business men that if the tlielee?leses obtain a license with a reservation, and then of tb'lr Own accord break such reservation, upon coining e,,fore the committee again they would probably incur ea refusal of the license. itoAlderman IlerinisoN here contended that Councillor h„bee tls was out of order. The Watch Committee veils, t;ic'aild. simply empowered to grant or withhold thee- °, dal licenses ; the question of granting or withholding, into°lng anything to encourage a license tor the sale of _ . -%lcating drinks never came before the Wetch Com- mittee at all. (Hear, hear.) oedlelrle. Aerofoil ruled that Councillor Roberts was in , Councillor ROBERTS said that in his opinion the RCCOM- MOdatiiin wib regard to drink is amply supplied in the immediate neighbourhood of the Royal Court Theatre. Alderman that? HARR/SON: What have we got to do with The Mayon: Mr. Councillor Roberts, I am advised by the Town Clerk that you cannutdiscuss the question as to the sale of intoxicatine liquors The Town Ceerne said the question as to whether a Person can sell intoxicating liquors was already decided by law. If lie obtained a theatrical license ier could obtain a further license for the sale of intoxicating liquors from the Excise. The question was eitra circa, and bad no right to be decided here. Councillor Roßerers : You will excuse me if I differ entirely from the Town Clerk in his ruling. (. Oh, oh.") Alderman Hettelsos : What is the use of having a lown Clerk if a layman be entitled to ignore what lie advises? We are coming to a pretty pass! Councillor Roßeters said he fortified himself in his opinion by the action of the Council of the City of Man- ,,eheater in regard to a similar procedure last year. The ,ueoUvcillor was again referring to the sale of drink when ‘lle Mayor again ruled him out of order. Councillor Boneues submitted that he had a perfect light to discuss the granting of a license which has been ;IMAM on conditions different from what was previously 11-8 ease. The pesiticn was merely this, the magistrates ta rd ;1141 aP°wer of geanting this license and they granted certain reservation. Why had that reservation irue;n insisted upon by the new granters of this thes' Why should the Watch Committee go out of ' way to grant it unconditionally ? 404seld.erinan CHANDLEY : Because they thought proper to Councillor Reimers : Certainly ; and if we as a 'c'utleil, and if I as a member of the Council, think pro- laaer to bring up a discussion to-night, I maintain that I elrltler i Perfectly in order in doing so. I am perfectly in " lee; sale ee .....scussine it in all its bearings as regards the ul drink, or the primary question of granting the ire et I. It is sheer nonsense to tell me-- I told you that I had beers advised by (lovirn Clerk, and I must rule you out of order. in c,lltieillOr ROBERTS : I cannot. understand your rul- In the face of facts. (Cries of " Chair.") I think case"l. Ought to give me an opportunity to put my Nnciller FIEATON : Keep in order. Roemers : I am is order. ule Moon you are nut of order. rd "CillOT ROBERTS : Do you rule that I am out of er in discussing the side issue of the granting of this sueatrieal license ? 7:lee Town CLERK : Yes. ha."uricillor Runes ; Well, I accept your ruling. I why" seen the minutes of the City Council of Manchester to tetril the questien was discussed of granting the license dis e Palace of Varieties, and the whole question was •oe eu"ecl - upon this point and granting unconditionally °them Aide, 1"• Roh 'Malt Ste-roe • Was surprised that Councillor Bits should per-Ant in defying the chair in this he was told distinctly that he wasout of order. be 'nersher of the Watch Committee he had always weeell given to understand that they had no power beyond to i 113. granting the license,and it was not in their power theegeallY, imPose any reservation at all. If they did net enforce the carrying of it out.. teootlfbr ROBERTS said that neithei Alderman Sub- "or. all the combined Aldermen of the Council would induce him to believe for one moment that the e„ th ch„CoMinittee have no power whatever to consider the-- "censes granted without reservation, and that ceey could imp-se no conditions. He only came to one I)‘;,llrelusieti, , and that was that there were certain mom. tehaontic7tCouncil who wanted to burke the discussian. the slightest objection to the granting of the then" lot' etage plays, his sole, entire objection was to , sale of drink. Alderman Ilenersoe : We have granted no such !/11lherit C Y' °llecillor ROBERTS: The license carries power with it Power to sell drink, and drink has been sold. The iiXeise cannot refuse the power after you grant the peeoenae• The whole thing is a perfect farce. The very Pie who have obtained the license from you are not earrei vey: rig on the business the license la intended to con- Alder e• Tr IM Johnson end elMarl otITTON (Messrs. lt es ) abuse their power we can refuse to grant the Jhe T t ovite Creeire : The Council most either grant or discrete se th license. They have a discretion it is true—a they must exercise on reasonable grounds.nion hu. nly ues two questions that can arise are these—is the uedin . • the 0 re Suitahle, is the applicant suitable 7 These are re. I:11Y questions that can arise, se"uncillor IiOnRMIS : Do you advise me that the Watch mittee has no moral right. ti It) .1‘"I•1 Ci.euiir :I am net concerned with the moral t'ght. (Laughtrr.) lam only concerned vrith the legal Coun • tnuti miler ROBERTS :IF eou are not I am. lam very concerned with the nicral question. Cars— (L HARRISON : We are a very immoral people. Laughter.) aughtmCouncillor Itonyiers •Speak for youreelf. (Renewed e) W - • hat I am concerned about, Mr. Mayer, is</p>
