      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>TB E EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 8, 1893.</head>
        <p>TB E EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 8, 1893.</p>
        <p>suggestions about plans for streets, he said they had not the power to interfere with the construction of the streets, but they woe very careful in examining plans of buildings, arid that partially met the difficulty com- plained of. The minutes were then adopted. MALTH COMMITTEE,—DR.C.MRSALL'S REPORT,</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON proposed the adoption of the mirmt,s of this committee. In doing so, the Alderman alluded to the letter from Dr. Guest Gornall, resigning the position of superintendent of the Hope Hospital, and stating that the epidemic was now dying out. He said there were only two or three cases now in the hospital, and they were expecting that when they had been dis- charged the epidemic would have entirely disappeared from the town .(1.1 ear,hear.)He thought the Council would supp:•rt the committee in thanking Dr. Gornal for his valuable services during the nine months in which he had been et the hospital as superintendent and as•istant medical officer. No one could have devoted him- self with more - energy to the difficult task than Dr. Guest Gornall had done. It was, there.</p>
        <p>fore, both gratifying to him and to themselves that they had observed that the patients who had been treated in the Elope Hospital were very ready to bear testimony to the kind treatment that they received in the hospitals. He also alluded to the excellent report which Dr. Guest Gornall had submitted to them on the smallpox epidemic. The report was a useful and com- prehensive one arid would be valuable not oily to War- rington hut to other towns all ever the country. Councillor HILL objected Us a deputation consisting of the Medical Officer of Health, and the Chairman of the ' Health Committee, attending the annual congress of the British Institute of Public Health. held in Edinburgh from the 27th July to August Ist. Councillor Discos; wanted to know when the Hope Hospital would be relinquished as he wanted to get out some of his plant from the works there. (A voice: "go and fetch it.") • Councillor SHAW drew attention to the fact that Dr. Guest Gornall in his report.' desired to express his thinks to Messrs. Moorfield and Wags" for the assiit- ance they had received from them. He did not know who Mr. Moorfiehl was and he did not remember hav- ing seen his appointment, and he did not know what his duties were. He was in the same position regarding Mr. Wass. He thought that theselection of those two names for publication in the report was rather invidious especially when they remembered such names as that of Mr. Bloomfield which had been omitted. ' Councillor A. Bric.mxn explained that Mr. Moorlield had assisted Dr. tiornall considerably, as canvasser, and had discharged a very important duty. The other officials such as Mr. Bloomfield would be thanked in another way.. Alderman SUTTON in answering the various remarks said that with respect to Councillor Hill's objection to the deputation to Edinburgh, he could assure the Coun- cil that he as Chairman of the Committee did not care whether he went or not. It was a loss of time to him, and ALSO a loss of money. With regard to Councillor Dixon's question he might say that he wuuld be able to get his plant almost at once. But the Committee would have to retain a portion of the building so as to provide f.r any fresh outbreak if one occurred. The minutes were then put and carried. SANITAHY WORKS COMMITTKE.—WATER SUPPLY AT LONGFORD. . .</p>
        <p>• ! The most important minute was that relating to the tender of Messrs. Timmins and Sony to sink a well and guarantee a supply of water of 4,000 gallons per hour at the Longford Works at a cost of £237 10s. Councillor BEAMAN moved the adoption of the minutes. lie explained the reasons which moved the Committee to adopt the course they had pursued in regard to the provision of a well, the paitictilars of which were stated in the minutes. The water for use at Longford had been drawn from the brooks contiguous to the works, but re- cently the supply had practically failed. A suggestion had been thrown out that Appleton wider should be used, but he was of opinion that in the immediate future there would he such a demand for it on the part of manu- facturers that there would be little to spare. It was al- so desirable to have a well inasmuch as during the year ending March, 1892, the bill for water from the Water Company was £176, owing to the failure of the supply from the streams. Councillor flitowsa seconded the adoption of the minu- tes. Ile urged the necessity for the sinking of a well,as at present not only was the supply from the brooks very precarious, but great expense was entailed in clearing the puinta pipes from mud sucked out of the beds of the streams. Couneillor HEPHERD asked what steps were being taken in regard to the removal of the pails at night time. Councillor BEAMAN replied that arrangements had been made for the removal of the nighsoil pails ia the night- time. (Hear, hear.) The arrangement would probably come into force in abAit a week from that night. (Hear, hear.) Councillor flitt, moved an amendment that the minute relating to the recommendation that Mr. Peas and the deputy-chairman be appointed to represent the Committee a*. the Annual Congress of the British Insti- . tote of Public Health be rescinded. He took that course because he did not think that Mr. Deas could be spared ! from his work. It was further a waste of money to send four persons to do what two were were quite capable of performing. . Councillor BOLTON seconded the amendment. Ile': observed that it would be better if tbe Coporation gave , the two gentlemen in question £24 and save theni the trouble of going to Edinburgh. He did not think that ! the reports that were submitted were of any value. • There was often more nonsense than sense in the reports. He thought that it would be better for all con- cerned if there were fewer deputations. Councillor Dr. CANNELL supported the amendment, and said that he considered that it would be sufficient to send the Medical'Officer and the manager of Longford Works. Councillor Roamers spoke in favour of the deputation in question, and said that he was sometimes pained to hear the flippant remarks made about Mr. Deas. Councillor Roberts then proceeded to speak in high terms of Mr. Deas' capabilities and concluded by urg- ing that every facility should he placed in his way for the purpose of increasing his general efficiency. The Towst CLERK then read a list of the subjects to be discussed, and pointed out that it was very prebable that subjects in which the Sanitary Works Committee only were interest ed would be discussed simiaaneously with matters affecting the Health Committee. The amendment was then put and lost. • The minutes were then carried. REPAIR OF THE MAIN ROADS. •• •</p>
        <p>---- - At a meeting of the Paving and Sewerage Committee it was resolved that the contract with the County Coun- cil of Lancashire for the repair of local main roads be renewed for 12 months. It was also resolvea to purchase a stonebreaker at a cost of £285, and to appoint Joseph Whitfield as foreman scavenger. Councillor HEATON moved, and Councillor BuTcHts- SON seconded a proposition that the minute be carried. . . _</p>
        <p>Counc:llor I)lo:inquired when the l'adgate sewerage scheme was likely to to be put into operation. Councillor HEATON replied that the plans would be ready by the next meeting of the Council. The matter would, however, require very grave cunsideration,for he understood that the scheme would be a costly one, ow- ing to the great obstacles in the way. The minutes wercrearried. SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Councillor Firnmo moved, and Councillor SHAW- GREEN seconded the adoption of the minutes. Councillor ROBERTS objected to the recommendation of the Committee" that the members of the ciAmmittee visit the training ship Clio on July 7th, and the Boys' Refuge Industrial School at Liverpool on July 11th next." did not think that all the members of the committee should go. He submitted that a limited number should be appointed by the committee to go. He moved an amendment that the minute be not passed. Councillor CANNEL]. seconded the amendment. Councillor FLEMING said that he was prepared to receive Councillor Roberts' recommendation without a vote being taken. In reply to a question, Councillor SUAW GRVV.N said that the amount contri- buted to the Liverpool Industrial School was over £lOO, and there were at present 30 children located there. The Council contributed £2O to the Clio, where there were 36 boys. The minutes were carried. MUSEUM, LIBRARY, ARTS, AND TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION</p>
        <p>COMMITTEE. A resolution of the above ceminittee is to be the effect that in future discarded books may be given to the local hospitals ; another, that 15 entrance scholarships be offered to elementary scholars, and that Miss A.Chri Ines, Miss 13. Lees' and Miss Laverock be appointed teachers of the School of Art. Councillor ROBERTS moved, and Alderman SUTTON Seconded, the adoption of the ininutes,wbicb was agreed to.</p>
        <p>CEMETERY COMMITTEE. Alderman Thomson moved, and Councillor Mum seconded the adoption of the minutes, which were con- firmed. TOWN HALL, rAIIK, AND BATHS COMMITTEE. The minutes related to the admission of school child- ren to one of the punlic baths on Friday and Saturday afternoon at the charge of a penny each, and to the application of the Workineinen's Com- mittee • of the Infirmary for the privilege ornsing part of the Recreation Uround for a brass band contest. Alderman CHANT/LAY, in proposing the adoption of the minutes, asked for an expressing of opinion 1,02) the Council regarding the particular minute as set out above. Councillor BotrOr; seconded the adoption of the minutes. He said that he did not know whether the Council had power to close the Park to the public and charge for admission. He questioned whether the pablic had not the power to demand admission. The TOWN CUERN said Unit they were enabled to close the Park to the general public and charge for achnission, , provided the receipts were to bo devoted in aid of a de- , serving institution. Councillor FLOOD Said that the Corporation could close the lark for four data. Alderman Idouttr said that he did not approve of the proposal to close the Park. He was afraid that the Council nould be petitioned for assistsuce by instith-</p>
        <p>tions that were not so deserving as the Infirmary. Councillor Punarros asked whether it WAS a fact that holders of season tickets for the haths were only allowed to bathe once a day or pay for , admittance afterwards. Several persons had asked him this question, who had been compelled to pay upon going for R second bath. The MAYOR : A season ticket will admitthent any num- ber of timts. Councillor MUIR : I am sure Councillor Plumpton must have made a mistake. Surely they--- Several members hero corroborated Councillor plump. ton. I The Tows CLERK said the manager of the baths must have been under misapprehension. The subject then dropped, after Alderman Chandley had said .that all those who had been compelled to pay would have their money returned upon application. The minutes were then put and carried. MARKETS COMMITTEE. These minutes were of a routine character, and they were adopted, on the motion of the MAYOR, seconded by Councillor liorcalssos.</p>
        <p>'ltusborm SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting Wllt4 held on Tuesday even- ing, Mr. J. Pritehsrd ()resided and there were present th- Rev. Dr. Gustsldi, Dr. MeGongs!), Messrs. S. Taylor, I. Ilandley, E. Imisou, G. Moss (clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>SCHOOL ATTXNDANCE.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTXNDANCE.</p>
        <p>The visitors' rep irts presented to the Attendance Committee, showed, that as compared with the corres- ponding month last year there was an increase of 57 On the rolls and of 32 in the average attendance. The per- centage of attendance was 84.2, being same as last year Within a fraction.—The reports were considered satis- factory arid were adopted on the motion of Dr. Gastaldi• seconded by Mr. Taylor. THE AUDIT. The Clerk reported that va the 9th ult. the Auditor examined the accounts of the Board and of the &amp;fame and Art Committee and found everything correct.</p>
        <p>THE FEE QUESTION IN RUNCORN. THE GUARDIANSI ATTITUDE DENOUNCED.</p>
        <p>Under date June 14, Mr. John Ashton, clerk to *Run- corn Union, wrote, stating that :—" At a meeting of the Board yesterday, I was directed to write and inform you that the Guardians require that free school places be provided tor all children whose parents are unable or unwilling to pay the BCIIOOI fees."—The Chairman : Have they been told the, we have abolished fees in our schools.—The Clerk replied in the affirmative.—Dr. Ciastaldi : Anyone made application for free education? —The Clerk : Not to my knowledge.—The Chairman said if the Guardians had any grievance they must apply to the Education Department.—Mr. Handley said ! Mr. Ashttai had sent that letter to the wrong authority. It was never intended to send such a letter to the School Board.—Mr. lmison : Is that the letter repre- sented as a request.—Dr. Gastaldi : Yes, that is the request.—Mr. Imison : All I have got to say is that it is a very funny way of making a request.— The Clerk said be had looked up the Education Act to see the position of the Board in this matter. It was very clear that applications for free education had to be made in the first place to the Education Department, who would institute an inquiry as to the sufhciency 01 free places, and if there were not sufficient, then they wonld direct the School Board to make provision.—Mr. Taylor said at the last meeting of the Guardians the idea was thrown out that, no more fees would be paid; if that were done what steps must the School Board take P—The Clerk :It is not lor us to take steps. If they refuse to pay, they are refusing to do their duty.—The Chairman said parents had thu choice of school, and the Guardians were bound to pay the lees of parents who were unable to do so.—The Clerk said Mr. Bowyer, the visitor, in his capacity of inquiry officer had received orders to attend before the Guardians and lay before them applications for school fees. If Mr. Bowyer was ; to attend at the Workhouse.he.yould require leave of . . _</p>
        <p>i absence from the Board.—Mr. Taylor said 'the first reso- lution was that the parents themselves must go.—The . Chairman : What is the reason for taking the matter out of the hands of Runcorn Guardians ?—Mr. Taylor: They could not trust them I suppose.—The Clerk said there were a number of applications for the payment of fees last Friday, and in the ordinary way tile fees would have been ordered to be paid. ' Mr. Imison protested against the word pauper children being applied to the celildren of Nor people, they were in no sense pauper children. The fact of the matter was the Guardians did not seem tie understand their duties; they did not seem to recognise that the poor had certain rights. The object of the Guardians' instruction to Bowyer was to make it impossible to work according to the present arrangement. That was the avowed ob- ject of Mr. Brandretie the mover of the resolution. He was pleased to see that there were two honourably manly protests from members of the School Board, (Messrs. Handley and Taylor) against dragging the poor people to Dutton, and Mr. Taylor appealed to Mr. Brandreth to withdraw his motion and allow the Run- corn Guardians to continue to deal with the school fees in the same way as at present. But Mr. Brandreth seemed to be a sort of dictator and in effect said the Runcorn Guardians had lost the confidence of the Board, and avowed his object of stopping the payment of fees. The only echools which were tree were the Board Schools and the Catholic school, and the Guard- ians said if the parents selected is school where fees had to be paid, the Guardiatis would decline to pay the fees. Now it was one of the primary rights of a man to select and direct the education of his own child, secured to him by Act oeParliament, cud the effect of the pulley of these gentlemen at Dutton would be to deprive par- ents of that right. He questioned if there as one Guardian who knew how many children there were, whose fees were paid from the rates, There were no answers. There were 54 children having fees paid for them. Thirteen of these children were the offsprings of widows, and charwomen; three boatmen, and nine labourers, and one tradesman. They received nothing from the Guardians but these fees and were in no sense paupers. The whole amount of the fees at the last audit was £2 17s. 91 for the quarter ending March, 1893, and these fees were not spread over the whole Union ; Runcorn absolutely paid every penny ; conse- quently the Union as a whole hail nothing whatever to do with payirsg towards them. The Uuien paid its share of the expense of the Inquiry Officer, whose salary was £5. Runcorn township was over a fourth of the rate- able value of the Union ; therefore, that left £3 153. for the rest of the Union to pay ; and the particular part whichMr.Brandreth represented wouldpay 3s.ed.towards the amount, an infinitesimal portion of a penny in the £. It was at the bidding of Dr. Gastaldi and Mr. Bran- dreth that the Guardians had taken this action. This Mr. Brandreth was the same gentleman who not long ago stated that Runcorn paid £1,500 to the Union fund and took more than £1,500 out for its poor alone. That statement was challenged, but lie never attempted to vindicate it nor had he apologised for or withdrawn it. Dr. Gastaldi had been very prominent in bringing for- ward this fee question. He had said " 1 have made my schools free, why don't other managers do the same ; every school ought to be free? The only misfortune was that the ether managers of schools were not in the game fortunate position as Dr. Gastaldi was at the time the Assisted Education Act came into force. Had they, like him, been in the happy position of gain- ing money inseead of losing money depend upon it every voluntary school in the town would have done what Dr. Gastaldi had done. The Aesisted Edu- cation Act had resulted in a loss of £6O or £7O to the National Schools, and if they had the accounts no doubt they would find a similar state of things existed in other voluntary schools in the town. Dr.Gastaldi found him- self richer than before, by the adoption of the Free Edu- cation Act by £l5 or £2O a year. Beiore that Act-came into force Dr. Gastaldi was receiving an income in fees of £77 2s. 9d.from an average attendance of 184. With the same average attendance he was now receiving £92. Therefore, it came with a very bad grace from him to taunt other school managers with not having followed his example. Their poverty prevented them following his example. The Guardians appeared to be taking a rise out of Runcorn. With seven Guardians Runcorn seemed to be in a worse position than when they had five. Mr. Brandreth showed his confidence in Runcorn Guardians by taking Runcorn matters out of their hands. At one time the Runcorn Guardians were con- sidered capable of inquiring into relief cases ; they had now to go to Dutton. Lost their confidence, At one time the Runcorn Guardians were considered good I enough to inquire into fees ; now applications must go to Dutton. Lest their confidence. And this had taken place at the dictation of Mr. Brandreth, and he was astonished that the Runcorn Guardians contented themselves with a mere protest, and that the resolution was carried unanimously. Ho considered this action ' !of the Guardians one of the most offensive forms of tyranny that any authority could try to bring about. They were attempting by illegal action to rois poor parents of the right of control over their own children, and of saying how and where they shall be educated. fle looked upon it as a mean contemptible shabby attempt by the Board of Guardians to evade their respon- sibilities. It was clear as daylight that where the I parent was not able to pay the fees demanded at the ' school at which it was attending, it was the duty of the l Guardians to pay the fees, and the Guardians ought to ' : consider it a privilege to assist such parents. There was far more money wasted by Guardians than this trifl- ing charge for fees, but the average guardians' mind always did seem to be microseopic. There was a mighty fuss some years ago about some potatoes that a certain , pony ate; now again there was a mighty fuss over £3 15s. spread over a Union of over £200,000 rateable value. 11 the Guardians would attend to their own duty and leave the Runcorn School Board to attend to education, which it was perfectly competent to do ; it would be better for themselves and for the interests of the ratepayers. A correspondent in a local paper, who was usually very reliable in his information stated that "con- "sidering that prior to the passing of the Free Education "Act the Guariians were not empowered to pay a higher "fee than 3d. per head, no matter:in what standard the "child might be,and that the managers now receive that "amount from the Imperial Exchequer, it does seem " both ridiculous and unjust that the local rates should " be called upon to give an additional sum." Govern- ment did not pay 3d. per week for each child and nobody with the slightest acquaintance with the accounts would have made such a statement. The Government paid a fraction over 20., and to make it appear that school managers received the maximum sum from the exchequer and then demanded an additional sum from the Guardians was unjust and absurd. He should not like to move avesolution, as he believed that if [Messrs. Taylor, Handley, and the other Runcorn Guardians would stiffen their backs, the difficulty now existing would be easily removud.—Mr. Taylor said at the last meeting of the Board of Guardians he could see clearly the meaning of these resolutions and he pretested against thein,but the Runcorn Guardians were powerless in face of the majority arrayed against them. He did not know what the ultimate result would he. Mr. Imisen had spoken about the five previous Guardians for Runcorn, and stated that now they had sevenehey were of no use. Seven votes were not of much avail amongst 42 mem- bers, and their protests in regard to this fee question : had been unheeded. The Runcorn Guardians as a body had entered their protests against the high handed mover; which had been made by a few men. Dr. Ciastaldi said as he had been attacked. Mr. Nilson: Ne, not attacked.—He believed that the children of all poor</p>
        <p>people should be educated free without beiag under the necessity of applying for the payment of fees. It would be a very charitable thing it the denomihational mane- gers would throw open their schools to the children of poor people. Mr. Imison had made a miscalculati.m in regard to the benefit Ids (Dr. GastalriPs) school hat derived from being declared free. If Mr. Imison. wool 1 come to his house and peruse the hooks for the past year he would see that the school was £64 in del.t. He was at the same time pleased that he had given all his poor people the chance of coming to school without having pence to pay. He added that he had entered his protest against the resolution passed by the Guardians.—Mr. Handley said this question had arisen before he was a Guardian, and he believed the discussion commenced on a certain issue by the Education Department saying that every child was entitled to free education. The way in which the Guardians had been trying to obtain this free education had been rather unfortunate. (Hear, hear.) The terms of the letter which had been sent to the De- nominational schools did not carry out the idea of the resolution passed by the Guardians. The resolution, which was passed on the motion of Dr. Gastaldi, was a request that the managers of Denominational schools to allow children, who had been in the habit of receiving tees from the Guardians, to enter free. It was not meant to be a demand, but a request for a favour. However, it was settled at the last meeting to lay the whole matter before the Education Department, which was the proper Step to take. When a reply was received they would know what step to taka.—Dr. McDougall contended that the Guarding had entirely misinterpreted the cir- cular from the Education Department. It was addressed to parents, and they had yet to learn that the Guardians had assumed the position of locum parentis for those parents who might from time to time apply for relief in the shape of school fees. It was exceedingly unfor- tunate also that Runcorn education should be so con- stantly "bandied" about by those who had so little know- ledge of the circutn•taticee. There was no child who was on the school fees list who was in the least a pauper. It was ultra roires to call them so. .The law of the land did not call them so. He quoted from the Act of Parliament, to stow that the parent had an inalienable right of saying to which school his child should go, and special machinery was created by which their fees should be paid by the Guardians. He condemned the proposal which had been made to drag the parents to Dutton, but with regard to Bowyer he thought they had no other alternative but to allow him to go. Ile did not know whether it was the intention of the Board of Guardians to declare that the Runcorn Guardians were incompetent to deal with the applica- tions for school fees or not, but as they had to pay the fees out of their own pockets, and out of the pockets of these they represented they were not likely to be extra- vagant. At the present time Runcorn School Board educated 500 children "free" at a cost of a thousand pounds to the rat, payers ; and if they were to take the step which seemed to be approved by some people of practically forcing the urifortunate denominational schools to close their doors they would not merely have a ap. rate for education but might look forward to a 24. rate in the near future. The denominational schools were deserving of all credit for doing so much edue,a... tional work at so little expense. He strongly resented the attitude of the Guardians, and condemned what he could only drscribe as an attempt to advertise the poverty of poor people who were in the unfortunate position of applying for school fees. Poverty was DO crime.—The Chairman said if the Guardians approached the denominational managers in a wis.. way, the matter would not be so difficult to deal with as was now , thought.—Mr. Itnison said he had no intention of having an angry controversy with Dr. Gastaldi. The fact that the state of his accounts showed a ' deficiency of £6O did not affect his argument in the slightest degree. He could show an account a great deal more than that on the wrong side. The fact remained that Dr. Gastaldi had a better income under free educe- tionthan he had from fees. Alluding to the fact that most schools were in low water, Mr. Imison mentioned that the Roman Catholics, under the lead of the Bishop of Salford, were agitatine for assistance from the rates and probably other denominational schools would fol. low their lead and the ultimate results might be that they would get some assistanCe from the rates. The denominational schools did the work as efficiently and economically as any schools could do.—Dr. Gastaldi concurred that the denominational schools should re- ceive help from the rates, and that all schools should be free.—The Chairman objected to Dr. McDoueall's state- ment as to the con of the Board Schools. The actual deficiency in the working of the schools of the education of 800 children was £l3O and £45 of that was attributed to the abolition of school fees. The other expenses . _ . . _</p>
        <p>amounted to hundreds, of which the Denominational</p>
        <p>Schools received equal advantages with the Board Schools.—Mr. Imison said deducting the administration expenses of £350 frcm £9OO lett the sum of £6OO as the charge upon the poor rate for School Board education in Runcorn. The voluntary schools provided buildings free, and if the School Board had to provide such build- ings the increase on the rates would be many hundreds of pounds.—Mr. Handley : Has not the Government helped to put up these denominational schools P—Mr.</p>
        <p>Imison : Not at the National schaols.—Mr.liandley said the Wesleyan and Holy Trinity Schools had received help from the Government, and therefore the Govern- ment required some control over these buildings. Ile contended that if the Board had to make provision for a further 600 children the cost would not exceed a penny in the £ on the rates, because they had the land and the administration expenses going on now. —The Chairman stopped the discussion by saying the question was not before them now.—lt was moved by Mr. Handley, seconded by Dr Gastaldi,atid resolved that the Clerk reply to the Guardians referring them to sec. 5 of the Education Act, 1891.—1 n regard to the applica- tions for school fees, Mr. Taylor instructed the Clerk to call the Runcorn Guardians together in the usual man- ner—(hear, hear)—and submit the applications. He would take the responsibility. If the Board of Guar- dians threw the application out, then they could appeal to the Education Department.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>GAS COMMITTEE : THE PROFITS.</head>
        <p>GAS COMMITTEE : THE PROFITS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this committee it was resolved that £4OB, being the amount of the net profit of the under- taking for the year ended 25th March last, be carried to the reserve fund.</p>
        <p>Councillor R. T. FAIRCLOIIMI moved the adoption of the minutes, with the exception of one relating to a ten- der by Mesrs. W. Jones and Sons to repair a boiler, the specifications proving to be inaccurate. Councillor REPRERD seconded the adoption of the minutes.</p>
        <p>...... Councillor SHAW GREEN was sorry to see that there was only a profit of £4OB on the gas works. The pro- fits the previous year were about £3,540. He would like to hear some explanation from the Chairman of the conunittee. He would suggest that inst.( ad of such a small sum as £4OB being carried to the reserve fund, that it be devoted to the relieving of the borough rate account. Councillor It. T. Faracr.onan replied that the diminu- -1 Lion ot profit arose from the following causes. Instead of raisingthe price of gas, as had been done in many districts the committee determined to maintain the pre- sent scale. It was hoped that they would just be able to manage, because the residuals had decreased in value so much. There was also a loss on the meter rents. The prices on the smaller meters had been re-arranged, and the consequent Les of revenue was £240. There had also been a less on coke of £573, and the wages had been increased by £4OB. Thus they would see that the revenue Was really the same as the preceding year. Councillor PLIIMPTON complained that the lamps in Priestley-street had not been lighted until eleven o'clock. recently. The minutes were then put and carried.</p>
        <p>FINE ENGINE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the above committee it was resolved that the new steam fire engine be called " The Captain," and that the firemen be insured in the Norwich and London Accident Insurance A,,sociation.</p>
        <p>Councillor Flurcutstsort moved. and Councillor Eiftl, HERD seconded, the adoption of the minutes, and they wore confirmed. The minute recommending that the Chairman and Borough Surveyor inspect the new steam fire engine was expunged.</p>
        <p>WATER COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The minutes related to the payment of annuities and interest.</p>
        <p>These minutes were carried, on the proposition ol Councillor HEPHERD, seconded hy, Councillor nmoi.</p>
        <p>GENERAL PCRPOSES COMMITTEE.-THE TELEPHONE AND ELECTRIC LIGHTING. The minutes contained a resolution appointing a sub- committee to inspect the Town Flails at St. Helens and Bootle, and offering a premium of £25 for the third selected competitive drawing of the new municipal offices, as well as the following resolution :—"That the Town Clerk's I.lndon agent be instructed to attend the joint committee ofithe Lords and Commons now sitting to inquire into the provisions to be inserted in Bills for the protection of telephone and electric lighting com- panies; and to watch the interests of Warrington so far as they may be affected by the proposed protective clauses, and which unless watched may seriously inter- fere with the use of electricity tor tramways, lighting, and other purposes." The %You moved, and Councillor HEATON seconded, the minutes, which were carried. The Council then rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>GREAT NAVAL DISASTER. ADMIRAL MARKFIAM'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE GREAT NAVAL DISASTER. ADMIRAL MARKFIAM'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The cause of the loss of the Victoria is officially ex- plained in despatches received on Sunday in London. In separate letters Admiral Markham. who commanded the Ca mperelown,and Captain 13ourke,of the Victoria,describe the circumstances leading up to the disaster,and the two accounts are in complete agreement. Put together they give the following narrative. Admiral Tryon'a eriginal intention was that the parallel columns headed by the Victoria and Camperdown should advance, separated from each other by six cable length ocabout 1,200 yards, to a certain point. At this point the ships were to re- verse their direction, each ship turning inwards towards the corresponding ship in the other column. Com- pletely to reverse Redirection each of the two leading vessels would, it is stated, describe a circle of three cables diameter. Beginning the circles simultaneously from points six cables apart, the ships would therefore be brought into the closest proximity to each other at the moment when each had described a semi-circle, and when both had thus reached exactly the middle point between their original positions in column. Captain 'Bourke states that when Admiral Tryon first proposed to execute the manceuvre at six cables distance his Staff Commander.• suggested a distance of eight cables as better, and that Admiral Tryon accepted the suggestion. : Lord Ciillford, however, the flag lieutenant on the Victoria, reports that the order received by him from the Adinirtil was six cables, and when the Staff Com- mander sent him word " to say that six cables were flying and he had said eight cables," the Admiral directed that the signal for six cables should not be counter- manded. At this distance the ships proceeded till the turn came.. The narrative is now carried on by Admiral Alarkham's letter. Receiving the signal to turn inwards and heeling that obedience to the order would bring the ships dangereusly close, Admiral Mark- ham paused for a moment, and was on the point of signalling tor a repetition or verification of the order,but at this moment the Commander-in-Chief flew a second signal to ask the reason of the Camperdown's delay,and there was nothing left for Admiral Markham but to obey the order thus reinforced From this moment the officers of both ships seem to have made desperate efforts to make the turn in time, the Camperdocc n early revers- ing her starboard screws and the Victoria her port screws in order to get the ships round in a circle of ot diameter less than the three cables which must bring the two together. From the angle at which they struck it is probable that the hopelessness of the attempt to pass clear of each other must soon have been realised, and both vessels before they met had for some moments been ring full speed astern, but before the speed of the Camperdown had been materially lessened she crushed iuto the Victoria almost to her centre line. NARROW ESCAPE OF THE CAMPERDOWN. MALTA, WEDNZSDAY.—Her Majesty's battle-ships Camperdown and Inflexible arrived this morning. The Camperdown has a large hole in the port bow 91t by 61t. which is covered with collision mats and plates. She will dock. shortly before the collision with the Victoria all the water-tight dcors of the Camperdown were shut, and the vessel went astern with one propeller and then with both. After the accident the Camperdown's deck forward was only one foot above the water. It is stated that if the doors had not been shut she would have sunk. Even with the doors closed it was difficult to keep her up till the collision mats could be placed in position. Many mln were saved from the Victoria by ropes thrown from the Camperdown and by boats.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>MIDDY'S VIEW OF THE CALAAIIfY.</head>
        <p>A MIDDY'S VIEW OF THE CALAAIIfY.</p>
        <p>A Midshipman who witnessed the sinking of the Victoria from one of the other ships of the squaslron writes home We have a lot of the survivors of the Victoria on board, but their accounts vary greatly. Some say the watertight doors were closed, but immedi- ately the ship went ahead they must have burst open. Anyhow, what is quite certain is that the admiral did not realise the gravity of his situation, or else they would have abandoned the ship at once, instead of try- ing to save her. The discipline was splendid. Not until Site order was given did a single man jump overboard. The last thing that was seen was the admiral refusing to try to save himself, whilst his coxswain was entreat- ing him to go. Another instance of pluck was exhibited by the boatswain of signals, who was making a general semaphore until the water washe.l him away. Unfor- tunately the poor chap was drowned. Many of the survivors are in a dreadful state of mental prostration. Most people say that. Admiral Markham should have refused to obey the signal, hut. I think that Admiral Tryon infused so much awe in most of the captains of he Fleet that few would have disobeyed him. How- ever, he stuck to his ship to the last, and went down in her."</p>
        <p>The Medimi Annual advises Practitioners to remember that when recommending Cocoas as food and treveroge. tor invalids, the name Ciannlag on any packet of Coco. IA a guAntotec of purity.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Joseph W. Plummer, a solicitor. a Cockermouth, vras On Monday at the Cumberland Assizes sentenced to five years' penal servitude.for a breach of trust by the mis- appropriation of £1,400 entrusted to him.</p>
