      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>TERRIBLE COLLIERY DISASTER.</head>
        <p>TERRIBLE COLLIERY DISASTER.</p>
        <p>136 LIVES LOST.</p>
        <p>A terrible colliery accident occurred on Tuesday at the village of Thornhill, near Dewsbury. About noon, whilst the men forming the etty shift were at work in the Combs Pit, belonging to Mr. Ingham, of Blake flail, an explosion took place with results which were app log. There were, it is stated, 120 or more men and boys in the pit, and unhappily there is reason to fear that not one has escaped. The mine was explored as far aa pos- sible during the afternoon and evening, and several bodies were round, including that of the under manager. After the explosion the pit took fire, and it was decided to Rood the mine. Combs Pit has been regarded as one of the safest in the West Riding. It contained several disused workings, and in one of these, it is supposed, there was an aecumulation of gas. It is believed there was an escape of gas, and as naked paraffin lights were in common use it is easy to guess how the explosion was caused.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday it was reported that the explosion had scarcely been so deadly as was at first anticipated. After the first futile efforts to search the workings the idea that any of the men might ever escape alive appears to have been abandoned, and the efforts of the officials in charge of the mine were directed Co potting out the fire which succeeded the explosim and restoring the venti- ation. On Wednesday afternoon, however, when the mine had been brought inio such a condition as to per- mit of its exploration by search parties, the searchers were astounded to find that some of the men were still alive. The work of penetrating into the furthest recesses of the mine was eagerly pressed on, and up to midnight no fewer than nine men and boys had been brought out alive. The explorers, however, are confident that no more living men will be found. The total number of lives lost is set down at 130. t4onie touching incidents occurred in the course of the search. One member of the party found his brother's body,and was terribly affected. On a corve ci e' by a cluster of bodies one of the dead men had written in chalk the following message to his wife :—" Good-bye, Betsy, love. Do the best you can. God help us all." Those who were engaged in the rescue work state that the men who have been killed appear to have succumbed to the effects of after-damp. The Queen, , through the Home Secretary,lias expressed her sympathy with the relatives and friends of the dead,and her desire to be furnished with the latest news. A relief fund has been opened, and it has been decided to add to it the amount which was to have been expended at Dewsbury in celebrating the Royal wedding. The inquest on the dead was formally opened in the course of Wednesday. Over one hundred bodies have been found in the work- ings, and at a late hour on Wednesday night about eighty of these had been brought to the surface.</p>
        <p>Shortly after two o'clock on Thursday afternoon the work of removing the dead babe.; from the Combs Pit at Thornhill WKS coneluded. Altogether it was found that 146 men and boys had been drawn out. Of that num- ber only nine were rescued alive, and of these one died after being brought to the surface. The Coroner's iti- rptiry was continued on Tharsday night for the purpose on identifying the victims, and will be resumed on Tues- day for the production of general evidence. Further contributions have been received to the Relief Fund, in- cluding one of £I,OOO from Mr. E. T. Ingham, the pro- prietor of the cutlery. It is estimated that a sum of £20,000 will be required.</p>
        <p>THE BEST NIOHTCAP.—IIave your sheets washed with trod NOD'S Soap, and they will always bo sweet as roses. troth es sea breezes. There will tee none of the disagreeable smell, so objectionable in other soeps.—A pure Dry hog; in time pow der.—Adtt.(2).</p>
        <p>From Preston it b 3 reported that the a family of pain- ter named William Miller, after partakinit of " pctted meat" prepared by a member dl the family, were taken seriously ill. Aehild of eight years has died, and four other persons suffered severely from the effects of the meat.</p>
