      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>I • IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>I • IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>! In the (louse of Commons on Monday Mr. H. GARDNER !answering a question, said the Board of agriculture had ! decided to issue orders that all cattle arriving from Canada should be slaughtered before being landed. Sir U. liev-SnuTTLEvrosern informed Mr. Heaton that it was nece:oiary that the court-martial as to the loss of the Victoria should be held at Malta. The House again went Into Committee on the Home Bill, resuming cinsideration on clause 5, relating to the executive authority. During the diecussion of one of amendments Mr. CitaranenterN and Mr. T. W. RUSSELL. referred to the speeches made in the past by Mr. Dillon to show that the Nationalist party were not fit to have dominion over the minority. This led Mr HARRINOTON to charge Mr. Chamberlain with having a few years ago held negotiations through Mr. Duigriam. of Walsallovith Irish members in gaol for the purpose of giving HOMO Rule, including the land question and the education question. A heated discussion ensued. After Mr. Cham- berlain had explained these transactions, Sir W. LIAR- COURT said that three months after a speech of Mr. Dillon's of which lie now complained the right hon. gentleman was willing to give the Irish peopie power over the police in as large a degree as it was given under this bill—a statement to which Mr. Chamberlain gave " the most absolute contradiction." The debate was adjourned at midnight.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Tuesday evening, Mr. GLADSTONE. replying to Mr. W. M'Laren, said he did not think the question of women's suffrage in c3nnection with the Irisk Legislature was one for which the Govern- mentcould give special facilities in the discussion of the Home Rule Bill. If any question was an Irish question this WAS one, and moreover the Bill was alreany suffi- ciently laden. Mr. DILLON made an explanation of the speeches by himself which had been called into question by Mr. Chamberlain, and Mr. Harrington quoted tram a letter written by the latter to show how far the right hon. gentleman had offered to go in the direction of Home Rule.</p>
        <p>The House again went into Committee on the Home Rule Bill, resuming consideration of clause 5. In speak- ing on an amendment respecting the prerogative of mercy, Mr. ARNOLD-FORSTER made reference 0 Mr. Sexton's relations with persons whom he accused of serious crimes, and an excited discussion ensued. Mr. Gtatisrostt said he had never in all his exper;ence known it more wanton introduction of venomous matter into a discussion. A number of amendments were re- jected, but sub-section 2 of clause 5 was still under dis- cussion wheu progress was reported at midnight. In the</p>
        <p>[louse of Lords the Earl of ROSEHEMT gave some explanatiens with regard to the intervention of the Governme:it on behalf of Ulf! Armenians condemned at Angora and elsewhere. The two professors had had their sentences confirmed on appeal, but the Sultan had informed the British Government that day that the two gentlemen would be immediately pardoned and sent out of the country. Lord HOSHBERY intimated that the Government were watching the conduct of the Turkish officials very closely.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>WEDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>- • In the House of Commons, on Wednesday. the Lanca- shire, Derbyshire, and East Coast Railway Bill was con- sidered on report, and ordered for third reading. Fll9 House went into Committee on the Home Rule r Bill, and discussion on the amendment moved by the M:1111119 of Carmarthen, to leave out the words "or as may be directed by Irish act," so that the members and officers of the Executive Committeeof the Privy Council of Ireland to aid and advise in the Government should be determined only by her Majesty, was resumed by Mr. PLUNKET. After discussion, the amendment was nega- tived without a division, and further amendments were also negatived. The closure was carried op the motion of Mr. J. Morley, while an amendment moved by Mr. Bousseld was under discussion, and progress was re- ported.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>THURSDAY</head>
        <p>THURSDAY</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Thursday evening, Sir E. tiusx stated that the Court of Cassation had con- firmed the sentences on the Angora prisoner', but that the Sultan had pardoned M. Thoumaian and M. Kayay- an, who, however, would have to leave the country. It VI,IS expected that some other s.enteuces would be re- vised.</p>
        <p>Mr. GIADSTONTY, replying to Mr. Channing, said he admitted that there was no use now in attempting to keep alive the proposal for a Select Committee on the subject of agricultural depression. That ta ing so, the Government were of opinion that it would be desirable to appoint a Royal Commission. Mr. CHAPLIN ques- tioned the Premeir about " the extr ,ordinary delay" in the matter, and was told that he himself had been the chief cause thereof.</p>
        <p>The House again went into Committee on the Home Rule Bill. Amendment.; of the usual kind were discussed up to ten o'clock, when after a fierce protest by Mr. limsonn, the CHAIRMAN, in accordance with the resolu- tion passed last Friday, applied the closure. Clause 5 was added to the bill by a majority of 35. On ctause 6, establishing a Legislation Assembly and a Legislative Council, the numberi were—for the clause, 315 ; against 300 ; majority, 15. Clauses 7 and 8 were carried by udajorities of 36 and 32 respectively. All these divisions took place amid great excitement, and as soon as the last was over the House adjourned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>AT OUR LOCAL CaURCTIES,</head>
        <p>AT OUR LOCAL CaURCTIES,</p>
        <p>THE P.S.A. AT WYCLIFFE. Owing to the holidays, there was not as good an at- tend:mem as usual at the P.S.A. meeting in Wycliffe Cnurch, on Sunday. Councillor Henry Roberts, J.P., presided. He said we had a great deal to be proud of in this country of ouri, and among.st other things was our magnificent navy. He did not speak of the glorious battleships, but of the men who manned those battleships. The history of this country teemed with instances which made us proud of our sailors, but he took it that there was not on record a more magnificent instance of devotion to duty and courage than that which time world witnessed a few days ago, when the Victoria went down with 300 men on board. As Bri- tishers they ought to go down on their knees and thank God that He had put in the hearts of their countrymen such devotion to duty. They were all here for a pur- pose. Those sailors had their duties, and they did them. It might never be the lot of any one in that room ts be Put into such circumstances as that, but every day of life brought its duties. There were little duties many times which they neglected, bid whenever they felt in- clined to let those things slip, let them think of the men on the Victoria and if an man could turn traitor to his duty he was it ,t worthy of tile name of Englishman. Councillor Lockman, of PeNclleton, delivered iirm excel- lent address, in which he gave a graphic description of the barbarous practices, arid the state of the Roman Empire before the dn g of Christ, and compared them with those of the preseat time. He rebutted the charge i made by certain, persons that the world s getting worse, and advised such people to read their hist irv.—Mums N. Roberts was the soloiA, and she sang two solos " He shall feed His flock," and a hymndn a pleacing manner. Mr. Thomas Wallington, Mr. J. Kenworthy, and Mr. A. Lang also took part in the service. REV. T. COOKE AT EOLD-SIREEr.</p>
        <p>The congregation at Bold-street Wesi.yan Chapel were fortunate in having a vet on Sunday by the Rev. Thornas Cooke, the well-known Connexional Evangelist, who has had such marked success in his evangelical work in England, and has recently returned from a very successful tour in South Africa. tie had good congrega- tions at both morning and evening service •,,-1 he preached two impressive sermons, the subject for •he evening being from the words " Son remember " iakeA from the parable or the rich man and Lazarua. He ar- gued that in the future life,the soul would remember the events of this life in en intensified degree, and that the memory of a wasted life would make hell sufficiently awful without any physical torture. Also the memories of a life well-spent mould add to the delights of Heaven.. He contended that nothing that a man did or said was ever lost, nr.d weuld, be brought to his reini•mbrance ire the future world. He therefore impressed upon his- hea re re the great need of living a good lite so that whet' the end came, there would he no wan regrets that their lives had been ‘• thrown away." The sermon made a deep impression on the congregation and large nuinbers stayed at the after meeting, and the minister's invitatiou to deulare themselves publicly as Christians was freely responded to.</p>
        <p>LOCAL PREACHERS' MUTUAL AID ASSOCIATION_ Specl sermons were preached in the Wesleyan Chapel, Pacluate,on Sunday, hr Mr. Broad,of Liverpool,in behalf. of this association. He explained that the associatiotk wag formed for the purpcse of assisting local preachers in their old age. should they require it. It was doing netike work, and deserved their support and aympalby.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>N INCENDIARY NAVAL OFFICE It.</head>
        <p>A N INCENDIARY NAVAL OFFICE It.</p>
        <p>At the IVinehe;ter Assizes on I uesday, Nathaniet Cotton, a commander in the Royal Navy. wag convicted with his wife, of arson. They had taken a house near ::-.4outha.mpton„ put some furniture in it, and affected a , heavy insurance. A fire soon afterwards Oct urred. The furniture wag found to be saturated with oil, sod there were other evidences of a prepared outbreak: Crtalli Cotton was sentenced td seven years and h 3 wife td five years' penal servitude.</p>
