      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>'EXAMINER:</head>
        <p>THE 'EXAMINER:</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, JULY 8, 1893.</p>
        <p>Tnerez are some noteworthy points Lancashire in the report for the past year of Colliers. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr.. HALL, Inspector of Mines for the :West Lancashire and North Wales .district., It is, for example, satisfactory to observe that there has been a reduction in the death-roll as ' compared with the previous year, 'there having been thirteen fewer deaths. But, even making allowance for this decline, the report is highly suggestive of the dangers of the miner's calling. Ne catastrophe of a4thing like the appalling nature of that which his occurred in Yorkshire this week, took place in the district'dur- ing the year, yet the accidents to miners reached ,the number of three hundred and fourteen, of which fifty-nine fatal and seventy .non-fatal hap- pened in West Lancashire, It Would seem, there- fore,, that this is something like the normal acci- dent rate amongst miners in Mr. HALL'S district; and if the same proportion rules in all the other mining districts of the country there must be a lamentably large number of miners who every year meet with an untimely end or are seriously maimed. According to Mr. Blau,, much of this loss of life might easily be avoided,. for he says that "it is not taking too sanguine a view to state that from tho moment gunpowder blasting in coal mines is forbidden by law the loss of life from colliery explosions will be reduced by fifty per cent., and this without any addition to the money cost of the production of mineral." If that be so there is surely little to be said in favour of gunpowder blasting, which has,it seerns,alrearly been discontinued by all the more important firms. In dealing with Another subject, Mr. HALL tretaks upon more debateable ground, and that is Iwith reference to the employment of women and Igirls in connection siith coal mines. In the new Mines Bill now before the House of Commons there is a clause forbidding the employment of females about mines, and Mr. HALL bluntly ex- presses a hope that the clause will never pass into law, lie considers that the energies of "would-be reformers "might be better used in an endeavour to abate the nuisance of the " dreadful language" used by miners,which seem to be worse in Lancashire than in any other county. It ap- pears that in some of the mines the language habitually used by the younger men is simply a "Babel of Oaths." Every one will admit that this is a state of things which might well be amended, but it is not at, ell improbable that the " would-be reformers," as Mr. .11sra, calls them, will argue Athat their metlioq is likely to prove as effective An any other. To 'say the least, it is not desirable that women and girls sLiould be placed, too much in the company of those whose language is simply a "Babel of oaths." That, it may easily be foreseen, will not tend to the elevation of home life, nor to the refinement of the rising generation, who will be the miners of the future. It is, almost of necessity; bound to have entirely the opposite consequences, and therefore the " would-he ieforiners " will really have another mid formidable argil:petit added to their repertoire by this indictment of the manners and language of Lancashire colliers. From other information given in the report it would seem that the labeur of the Lancashire miner is more effective than that of his fellow-workman in North Wales, In West Lancashire there are two hundred and eighty tons of miner.da raised in the.</p>
      </div>
