        <p>year for each person employed, while 10 Wales it is only two hundred and thirty-eln tons. But the difference, it would appear, la, Pu: tially accounted for at all events by the fact Oa; the mines in this,county are, more extensive all( allow of a greater use of machinery.</p>
        <p>SOME pope are fond of pi BritiA Legislation. that thepreseik Session of - nient will be completely barren legislation--except,_of course, the Home Pule and that too, we are told, is to be "thrown by the House of Landlords. These peoPle only predict, but tiy to bring about the fulf of their prediction by means of obstrta Fortunately their opportunities for rnisclii( that direction have been limited by the resell of last week, but still they indulge the hope the session will be a barren one after an. _</p>
        <p>Mi. GLADSTONE intends, so far as it lies power, to thwart the schemes of these poli' "wreckers" is evident from tho letter which read from him on Wednead.i.y at a meeting of Midlothian Liberal -Association. He praises self-denial which has been imposed upoll by the majority which supports 'bit", Payliament, a ' self-denial which hes accepted by the constituencies, and he adds the significant words, "but I not less confident than I was six month! that :this year will not pass away le leaving recorded on the statute book measures of re3at value and mu,, unless these also .should be proscribe" adverse influences, alter having received the liberate approval of the Commons." The" • influences" that hp refers to are to be fel. the House of Lords, and there can be little' that the hereditary legislators would wale! scribe all the do mocratic legislation that: I submitted to them, but their lordships 114' more than one occasion, and in spite of load liminary vannting,s, sho wii that they CO! realise the force of the maxim that discret</p>
        <p>sometimes the better part (d valour. As GLADSTONE says, their method of dealing wUI Home Rule,Bill will probably raise a great "</p>
        <p>cal issue, and it is just possible that the will not care to add to the weight of tile ment that will be laid against them. by - British measures.</p>
        <p>Tim governing autlioritY The Great Drink Question. democratic Switzerland seems, face to face with the drink question. It is said that medical f show that no fewer than fifty per cent. 19, young men, who would otherwise be eligib'e military service, are rendered unfit for the by the physical deterioration which has caused in them by excessive 'drinking• truly a marked and lamentable degeneratit in former ages the hardy mountaineers of, land were the most famous soldiers of And this high reputation of the Swiss based upon the nursery tale of William le' the cruel Gessler, but was gained by valiant '; performed sometimes as patriots fighting freedom of their native hills, and sometimes hired" mercenaries of neighbouring pewBril. now we are told that fifty per cent. of th. men of the country are physically fir' smilitary service. This degradation bag brought about by an insidious enemY as "Schnapps," a vile decoction from maize and potatoes. This licincir it was thought, consumed by the. poorer! of`the peasantry becsiuse it iviia clicept raising of the price of it has not bad the' effect it was hoped it would have. The t having formed the pernicious appetite, i 10800 his "schnapps " even if it be at the sacr ft! healthy food and drink. It may, therefcreL, truly be said that Switzerland; like this 017 other, donutries that might be named, "drink question," and there, as in Euilarlder future well-being of the nation is grestlY dent npou the way in which that question "• With.</p>
