        <p>Th Sr. AMBROSE CHURCH, WIDNES. -•ev. H. E Sampson, curate, preached his fare- -70 n 'sermon in this Church on Sunday morning. In evening the Rev. F.Nighticgale the newly appointed r`oste conducted a portion of the service. LACRY-ST.,WISLEYAN CHAPEL, WIDNES. anniversary' sermons were ;etched in Victoria- rsoad Chapel „ en Sunday by the Rev. R. Harper, of llt formerly. of Widn4s. In the afternoon 'C'rf n'br, delivered an addres4 to the scholars. lections were made in aid of the school funds. ' ST. MICHAEL'S CHURCH, RUNCORN. k-'''half Of the choir of this church, the annual Her- Vje,r6 Zerre preached on Sunday, by the Ilev.J. C.Elstoh, I]nd r 'ar"'sherne. The choir rendered special music. W.:_e the leadership of Mr. Goodwell ; Mr. C. Pownall 4:11 the organ. The c,ollection realised LS 16s. id., relnualifn%after.dfrayi•og the cost of the treat a balance</p>
        <p>RATIIIN6 FATALITY AT RUNCORN. An,incluest was held at the George Inn, Runcorn, on 17 11" Y, on the body of Richard Greenly' nk Sale, aged eria;"rst ^1 5, Poole:lane, Runoorn. On Friday, the de- ifsPri who was a fireman on the Ship Canal works at wia4teFraeldt was bathing with some cornpaniona in that In the centre he sank and a youth named bUnt.74ale Henry Turner seizAl hold of him by the hair, d 'ailed to retain his grasp and the deceased was gPalownnr. Three hours liter his tinily was recovered by turn'a-deia• a. verdict of" Accidentally drowned" was re- MORN WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCUOOL, RUNCORN, iwer service was held on Sunday in Canada • Ile interior of the building was decorated with 1(3.3 aents from the conservatories- of Mr. P. Speakman J.P. sre 1118. Hatlehurst. and Mr. A. Harrison and flowers ° supplied re Plentifull d b the scholars, and were ar- b 'e r 5' a number of ladies of the congregatioh. In a ternonn VI: Rev. J. Hanby delivered an address 'ern the text " Consider the lilies," and the evening aervic, , • nia..Y! he d •w as conducted by Mr. Williams. During the choir and scholais rendered specially selected st."„Sie under the conductorship of Mr. S. Barley. Mr. Littler o was at the organ. On Monday morning the :oisetr.f.s_RWrperfteirfcoarzded. to Dutton Workhouse. at ~1 and Jeweller, 51, High- tu"eon .—.4drt. BRUNsWICK ‘VESLEY AN SUNDAY SCHOOL. RUNCORN. 5t.11.1-43 anniversPrV of this school will be celebrated in tstatili ant's Chapel, to-morrow (Sunday). The school was turaheil a.t the very beginning of the present cen- :Yt and since that time has ever occupied a permanent ...ns_itian and has accomplished a good and lasting work C. 172 Yming The services of the Rev. R. P. Downes for ~3)'• editor oh" Great Thoughts,- have been secured In -"morrow, and on Monday evening the rev, gentle- an will deliver a lecture oi " Tr-tines( n, the Poet Gifted 'with gr:nt literary taste and burn- -714 "nee, Mr. Downes is making his individual.tv d e the religious life of the present time. On Mon- the chair will be taken by Mr. Frederick an old and will-known RODCOrDilill from ooton. Tnianar. To MESSRS. OCELESTON'S WORKPEOPLE,_RUNCOUN. (.140..LTnuisinnration of tile recent marriage of Mr. Win. RP4 'r B.."`en. nf Flookersbrook, Chester, to Miss Miller of 01tewsliire, the emyl oyes of the firm of Messrs. John isad".":4ll and Sons, tannery, Halton-roact, Runcorn, orhnliri to Black pool on Monday. The workpeople, and-1.714d each been supplied With Ft couple of tickets, ends to the number of about 200 left RtItICOrII in ,r 4 asPaeeriaa,ii.t.rain a few minutes after eight, and Blackpool s thed about 10.30. Inclination was the only gm3unie that ruled during the day, and as the weather was autifully fine the many attractions of the seaside Z.rt Zere seen at their best, and appreciated to the otide,,tlor. John and Mr. Wm. Ockleston, Mrs. W. Mrs. Vv n, Miss Ockleston, Mr. G. J. Knight, Mr. and . K As the salnonaYnarecompanied the excursion in a saloon. at nog:dr nienyt otthhreee Chesterhea hearty express Ditton on tPhetYret.''ha juxrenwere given for the newly married couple andy fo'reetlr:e firm, whose kind generosity will not soon be forgotton. REVOLVER CARRYING AT WIDNES. Messrs, Thursday at the Widnes Petty Sessions before J. Brock and 11. Wade Deacon, a man' of hide- Pendant means named Aaron Wat kin, living at Cronton was placed in the dock charged with having been drunk, arn.,d also with having in his possession a loaded revolver. Cracken deposed that last evening, .;4.l7la‘c:9lctel ;ealganreit SVght o'clock, he saw the defendant on the rwev in land at the rear of St. Paul's Church with the f„..(l)llyer in his hand. He took him into custody, and cl that the revolver was a six chamber one, and five cartridges had been discharged. Witness heard one of ov- I'dia fired, and a man told him that a bullet whizz,d lOg his head. Police Constable Farish spoke to see- ant fire one shot. The Chairman said the 'endant and other people must understand that they tonic] n_ Ile "t carry firearms about with impunity, especi- ' 'alum they were drunk. Defendant would have to tinS of 20n. and coals. S4•-•18E'll PRITCHARD 51, High-street, Runcorn. keeps `era CLEO to snit all 'sights, from a shilling a lain—Advt. Th RUNCI:MN WOMF:N'S TEMPERANCE ASSOCIATION. viti.t. Officers and members of this society, in company ti a few friends had a picnic to Helsbv Hills on 4ttii? I 1:s. -• afternoon, The party, numbering between had glorious weather. They rambled on the san7trierouehly enjoyed themselves ; after which T'lti'sr Partook of'an'excellent tea, provided by Mr. Jeffs. .e Party then returned to the hills and held a temper- 'rZrne Meeting, when several pieces were sung. Messrs. wotZal Cooke and Daniel Bisbrown referred to the good done by the society in the past, as they had, set an •'lle to the other organizations in the town. Several in tiF,B from Chester, who had joined the party, took part arta IZim"ting, namely :—Mrs. Williams, Mrs. Taylor, it Killon. The meeting was thrown open, when tditeresting conversation took place, in which the H,,„.,-)wieg ladies joined : Mrs. Simpkins, Mrs. Wynn ev st.,"'Phrs, Mrs. J. D. Jones, Mrs. Garner, Mrs. R. LittLl7:. .3"lisS Burton, Mrs. Wilton, and Miss Annie The half. RUNCORN SoNS OF TEMPERANCE. ')fl Sat Yearly meeting of the above order. was hell Holt. UrnaY, under the presidency of. Mr. Thomas t. 11*.s.itt..hrissuVt: interesting programme having beenf in- e election of officers took place, with the o .ow- i—Patriarell, Mr. James Johnson ; associate, notsvoid Smith ; ecnductors, Messrs. John Taylor and BUTer ‘ll. ; chaplain, Mr. Samuel M. Temperley ; trea- Messr's '''Sr• Daniel Bisbrown ; financial. secretaries, Sir. 1,4 'llionias and William Cooke; recording secretary, John ;card Trlohnsrii(rei sentinels, Messrs. ThomaS Clarke and Terime-D, !lee ; trustees, Messrs. John Jonei!,. Samuel Jarra,—st.!eY. arid Samuel Henderson ; sick visitor, Mr. Mr. superintendent of cadets or juveniles, , . ainiit.n. °lnas . Whyte ; secretary, Mr. Edward y The rol,'• Ileum 4arm•Fi Bisbrown and S. M. Temperley. ffi thp d)wing ocere were elected as representatives to inst. ,-";ll)ivision, to he held at Northwich, on the 17th Jatu„.--"'estowarti Daniel Bisbrown, Thomas Clarke, and a 'Blab • yol n. lake want your WATCIi REPAIRED by skilled workmen PRITCHARD'S.-4d,t „lass 'Lb ' -OSSAGE'S %Vona PEOPLE AT BLACEPOCL. (iosse annual excursion of the workpeople of Messrs. Plae a" Sind Bon Soap Manufacturers, Whines, took aiwe on Saturday! The choice of the place to he visited fres ,fr, s left with the men, and they exercised their utsi 'lll by cleclarirg for Blackpool by a considerable etit-ertV• erhe than acted on this occasion as on all giver.s with the utmost generrsity. To each man was ac,eplpt„wri tickets, in order that wife or sweetheart might ts'ailY him, and to each youth and hoc is given one tison,„* lit addition they are.' provided with a gift in outia in order.to some, extent detray the enst of the bare eenvey the 1,400 persons entitled to take , the trip, thin',' special trains were needed, and Ot Of LIIPSOI-ft Widnes by 7.15 a.m. amid a great v,.`d fog signals. rue joi.irney was accomplished by an alP"t ntite. and soon the IVitinesians were scattered ir if ti • ltrihrom, of the North. Blackpool is 'nsent.,rs,e'thei place for "lay trippers." The amuse- nit,. 0,1 thore are very numerous, and the opportu- for a sail on thiq Ir-riny, many and conycnient, and t sinrs from th‘• metropolia to judge by tipot,rll'a rifler tray 111,ted hither and thither and the • and Finial'," c.ires they presented had enjoyed eives to hi. full. The hrilliant weather which pre- allow ,4dded no little to the plenanre of the visitors. It he added that Gotet.oe's Prize Band accompanied f eaciirsion, and their admirable playing caused much "p„Nan'able comment. At 6.40 the first special left xv,rd?t;rnad Station and the last at 6.50, and the home- d'',urneY watt accomplished a few minutes after einck. The e.hiefa in the office and in the work- accompanied the excursionists. The best of order good behaviour prevailed throughout.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN SEAMEN'S INSTITUTE. The teachers and choir of this' Institute had a picnic to Delamere Forrest on Monday. The drive was highly enjoyed, and a pleasant cliy spent. WILLIAMS DEACON AND MANCHESTER AND SALFORD BANK. On Monday this Bank removed to their new premises in Victoria Buildings, Savages Bridge, Runcorn. The rooms are cowl.' )dioes,handsomely furnished in walnut, .and there is a strong room of special construction and the necessary safes. DEATH STATISTICS OF RUNCORN. The annuisl report of Dr. McDougall, medical officer of Runcorn, shews that the number of deaths from all causes registered during the year, within the Authority's sanitary district was 441, compared with 463. in the previous year. The 441 deaths make an average death rate of 22 per thousand of the estimated population for the 12 months, compared with 24 per thousand for the preceding year. Excluding the 26 deaths due to injuries, the death rate was reduced to 20.7 per thousand of the estimated population. Go to PRITCIIARD'S, 51. High-street. Renown, for the bee. FIVE GUINEA WATCH a workingman can have.—Adrt. Sr. EDWARD'S CATHOLIC CHURCH, RUNCORN. A great festivity was celebrated at this church on Sun- day, in common with all the Catholic churches in Eng- land, who niacte a solemn consecration of this country 0 Mary the mother of God, and St. Peter the prince of the Apostles. The sacred building as well as its surround- ' ings were beautifully decorated, bunting, statues, and' flowers being tastefully introduced. In the morning Father Diabel. S.J., of Ditton College, delivered an eloquent sermon on St. Aloysius Goudryaga, the patron of purity for the youth of both sexes.—After the sermon the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi made a few remarks, and em- phasized the doctrines couzained in the discourse of Father Nebel. After this the consecration service was racited by the rev, doctor a...A the congregation. ' This concluded, a procession was formed. In advance walked the altar boys carrying the Cross and lighted candles; next came the school girls attired in white, bearing flowers, who were succeeded by the members of the League of the Cross with the statue of Our Lady, and after them were the members of the Sacred Heart, followed by four gentlemen bearing aloft a beautitill new statue of St. Peter. The rear was brought up by Father Dialsel in his officiating garments, and the members of the congregation. Dr. Gastaldi ably officiated as master of the ceremonies.</p>
        <p>MARMAGE OF Miss CROSBY, RI:WORN. On Thursday morning the nuptials between the Rev. C. E. Norman, of Swindon, youngest son of the late Mr. 'fhomas Norman, of Lindon Lodge, nigher Runcorn,and Miss Elizabeth S. Crosby, daughter of Mr. T. Crosby, Greenway-road, Runcorn, . were celebrated at Trinity Wesleyan Chapel, Halton. The officiating minister was the Rev. J. Hanby. The eveht, although a very quite one, was witnessed by a large number of friends and well-wishers, the contracting parties being exceedingly well-known throughout the district. Mr. F. J. Norman acted as best man for his brother, and Miss Polly Crosby filled the capacity of maid to her sister. The happy pair left soon alter the service for Ilfracombe, where the honeymoon will be spent. The presents were numerous and costly. They included a Doulton ware reading lamp aad ladies companion, from the scholars and teachers of Granville-street Wesleyan School, where she had been mistress for five years. Mr. John Simpson, in handing over the gifts on Wednesday afternoon, referred to the valuable work Miss Crosby had performed in connection with educational matters in Rimcorn. She had been associated with the school for nearly a dozen years, in the position of teacher and mist-fog, and had gained the respect, esteem, and lore ef the children of many families in the town. By her departure they would sustain a great loss. He trusted her married life would be a happy, long, and prosperous one. Miss Crosby returned her sincere thanks for the demonstration of kindly feeling extended to her by the scholars and her co-workers. Parrcusao's hare now stood the test for fifty years, and quality and fiNt-ehiss Workmanship are still guaranteed.— _deist .</p>
        <p>A CHEMICAL WORKS FOREMAN AND HIS WIFE. OR Monday morning at the Runcorn Petty Sessions, before Mr. C. Reynolds and Major Hazt,hurst, James Tudor, foreman at the Weston Chemical Works, was summoned by his wife f,r deserting her—Mr. A. Fletcher, Northwich, defended. The complainant stated that she married the defendant about 14 years ago. Seven years afterwards her husband left her and went to live at Dublin. She followed him there, and lived with him for about three months, when he left her and her two children destitute, and went across to Runcorn. She made application for relief at Duhlin, and the authorities paid her passage back to Runcorn. and ad- vised her to go to the Workhouse, which she did. After staying at Dutton Workhouse for a short time she came out, and since then had earned her own living. Defen- dant paid her 6i. per week for the maintenance of the two children. One of them died,and then defendant re- duced the payment to 3s. per week.—Cross-examined by Mr. Fletcher : She was married to defendant in November, 1877, and they separated in 1878. A,ter liv- My apart until 1884 they agreed to live together again, and they took a DOOM' at Runcorn. She admitted sign- ing an agreement in 1888 to live apart. Since then she had summoned him for desertion, but after her solicitor had gone into the case, it was withdrawn. Mr. Fletcher contended that there was no desertion in this case, as an agreement had been drawn up. The bench dismissed the case, and Mr: -Fletcher, on behalf of the defendant agreed to pay the plaintiff 3s. 6d. a week rather than she should go into the Workhouse. LATCIIFORD CHOIR TRIP. The annual trip in connection with Christ Church choir, Latchford, took place on Saturday last to Southport. The members, together with their wives and a few friends, left Warrington at 7 10 a.m.,Southport being reached in due course, and an enjoyable day was spent. The arsangements of the picnic were satisfactorily carried out by Messrs. R. Gorton and W. Starkey. CLAIR AGAINST A RUNCORN SILT MERCHANT. At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before his Honour Judge Ffoulkes, John Cleave, boatman, Barnton, claimed from Messrs. Raynor ' and Co., salt merchants, Runcorn, the sum of .£3 181. 7d.,for damages sustained to the boat ' Harriet,' and loss of time, owing to a collision, which it was alleged was due to the carelessness of the defendants. Mr. Longton, Warrington,appeared for the plaintiff and stated that the claims was made upon repairs to the boat. On March 30, plaintiff was going to- wards Barnton from Preston Brook, on the North Stafford canal. At the Fir Tree curve at Dutten,he shouted to the boy who was in charge of the horse to slacken time horse's head and shorten the line. Four bouts belonging to defendant were coming in the opposite direction drawn by two horses. The driver in passing, lifted the lines over the bead of the plaintiffs horse,and thereby pulled his own boat on one shie,the result being that it collided with plsin- tiff's boat, and staved in two planks. Mr. Burton, of Runcorn, on behalf of defendants denied that there was any negligence on their part. His Honour I gave judgment for the whole claim.</p>
