      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>ROYAL WEDDING CELEBRATIONS.</head>
        <p>ROYAL WEDDING CELEBRATIONS.</p>
        <p>WIDNES. The Royal welding celebrations at Widnes took the form of a treat to 10.000 school children' the cost of which defrayed by public subscription. The Mayor had not deel4rea the day a general holiday, but it being Thursday (the tradesmen's weekly half-holiday in the borough), the great majority of the places of business in the toyer' were closed at noon. The afternoon's proceed- ings commenced about one o'clock, when the scholars assembled at their various schools, and from there they made their way to the vacant land at the rear of St. Paul's Church. All the twenty-three schools arrived here at half-past one, and proceeded to their allotted places in the square fronting the Town Hall. The Mayor, • attired in his robes and chain, and the Corporation • walked round the Town [tall square, and afterwards ascended to the balcony of the Town Hall. From here the Mayor addressed the children. Three cheers were given for the Mayor and Mayoress, and the children then re-formed into procession, and ! paraded the town. The school managers were 'allowed 6d. per.head by the Celebration Committee. The day's feigivities concluded with a display of fireworks provided by the Mayor. It is probable that the surplus of the fund will be devoted to giving the old folks a treat in abut a week's time. RUNCORN. There was no organised celebration here. Work pro- ceeded as usual. 'lite solicitors and a taw tradesmen made holiday. ;tint there was a display of flags and bunt- ing in the streets and at the Deck. In the evening a fireworks display was given at Picow Farm by Mr. James Percival who had collected sub-scriptions. Some hundreds of peonle were present. FRODSHAM. There was no organised effort made to celebrate the Royal marriage. Business was conducted as Usual. In honour of the occasion, however, the workpeople of Mr. Rigby, of Frodsham Midge Corn Mills, and a number of those belonging to the Union Church, Frodsham,were conveyed to Belsby, where a thoroughly enjoyable time was spent. HALTON. In honour of Vie nat ions! event the children attend- ing 1141 ton Schools, as well as ttoir parents, were pre- sent at a garden party kindly given by Sir Richard and Lady Brooke, of Norton Priory. The guests numbered between 600 and 700. The children were attended by ir teachers. Soon alter entering the grounds (hey sang the Nst;onal Anthem to the accompaniment of the band of the 2nd Em viol Chester's Regiment, under the conductorship of Mr. Outhwaitc The cateting was ably carried out by Messrs. Gamon and Rowe, of Hal- ton. In the absenco ot Sir Richard and Lady Brooke. The numerous visitors had their wants zealously attended to by the agent, Mr. Evans, assisted by Mr. Lathom, Clerk of works, and others connected with the estate. • During the afternoon and evening the Runcorn Volun- teer Band played numerous selections, to the delectation Gf all present.</p>
      </div>
