      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>EN MINER, SATURDAY, 'JULY 8, 1893. TOWN COUNCIL NOTES.</head>
        <p>THE EN MINER, SATURDAY, 'JULY 8, 1893. TOWN COUNCIL NOTES.</p>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Warrington Town Councillors were, on Tuesday evening, in a somewhat talkative mood. One or other of them had something to say on nearly every item on the agenda. The ball was opened by Councillor 11. Roberts who challenged two resolu- tions submitted by the Watch Committee, granting licenses for the performance of stage plays at the Royal Court Theatre, and the Theatre Royal, Scot- land-road. Mr. Roberts did not object to the per- formance of stage plays at either of these places ; what he objected to was a power to sell liquors at both which the licem,es carried with them. It was said that until the theatrescame in- to the hands of their present prvtietors the licenses had always been granted subject to a condition that drink should not be sold ; now no such con- dition was attached to the licenses, and it was to this " new departure " on the part of the licensing authority—the Watch Committee—that Councillor Roberts objected. 1</p>
        <p>The Councillor's speech was broken into by a series of interruptions. Alderman Harr'son was soon on his feet to complain that Mr. Itoberts' objections were itrevelant, inasmuch as the Watch Committee had, he said, only to consider whether a theatrical license should be granted or withheld; as to whether (mink should or should not be sold, they had no power to say. The Mayor ruled this objection out of order, but in a very few minutes after he said he was ad- vised by the Town Clerk that Councillor Roberts was out of order in raising the question as to the sale of intoxicating drinks. Mr. Itoberts, however, continued to tight his battle tenaciously and adroitly, and by a little strategic " tacking," so to speak, he contrived to say what he had to say, in spite of interruptions and calls to order—authorised and unauthorised—which were neither few nor far between. In conclusion, he moved as an amend- ment that the two resolutions be referred back to the Committee. Councillor Dixon seconded this amendment, and it was supported by Councillors Fleming, Browne, and Shaw-Green. Mr. Fleming, speaking as a member ot the Watch Committee, aid not think the theatres were suitable places for the sale of liquor. Mr. Browne commented upon the un- fairness of allowing the present proprie:ors of the theatres to sell liquors,while the privilege had been rigidly refused to other applicants ; and Mr. &amp;law Green expressed the opinion ',hat there had been a deliberate intention to give the unconditional license now that the theatres were in ()trier hands. On the other hand the defence of the Committee wa4 undertaken by Aldermen Chandley, Sutton and Harrison. It was to the effect that the only two pints the Committee had to consider when applied to for a license were the character of .the applicants and the P uitabili ty of the buildings. If the com- mittee were satisfied on these two points and granted a license, the licensees could get tie power to sell liquor from the excise autheriries, whether the Committee wished it or not. After tile pros and cons had been exhaustively debated, lit. Roberts's amendment was put to the meeting and defeated, but only by eleven votes to nine, tl.ree or lour members remaining neutral.</p>
        <p>Councillor Shaw then, in racing parlanco " took up the running." Ile moved an amendment to the Committee's minutes on the same lines as that of Councillor Roberts, but only applying to the theatre in Scotland-road. According to his own experience the performances at this place were more of the nature of variety entertainments, and as entertain- ments of this description drew together more child- ren the sale of drink wsuld be "setting an example to children of tender age that ought not to be set," This apparently unexpected moVeMent on the part of Mr. Shaw seemed to disturb the equanimity of the" liquor" party and practically no opposition was offered to the amendment. Alderman Harrison was partially apologetic on behalf of the Watch Committee, and Alderman t,utton was willing to have the resolution Bent back " for further consid- elation." Accordingly it was sent back.</p>
        <p>On the minutes of the Street Improvement and Lighting Committee the discussion was desultory. Two or three members appeared to be so anxious about the lighting and erection of lamps as to lead Alderman Sutton to remark that they seemed to think more about lamps at this time of the year than when illumination was more needed. .Alder, man Chandley suggested that the tenants of Cor- poration property should he placed under a six months tenure rather than three months as was proposed, atid this suggestion was agreed to. Councillor B. T. Fairclough urged that the Corpora- lien should have submitted to it plans of the pro- posed laying out of land for streets, but it was !explained that the Corporation had no power to insist upon this. Mr. Fairclough, also again raised the subject of removing the railings—" spears " he called them—from the front of the Lion,liotel, and he thought that as Nlest-re. Walker and Company ! had been granted by the Corporation the privilege lof laying level crossings in•Vallain-Isne they should reciprocate with regard to the railings. At this point Councillor Bolton threw out the suggestion : Oh, give them a license or two."</p>
        <p>In moving the minutes of the Health Committee Alderman Sutton mentioned that the smallpox epidemic was practically extinct, there being only two or three cases in the hospital, and these, it was expected, .would be discharged very soon. lie also spoke in praise of tie services of Dr. Guest Gonna' at the smallpox hospital, and of the valuable report on the epidemic that had been prepared by WM! Councillor Hill raised some objection to the sending of a deputation to Edinburgh, but this objection was withdrawn after an explanation by Alderman Sutton. The Sanitary Works Committesis minutes were submitted and explained by Councillor Beaman, the chief proposal being that, for sinking a well at Longford works from which it is expected to get a cheaper supply of water. It had been suggested, Mr. Beaman said, that the Committee should get Appleton water, but he anticipated that in the not distant future there would be such a demand for that water by manu- facturers that there would be little to pire. Coun- cillor Hill again objected to the sending of a ' deputation, consist ing of the deputy-chairman and Mr. Dear, the manager at Longford works, to the Congress of the British Institute of Public Health lat Edinburgh. Councillor Bolton, who said he had never been on a dsputation and therefore did not .know what they did, seconded the amendment. In supporting the Committee Councillor Roberts eulogiscd the work of Mr. Dens at Longford. He said the CorporVinti had not a more capabls and a more loyal man over any of its departments. The work at Longford was being douo in the most. pleasant and the most successful manner possible. The amendment of Mr. Hill was only supported by four votes, and the Committee's minutes were passed in their entirety.</p>
        <p>Among the proposals of the Paving and Sewerage Committee was one for the pawing of the upper part of Ilridge-,treet with setts, Alderman Chandley, the Mayor, and Councillor Roberts dis- sented from this proposal, the Mayor remarking with collcquial force and simplicity that there was hound to be a row" amongst the shopkeepers if the Committee carried out their intention. The minutes were, nevertheless confirmed.. The School Attendance Committee's proposal that the whole committee should pay a visit to the" Clio " woe not • received with favour, and an amendment that a , smaller deputation be sent on these annual excur- I adorn', moved by CJuncillor ltvberts, was accepted by Councillor Fleming, the chairman, without a discuseiou. '</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>'The business of the Museum, &amp;c., tiommittee, the Cemetery Committee, Chi' the Baths Committee next passed under the review of the Council. Councillor Pluilepton, u!cin the minutes of the last- named Committee, raised the question as to whether season ticket holders were to be charged for going into the baths if they went a second time on any particular day. It was explained that such charges mu-t, haVe been mule through a mieunder- colliding, as season ticket holders could go to the baths as often us they liked, and Alderman Chand- ley promised that all cLarges that had been made would he tefonded. On the Gam Coma-line's minutes Count:Tor Shaw Green brought out the fact that - there has been a considerable drop in the profits, mainly due, it was explaine,T, Illy Councillor K. T. Pairclougb, to a re-adjusts 'silent of meter charges, a fall in the value of coal, and a rise in wages. The Fire Engine Com- mittee had provided that the Chairman and the Borough Surveyor should .go to London to see the new tire engine before it was painted, but this par- ticular deputation was vetoed remorselessly by the Council. The adoption of the General Purposes i Committee ei recommendation without discussion, I brought a rather prolonged tneetirg to an end.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</head>
        <p>BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>As Sit' John Rigby i •ft native of Runcorn, the following tribute paid to him by "P. P.." in " The Sun" on Wednesday, will be read with interest " Sir John Rigby, the Solicitor-General,has steadily advanced in, the. favour of the House of Commons, and is now one of its most popular members. The :peculiarities of speech and delivery which at. one time menaced his Parliamentary effectiveness, have 'positively become part of the stock of delight and pl,easure in the 11011RO of Commow,and nobody now whuld have him different or planed down to the dull monotony oil he typidal Parliamentary level. Above I. on TI urs lay night, the: int el ectual lion which lurks under his massive and tranquil frame burst forth. tie lind been tor ured for hours by answer- ing and answerieg Net again, all kinds of at lipid and silly questions, and at last he fired out ; and With a certain tine, though not uncomic or anll'n%r Is - vehemence of manner, told his as.sailants it he thought them very stupid and very igno- rant.</p>
        <p>Thi.4 raised a little storm. There were all kinds of el.arges against the Solicttor-General. lie Wtl.4 t-is and t hat, and above all things he had lost his tettmer. But Sir John beamed all over his tine,kindtv UV! intellectual face, and in the end abashed his enemies into delight and tranquility. And thus manntlering gently from night to night, repeating the same arguments, uttering the same old taunts, asking the same old questions, now and then burst- ing into the fiery lii me of passion and disappoint- m and baffled venom, we amble through the Rowe Rule Bill."</p>
        <p>The L. aiol N. W.-Railway Company have reduced the fare LA bicycles across Runcorn Bridge tor the single journey from threepence to twopence, and the double journey instead of being lOxpence is now threepence: Lcongratulate tlte cyclists upon the concessioeS they have f)htained. At the same time the reductions should have been more far reaching. Threepence is still charged for the privilege of tak- ing a perambulator acros the bridge. Considering that the company render no help in getting the vehicle up the steps or in propelling it across the Bridge, I think the charge is extortionate, and ought to be reduced to at least as lOW a fare as that asked from cyclists. It's an expensive matter fora poor woman to visit her friends in Widnes, when 81:eline to take the baby in a perambulator.</p>
        <p>Mr. Imison's reply tot he Runcorn Board of Guar- dians on the school fee question, made at the meet- ing of the School B;tard. on Tuesday is well worth rending. It was getting time that someone spoke out. There is only one idea the Guardians have clearly fixed, and that is their objec- tion to, paying school fees, under a Free Education Act, but from the resolutions and speeches made they had no knowledge of how to accomplish what they desired. It was clearly shown at the School Board on Tuesday that the parents have the choice of School and that the I Guardians must pay the fees of such as are unable to do so. This is an issue that the Guardians , cannot evade. Mr. Brandreth and his friends had evidently realised this when I hey proposed that the ; unfortunate people should he dragged to Dutton Workhouse to make applications for fees and then finding this would raise a storm of indignation de- cided that the Inquiry officer should be put to that trouble, and the power of granting fees taken out of the hands of the Guardians. It is unworthy of a public body to indulge in a system of harass ; like this, and the Guardians of the township are to be congratulated upon determining to combine to deal with application' for school fees as usual. If the law is bad the Union Authority ought to seek to have it amended in a proper and dignified manner.</p>
        <p>There is no doubt the law does require amend- ment. Prior to its passing the Guardians were empowered to pay a school tee of only threepence. The Government now make a grant within a frac- tion of that amount, Mr. Imison was severely censorious because it was stated in this column last week that the payments equalled threepence. Reckoned on the basis of the, Education Depart- ment the free grant is equal t 2i., so that the mistake was not so glaring. For those children whom the Guardians only paid 3.1. for previously the Denominational schools receive 2fl. from the Government and in addition receive ahe grant from the Guardians. There is no doubt that the Legislature did not intend much a state ot things to exist. The Education Acts cannot remain in their present form. The next step, and it will he taken in the near future will be to declare all schools free and put them under the control of the rat epayers through properly elected repreFeutati ces. And considering that Denominational schools, of what- ever description, are maintained by public funds, this is only a just conclusion. In the meantime, however, let thereto no petty squabbling; and no resolutions passed meant only to harass;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>ANNUAL Till' OF WORKHOUSE CHILDREN FROKDI;ITON.</head>
        <p>ANNUAL Till' OF WORKHOUSE CHILDREN FROKDI;ITON.</p>
        <p>The children of the Dutton Workhouse, aCcotnpa- nied by the master and matron, Mr. end Mrs. Cun- nall, and Mr. Robert Naylor and Miss Elizabeth Moran, schoolmaster and schoolmistress, alsa Mr. Parkinson, Ashton, and Mr. Heywood, Frodsham, guardians of the Runcorn Union, had their fifth annual trip to the seaside on Monday. The place chßsen I this year was Blackpool, Leaving Preston Brook by the 1 7.53 a.m. train, and journeying by the London and North-Western Railway, the much-favoured watering- place was reached at eleven o'clock. Before starting the children were supplied with buns and r,i ,v,te with meat pies. Immediately on arrival they were regaled with ginger beer, then a visit was paid to the Prince of Wales Baths, where a varied and instructive entertain- ment of scientific and ornamental swimming was wit- missed. Dinner, which consisted of a bountiful supply of mast beef and mutton,potatoes and other vegetables, and afterwards fruit pies,was partaken of at one o'clock the children doing ample justice to the good things pro- vided. After spending a pleasant afternoon, tea, which consisted of bread and butter, seed and bun loaf' was served at 5.30 p.m., the catering of Mr. and Mr. Riles- ton, of the Talbot dining rooms,giving very satisfaction. The return journey was commenced at sever, p.m. and all reached the Union fLuse, Dutton, safely about: 9 30 p.m., well satisfied with their day's outing. A quantity of sweets and ginger nuts were provided by the genero- sity of Messrs. Heywood and Williams. guardians 'for Frodsliatn. The trip fund " was subsaribed by the guardians and ex•olicio guardians and a few friends, to whom the best thanks are due for their liberality.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>WIDNES. SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>WIDNES. SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>• The monthly meeting was held -41 Tuest,!ay tinder the presidency el Mr. 11. Wade Deacon, .1.1'.. CC. present : Major NS arcing, Dr. OT.e.effe, Messrs. Quinn, Barlow, Brown and Flynn. The District Ifclucational Committee reported that during June there were 16 applications for free places which were granted and :247 persons were summoned I before the Committee for contravention of the Education Act; 32 were prosecuted 'Wore, and 31 con- , victed by the magistrates. One case was withdrawn,— , The report was adopted.—The Finance Comniittee recommended the payment of accounts amounting to £915 195. 101, and reported that • the receiptf. for the month amounted to £1,889 Bs. Id. The Committee further reported that the Corporation of Widnes have paid £1,500 to the credit of the Board's account since the last meeting. The Board passed accounts at the meeting amounting to £915 19s. 10c1. which when paid will leave the Board £970 54. sd. .debtor to the Treasurer and they beg to recommend that a letter be sent t the Corporation urging upon them the Icsirahility of paying the balance of the precept (ago) without delay .—The minutes were passed without comment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>11RODS111A)i.</head>
        <p>11RODS111A)i.</p>
        <p>--- Msyrnonisr FREE exuacit.—On Sanday the annual Services in connection with the sunday Schead were conducted by Mr. 11. Hatton, of Winsford. The choir Was augmented by the elder scholars and teichers, and accompanied hy the ornin end. orchestral band. The offettnries realized £l4 CA. 2id. SUNDAY SCROOT. ANNIVERSARY AT EINGSL anniversary Fbrvices in aid of the Wesleyan Methodist Sunday tiara d werelida OR Sllnd ay last, ri I ter.'.oon and evening, whf.ll Mr. 13. Wai n %wig ht ,of Ormsk irk preached. Tbecolleetions aim.unted to £l4 Os. 7d. A Lamm 13mrt34.—During the lt,iyal Show at chei;ter, Messrs., Heywood and Masiie, Chemical end Patent Manufacturing Firm, PrOtiSIISIII Bridge. purchased the largest holler exhibited by Messrs. llawksley Wild and Co., Sheffield. The boiler is constructed of live eighths inch steel plates, weighs 20 ton, and was catalogued at £4OO. Two days were occupied in removing the h irone the show yard to its destination bv means of a traction engine. A start Was made on Monday morn- ing, and Frodsham Bridge was not reached until Tues- day night. Time delay was cawel,throutth the road at Helsby, which is under the control of the County I twice giving way, and the trolly bearing the boiler was embedded up to the a difil.mity was (•;Iperi- 'need in releasing it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>WEE ROYAL WEDDING.</head>
        <p>WEE ROYAL WEDDING.</p>
        <p>A BRILLIANT CEREMONY.</p>
        <p>In magnificent weather as early as six o'clock on Thursday morning the public commenced to take up positions around the approaches to St. James's Palace and Marlborough House, and by nine o'clock dense masses of peoplu lined Pall Mall and St. James-st. No one was admitted to the Chapel Royal until after ten eclock,although many favoured per- sons holding tickets were in waiting much earlier at the Ambassadors Oourt. Pall Mall was literally one blaze of crimson, and even the garden walls of the Prince of Wales's residence were covered with draperies of the same Royal colour. St. James's-at. was all aglow with .the successful efforts of the West-end decorators,&amp;c.,a garlanded length of ever- greens terminating at the Palace end with a couple ot stately Corinthian columns of imitation marble, between which was suspended a rich purple velvet banner bearing the inscriptions, in letters of gold, "All happiness be yours for evermore." AT BUCKINGHAM PALACE. The front of Buckingham Palace presented an anim- ated scene. The railings of St. James'-park were thronged with the public. The military guards kept the way, and there were about 5,000 additional police on duty. Far away over the greensward in St. James'-park the country people made their way towards the vicinity of Buckingham Palace, all anxious to get a glimpse of the Queen. As the morning wore on the sun shone out with tropical heat, and unitnellas and parasols were brought into general use, both on the large stand erected within the palace gates and by the spectators in the park. A troop of Lancers with their waving plumes added to thegeneral effect of a spectacle already lull of colour, contributed by the toilets of the ladies and the uniforms of the mili- tary. The Blue Marines, the Coldstreams, the Middle- sex Yeomanry, the Lite Guards, the Metropolitan Volim- teer Engineers, the 15th Middlesex, and the Post Office Volunteers were all represent,d either in the guard of honour or in keeping the ways iii the vicinity of the Palace. At a quarter to elevenths' band of the Cold- stream Guards paraded in front of the Palace, playing martial airs. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>FAIN ri NG SPEC TATORS.</head>
        <p>FAIN ri NG SPEC TATORS.</p>
        <p>The heat became so oppressive that severs] ladies fainted. Even some of the volunteers on duty had to be removed into the shade. Every now and again members of the ambulance corps could be seen running hither and thither in the crowd and bearing off some fainting spectator, male or female. It was about this time that the Horse Guards Blue, with helmets gleaming and swords Bashing, trooped into the court yard and drew up to the right of the Royal entrance. The Life Guards, their breastplates shining in the sun and their white plumes drooping, followed, and were marshalled on the opposite side, the two detachments being thus ready to torm escorts later on. When shortly before .half-past twelve the troops assembled in the courtyard drew up into close order, and were recruited by the Indian and colonial military from without the gates, tlu. scene was a striking one. The spacious courtyard was converted into a sea of white and pink plumes, inter- spersed with which were the picturesque turbans of the Indians and the snow-white helmets of the colonials. Away in the distance along the Mall and beneath the trees in the park was one dense mass of people.</p>
        <p>THE ROYAL PROCESSIONS. At ten minutes past twelve the patience of the crowd was rewarded, and the head of the first procession, con- sisting of members of the Royal family and Royal guests was seen to turn out of Piccadilly into St,. James-St. The procession, which was escorted by a field officer's escort of the Horse Guards Blue, came in for a 'warty reception, the King and Queen of Denmark in particular being loudly. cheered. As it approached the palace the guardsmen presented arms, the cavalry came to the salute, and the band of the Coldstream Guards played the National Anthem. A minute later these 11,..yal personages were followed I by the bridegroom's procession, attended by a strong escort of the Royal Horse Guard's. Again the troops presented arms and the strains of the National Anthem once more burst forth as the carriage containing the Duke of York, the Prince of Wales, and the Duke of Edinburgh passed the Garden entrance. There -vas another short pause and then tumultuous ! cheering announced the approach of the bride's proces- sion as she passed down St. James-street. The Princess May, who was accompanied by her father and Prince Adolphus of 'Peck, had a magnificent reception, and the air was rent with cheers as she drove past the Quad- rangle into the Mall, where another ovation awaited her. At 12.30 the Queen's procession approached, and the enthusiasm of the loyal crowd was once more thoroughly- ! aroused. The only occupant of her Majesty's carriage was the bride's inother. Both royal la lies evemed to be much gratified at the reception accorded to them, and repeatedly bowed in acknowledgment. Her Majesty, who was looking vr,mderfully well, was accompanied by a field officer's escort of the Royal Hose Guards, a con- tingent of the Victorian Horse Artillery, and a detach- ment of Indian cavalry.</p>
        <p>The Queen arrived in State at 12.20. She wore a black. skirt of real lace of priceless value, and a splendid veil ot the same material, and above all was a small coronet of pearls. which were remarkable for size and beauty. The. Queen also wore drop pearls in her ears, the Order ot the Garter, and several other orders were carried across 'the shoulders. Her Majesty took a seat on the left side of the altar, the Princess of %Vales being on the other side. with the King and Queen of Denmark. The bride looked lovely with a lace veil thrown back over her shoulders.</p>
        <p>1 THE MARRIAGE CEREMONY. The Queen remained seated whilst the hymn," Father of light," was sung, but all the rest of the company stood, the bride and bridegroom standing together mean- while. At the altar the Archbishop of Canterbury pro- ceeded in due course with the marriage service in the presence of a truly brilliant assemblage. The responses and declarations of the bridegroom were distinctly heard but those of the bride were more faint and scarcely audible except by those immediately around her, After I the putting on of the marriage ring the usual prayers followed. As the Archbishop was pronouncing the Bene- diction cannons were heard announcing to tly: outside. world the completion of the marriage ceremony.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>INSIDE THE CHAPEL.</head>
        <p>INSIDE THE CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>Representatives of the press were the first, admitted to theay ,s CllP which, although the centre of the d interest, Yarelstto,wranch less adorned than most other points. The table suhatituted for the altar was loaded with the valuable gold sacramental plate belonging to the chapel, and on tl:e eastern wail there was a tasteful but no too lavish use of floral decorations. The only other additional feature of the embellishment was a an antique character. consisting of two squares of finely preserved old Mortlake tapestry from Hampton Court, and dating from the time id Charles I. As all the guest S, , admitted to the-chapel had their seats reserved, they had ,no occasion to arrive early. Tint Bareness Burdett- Coutts wore a hugh tiara of diamonds. The Marquis of Ripon passed to his seat in the chapel with the Rajah of ilikOWangar, who was dressed in the rlehest costume. Countess Spencer, wearing cream and a hugh tiara, fol- lowed, and then came the Portuguese Minister, Lord and Lady Kimberley, the Russian Ambassador and Madame de Staid, and Lord Herschel!. The Prince And Princess Edward of Saxe-Weimar were also conspicuons amongst those present, together with the Duke and Duchess of Devonshire on the ground Boor. Lord and Lady George Hamilton were among the tirst arrivals. The, former wore the uniform of the Trinity House his wife wore a costume of sage green. Then came Mr. and Mrs. Brsce, Lord and Lady Cross, Lord and Lady Salisbury, Mr. John Morley, and ,other members of the present or former Cabinet, all resplemient in levee or court dress. Still mare magnificent in their Oriental attire, sparkling with jewels, wers the Indian Princesses, who, with the Rhanee of Gondol, occupied seats in front of one of the Royal galleries. Next canoe some leading members of the English aristocracy,including the stately Countess of BreadaP.onte,faowed so or by the venerable Duke oh Rutland and the Duchess «if Rutland, Lord and Lady Salistiury,the Duke of Argyle in Highland costume, with kilts, and the Speaker, in Court dress. All the ladies were gorgeous with their richest jewels. At five minutes past twelve Mr. sod Mrs. Gladstone arrived, and a percept ible murmur passed through the building. Mrs. (iiadstone, dressed in black silk, with is great deal of deep and flowing lace about the bodice and long lace drapery pendant from the hair, walked quickly to her seat, Mr. Glade.. me walking with less briskness behind with the aid of a stick. He, too, was dressed as he always is on State occasions in the uniform of an elder brother of Trinity House. There were therefore three of them much coveted uniforms to be seen—those of Mr. Gladstone, Lord Rosehery, and Lord George Hamilton. This red cuffs and heavy gold epaulettes were easily recognisable. The Princess of who was amcng the earliest of the Royal arrivals, %yes vociferously welcomed, as were also the other Royal personages. The Marchioness of Lorne were white, and the Marquis was in Highland costume. Princess Louise of Battenberg was dressed in cream satin, aud Princess Christian in purple, while the Duchess.of Connaught wore light green. The Princess at Wales was gowned in soft cream satin, and the Duchess of Mecklenburg in purple satin, aed it was noticed that the Prince of Wales, the Duke of Edinburgh of ' York, who ales wore a Navy and the Ciarewitch wore naval uniform, out of compli- )rder of the Garter. mcoitenutmteo, twheithDtihikeec</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>ARCHBISHOP'S ADDRESS.</head>
        <p>THE ARCHBISHOP'S ADDRESS.</p>
        <p>The Archbishop of Canterbury conducted the cere- mony with charming grace, adding at the end a short address upon the duties of the exalted pair to the nation. He enjoined upon them to cultivate moderation, discretion, and wisdom, coupled with enthusiasm, to all worthy ends. After a closing prayer, the organ pealed forth the lVedding Mardi, and the bride and bride- groom left the chapel for Buckingham Palace, being greeted with the same enthusiasm on returning as they had received while on their way to the Chapel Royal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>RETURN OF THE WIDDING PARTY.</head>
        <p>RETURN OF THE WIDDING PARTY.</p>
        <p>The bride and bridegroom after the benediction re- c‘Aved embraces from all the Royal family, the Queen leading by sahr.ing trith twst affectionately. It was after one o'clock when the marriage service was com- pleted, and the process:on was reformed for the return journey to Buckingham Palace. As the Queen's car- riage drove out of the courtyard the troops presented arms, and the Guards' band struck tip the National Anthem. The crowd, who had been waiting most patiently in the scorchin4 rays of the sun for nearly an hour, once again gave vent to their feelings of loyalty in prolonged aid ringing ( heers. As the newly-wedded pair emerged into the Mall dui/ were received by the immense crowd with a perfect storm of cheers, and the ovation coatinued as they progressed up St. James's-st., with tie escort furnished by the 2nd Life Guards. The scene was most imposing, undone that will never be forgotton by those who witnessed it. The Queen drove back to Buckingham Palace after the ceremony, accompanied by. the Duchess of Tecit. The Duchess of York looked very pale. Subsequently the Queen, the Duke and Duchess of York, and the Duke of 'leek appeared on the balcony of the Palace, and were loudly cheered. The Queen was seated on the balcony several minutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>71IE GOING AWAY.</head>
        <p>71IE GOING AWAY.</p>
        <p>Precisely at a quarter to five the Queen came in her chair to the Royal entrance at Buckingham Palace, in the inner quadrangle, whore all the Royal guests were assembled, to witness the departure of the bride and bridegroom. The Duke of Connaught accompanied the ; Duchess of Teck, who W 54 visibly affected while bidding I • adieu to her daughter. The Duke of Teck WAS one of ! the last to say " Good-be " to the Princess, who mean- while had affectionately kissed all the lady members of the Royal family present. As she entered her carriage a shower of rice and satin slippers rained over her. As Prince George entered the carriage, the bead of the Scots Guards played " Auld Lang Syne." The Prince of Wales, the Duke of Edinburgh, and the other male members of tne Royal family present escorted the bride and bridegroom to the outer gate, where there was a final handshaking and a shower of rice thrown by the Duke of Connaught. Meanwhile her Majesty appeared at one of the windows overlooking the outer quadrangle and in view ot the vast crowd outside waved an adieu. Accotnpanying the Q•leen were the Duke and Duchess of Teck. 'l' he Prince of Wales and the other •Princes stood at the outer gate until the carriage containing the Duke ad Duchess of York disappeared in the distance. Sub- sequently, the Prince, accompanied by the Princess, returned to Marlborough House, where later in the evening they gave a State dinner, at which the foreign Royal guests and members of the Royal family were present, except the Queen. Her Majesty returned to ; 'Windsor on Friday morning. Hundreds of thousands of the populace were pr.*a- Mg westward in the early foreueon, and covered the entire line of route between Buckingham Palace and Liverpool-st. Station, which was to be tra- versed by the Royal couple on their departure. : Pall Mall was one long vista of crimsom gold. The front of Liverpool-street Station was one blaze of colour, and as the Royal pair Came in sight the specta- tors lining the pathway or occupying a favoured posi- tion, thousands in number, cheered with 1i the ardour of their loyalty. Several roses were tossed into the carriage, which proceeded at unite to the station ritrainte. The train had been timed to leave at 5.30. MA it west a little later than that when the Duke and Duchess arrived. From the doorway to the platform Was a grove formed almost exclusively of beautifal palms, and en passing through this the Royal couple were met by Mr. C. 11. Parker, chairman ,if the Great Eastern Railway. the Board of Directors, Lord George Hamilt an, (deputy chairman), Mr. W. Dirt, the general manager, and Mr. J. 11. N-ttleslilp, superintend- ent of the line, all of selio n, with the excep- tion of Mr. Parker, travelled by the Royal train. The Essex Artillery Volunteers formed a guard of honour. Just tY.fore entering the special Saloon carriage, Miss Constanee Dirt, the little daughter of the ma pager of the line, handed a bunch of roses to the Duchess, who re- turned thanks with a kiss. The saloon carriage WitA charmingly upholstered in blue and gold. Upon the table was a basket of white and blush roses,plaeed there by the Duke as a present to his wife. Whan the signal Was given, the Duchess stood at the window, and tear; filled her eyes as the train slowly left the station amid a roar of cheers. THE JOURNEY To SANDRINGHAM.</p>
        <p>At Cambridge, according to arrangement, the Mayor and Corporation, with other inhabitants, met the special train. The Mayor presented an illuminated ad4ress offering congratulations on t he marriage which had been celebrated, to the great joy of all her Majesty's loyal sub- jeeti. and recalling the associations of the Prince Con- sort and the Prince of Wales with the University. The Duke and Duchess of York were due at Wolferton for Sandringhatn at eight o'clock, and the special train arrived punctually. Many thousands of people assemh- 1. led ond gave the la ido aria bridegroom a very enthusi- : astic greeting. A detachment of Norfolk Yeomanry formed an escort, ‘tal their Royal Ilighnesees were formally received by the Chief Constable and Mr. Beek, the Prince of Wales's 1 Sandriri tam agent. r 5 • ..DF.SCRIPTION OF THE WEDDINei DItEs3S. Princess May's wedding gown is says the " Daily News," exceedingly beautiful, and at the same time has that cachet of simple elegance which distinguishes the toilets of all nor young Princesses. [he material uscd is, says the Press Association, a rick silver and white broeade_ woven at Spitallields—of, exquisite texture and workmanship. The design thrown up in silver from the soft background, is a singularly happy group- ing of the shamrock, rose, and thistle, with Silver rib- bons entwined bstween each cluster of the national emblems. The train, without being, full, is long. Prin- COPS May has not patronised any of the grac-fully draped or the folded ceraages so much in favour at the present time, but has' remained faithful to the plain Court boilice—short on the hips and slightly pointed back and front. It is (pile untrionwrl except by a narr-w berths of finest Minium point lase, he tded by is trail of orange blossom, gathered into a cluster at the bust, and with a sprig of white hcather introduced. The very short sleeves are . com- posed of a frill of chiffon under one of lace. The front of the bridal gown is of beautiful white satin, arranged with three festooned ft Ainceg of lioniton point, matching that on the corsage, hut much wider. The upper flounce is outlined by a.trail of orange blossoms caught in the centre with heather, and falling in two ends to the foot of the skirt. The side attains marking the division between the silver brocade and the white satin petticoat are overlaid with trails of orange blos- soms terminating in a cluster, in which the white heather again appears. Tke foot'of the petticoat is finished by soft frills of chiffon, and the tram is edged inside by a thick white satin ruche. Princes May's bridal veil is of fine old noniton point, and WNS 11,Sed by the Duchess of 'reek at her own wedding. -It was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <p>thrown back off the face, as is the custom with all our Royal brides. The Pincess carried a shower bouquet composed of rare white flowers—the old white Pro eece. t ose House of York predominating choice white orchids, the lovely new white carnation appropriately christened "The Bride," lily of the valley, myrtle, orange blossoms, delicate fern and other foliage com- plete the requisite bouquet, which was tied with wlite corded ribbons. The ten bridesmaids were :—The Princesses Victoria Maud a Wales ; Princesses Victoria, Alexan'lra, and trice of Edinburgh ; Princesses Margaret and Victoria ric'a of Connaught, Princess Vitt iris of Schles ig- sum, Priue, SS Victoria Eugenie of Battenberg, and ..,ce:s Alice of Battenberg, wore white satin gowns. ' trimmed with silver lace made in Buckinghamshire. The elder bridesmaids had square-cut low bodices, edged with the silver lace, which encircles the waist, and is continued down th, side as a girdle in two rows rinished by a silver tassel fringe. The skirts s.re vandyknd at the foot, and bordered with the silver trimming.. A soft background for the silver tipped points is formed by frills of white chiffon arranged on the hem. The sleeves of chiffon are in a puff to the elbow. A touch of colour- ;Mg is given to these gowns by a cluster !of moss rosebuds tied on the left side of 1 the bodice basque by loops and ends of moss..- rose pink velvet ribbons. The elder princesses wore !Clusters of roses to match in their hair. The frocks of the younger Princesses were made with baby bodices of I chiffon and the silver trimming comes in straight bands at the skirt foot. The bridesmaids carried bouquets of white roses, orchids, and lily of the valley mixed with the pretty Cattleya Mendelli. The bouquets held by the Priocess of Wales and the other Royal Princesses were of white roses, carnations, and orchids. The bride's going-away costume was of rich cream Irish poplin. hand-embroidered with pure gold thread. The embroidery trims the skirt in festocrns mingled with a narrow cream silk cord and tiny single blossoms wrought in finely cut gold beads. The bodice seams are cutliued with the gold work, showing the material between, flecked over with the little sibgle blossoms. The yoke is embroidered in three rows of festoons match- ing the ornamentation of the skirt. The scolloped bodice basque is -dged with gold trimming, and the cuffs of the moderately high sleeves are similarly treated. An exceedingly smart cape to match is cut round the edge . in scollops, which, with the seams, are worked over with the same beautiful embroidery as shown on the gown. The cape is held to the figure at the waist by a cre iin satin ribbon, and is completed by a deep frill of rich Irish lace. With this was worn a close-fitting little gold boncet, a cluster of cream plumes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>CELEBRATIONS AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>CELEBRATIONS AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>MUSIC AND FIREWORKS.</p>
        <p>Though the attempt made to publicly organize celebrations in commemoration of the &amp;psi we('ding failed, a private attempt, initiated by the Mayoress, succeeded. Mrs. Greeuall, who has been assisted by the Mayor and several other gentlemen is.su'N.l a circular last week 'expressing the hope that " Warrington would not be behind ether towns in expressing its loyalty on the occasion of the Royal wedding." By -Wednesday night more than .£lOO had been received in response to this appeal, and accordingly Mr. Prince was commissioned to arrange the extensive display of fireworks which was witnessed on the Grammar School Cricket ground in Arpley on Tiureday night. In the town man" shopkeepers and householders energetically set about decorat- ing the exterior of their houses, and places of business, and consequently, on Thursday, the streets were all enlivened witn flags and banners. In Sanksy- st., Ilridge-st., Horsemarket-st., and Buttermarket-st., there was quite a profusion of flags, whilst even more insignifieant street 4 like Dixon-st., or Legn-atreet, came up smiling as it were,with afew inall harmers protruding from bedroom windows. Even in the less fashionable pre- cincts of Town Hill and Fennell-st.,loyalty was to be seen 6tantting nobly forth from bedroom windows, in the shape of, perhaps. a line of variously coloured stockings. The Town Hall was decorated with many flags and bannerettes suspended in long lines from the roof, and also from two tall tapering Venetian masts, placed at each side the entrance steps. There were also flags fly- front the Liberal and Conservative Clubs. at Bank Quay the tall mast on the bowling green had been pro- fusely hung with bunting. During the whole of the • day merry peals were rung on the bells at the Parish Church and also at St. Paul's Church.</p>
        <p>Ilusiness was conducted in the town pretty much as usual, none of the local Works being closed. The only phiee where the day was recognised as a holiday was the workhouse.</p>
        <p>At 12 o'clock at noon, dinner, similar to that given at Chistinas, was partaken of by 259 male and female inmates, and 50 children. The dinner was the old- , fashioned English one, " Boast beef and ' plum pudding !" to which the adults were allowed a pint of beer or of any ether suitable beverage. After dinner tobacco and snuff were distri- buted. But before the dinner was over the inmates were visited by several members of the Board of Guar- dians including Messrs. Ratcliffe, Davies, A. J. Jolley,A. J•illey,W.lloughton, E.A ukland, Smith, Heaton, Turton, I Fairclough, Swinton, Pilling Hill, Shaw, Mr. S. Sutton (clerk), Mr. C. fl. Ward and Mrs. Ward (master and matron). After expressieg the hope that the Inmates would enjoy themselves, the guardians adjourned to the I Boardroom and partook of dinner. A special tea WAS ; given to the inmates in the evening, after which there was a concert, the following being the vornlkts :—Mr. Ward, Mrs. and Miss Ward, Mr. George Parr, and Mr. Meakin (of Liverpool). Mr. Muir acted as accompa- nist. In the evening the Warrington Borough Band, under the conductorship of Mr. Rimmer, of Southport, played for dancing in the Park, and afterwards marched down to the Grammar School Cricket Field, at Arpley, where the fireworks display was given. There was an immense crowd gathered together at Arpley, estimated at about 10,000 persons. Amongst those present were the Mayor and Mayoress. The fireworks were arranged in a most successful manner and gave general satisfaction. , Amongst the many pyrotechnic designs there was a large portrait of the Duke of York and Prin- cess May, and another with the words " Long Life and Happiness," which was art off to the accompaniment of " God Bless the l'rince of Wales," played by the Borough Band. The fire- works it may be mentioned, were supplied through Mr. Prince, of Horsetuarket-street. The musical ar- rangemeots of the evening were made by Mr. J. Taylor, secretary to the Warrington Association of Bands. At the conclusion of the display the thanks of those as- ' ponied -a ere enthusiastically accorded to the Mayoress on film motion of Mr. John Crosfleld. In the town itself there were very few illuminations. The Lieu Hotel dis- played a jet device of a British Lion, and on the bal- cony of Mr. Pearson's house, in Bold-street there were a large number of different hued glow lamps. For about an hour during the evening, the Well's lime lights were burned on the roof of the Town Hall..</p>
        <p>GRAPPENFIALL CELEBRATIONS. Grappenhall was ea fete on Thursday afternoon, on the occasion of the Royal Wedding. The afternoon was observed as a general holiday, and the two tanneries of Mr. W. Long, J.P., and Mr. A. Dakin, ceased work at dinner time. Flags were floating on the church tower, and from many of the gentlemen's houses. The inhabi-- ants of the village, blth old and young. ass,:inbled at the large room] at Grappenhall.and walked in procAssion to the park of Mr.J. Charlton Parr, J.P ,C.C.,kiudly 14nt ' for the occasion. headed by the band of the Warrington Volunteers, under the conductorship of Mr. Armstrong. fire, Sports and tea, provided by public subscription, were indulged in. Amongst those present were :—Mr. W. Long, J.P., Mr. A. Dakin, Rev R. Greenall, Mr. A. Greenall, Mr. Lister Kave, and Colonel Kennedy. At ! the sports, which took the form of old English sports, ps-izes were competed for. At fire o'clock an excellent tea was provided be Mr. Speakman, of the Saracen's Hotel, Wilderspool, and the good . things provided were quickly disposed of. Dancing was afterwards indulged in to the strains of the Volunteer Band, aml wss kept up' until dusk, when some journeyed to Warrington to see the fire% orks. During the day the bells at the church an merry peals.</p>
        <p>WKLY IiEPORT Or THE HEAT:En OF WARIIINCTON.</p>
        <p>During the week, which was the 26;h of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered :—Births : Ivarrington, 28 ; Latchford, 3; total, 31. Deaths : NVerrington, 43 ; Latchford, 2, total 45. The excess of deaths over births was 14. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 42.4 per 1.000, against 24 the average of the previous four weeks, and agniu.st 21.1 the corresponding week of last year. The death-rate from the seven chief ay moth; diseases was equal to 20.0 per I,ooo,agamst B.9.the average rate of the four previous weeks, and against 2.8 the corresponding week of 1899. The /1(.4 of mortality were :--Warrington :oto I, 21; 1 to 5,13; sto 15.0; 15 t 095,1 ; 25 to 60,6-1; 60 up 7-3 total, , 45. The deaths, included-15 from ditirrtoia ; 7 from measles ; 2 from tubercular disease; 2 frotn.old age; 2 from heart disease; 3 from brain • disease; 2 from lung disease : 1 I from drowning. The following cages of infectious disease have been reported dur- ing the vveck :—Smallpox, nil; scarlet fever, 6; diphtheria, 0; typhus lever, 0 ; enteric fever, 0; relapsing fever, 0; puerperal fever; 0. Idetereelogl. The wean temperature was 39'7 Fah. and therefore a little lower than the I preceding week. The maximum temperature 75F., was ' recorded on the Ist inst., and the minimum temperature 45F., on the. 30; h it. The rainfall measured 174 in. The general direction was S.E., the reading of the Anemometer denoting 1.335 miles.-4Oltx H. Coma.u., Medical Officer of . , Ilea It h.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
