      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>UNIVERSAL BOON.</head>
        <p>A UNIVERSAL BOON.</p>
        <p>It is seldom that any discovery of science is such as will benefit al; classes of people. Same of the greatest discoveries of the age, while proving beneficial to thousands, have also at, least for a time, injured other thousands. liv. n some of - best zwelicinal preparations, however, much they may do,. remove one class of disease or suit some constitutions, at same time either deepen the tydd of another disease, or I VeS it entirely untouched, or, if they tenefit one Class of pati they may injure another. Now,</p>
        <p>THE GREAT NERD OF THE AGE. ia acme scientifically compounded preparation which will effeetuidly cope with the prevalent diseases of this country, which will he certain to do good when fairly tried, which will be equally adapted to the needs of the merchant, the profes- sional man, end he who wins his bread by the sweat of his brow, the clerk. tha factory hand, the miner, the workers in metals. and the roadside labourers. It should also he such a preparation as contains no injurious ingredients, and which may be takeu with impunity by the weak and sickly child or the delicate female, as well as by the stronger constituted man. Such preparation would indeed he</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>BOON TO HUMANITY.</head>
        <p>A BOON TO HUMANITY.</p>
        <p>After long-continued and diligent research, this much- needed boon has been found, and repeatedly tested by analysts, who declare it to be pure and harmless. This desirable prenauttinn is that wellknown Vegetable Tonic called Gwily m EVARI. Quinine Bitters, and it has gained such a widespread reputation for efficacy that many unprejudiced doctors, who have given it a fel: trial, frequently recommend it to their patients in cases of great Weakness, Nervousness, or any disorder of the digestive organs. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is so invariably efficacious and produces snch satisfactory results, that when once tried it is always recommended. It has proved successful when all other medicines had failed to give relief. It has also in many case; been found to be permanently beneficial when other preparations at best only give temporary relief. Gwilyrn Brans' Bitters is purely vegetable, and contains neither</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>MERCURY NOR IRON.</head>
        <p>MERCURY NOR IRON.</p>
        <p>nor any poisonous or other injurious substances, and thou- sands who lisve benefitted by its use agree in saying It is a specific for all symptoms of Indigestion, Nervousness. and Weakness. It is also confidently recommended for Low Spirits Liver Disorders, and Chest Affections. The unporalleled success of Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters has induced unprincipled persons to offer base and spurious Imitations. somewhat similar in name and appearance. but possessing none of the virtues of this great national remedy. Rememner that none are genuine unless the name Gwilpm Evans is on label, stamp, and bottle. Sold by all Chemists, in bottles, at Is. 14c1., 2s. 93. and O. 63. each, or may be obtained, carriage free, direct from the Proprietors :—Quinine Bitters Manufactaring Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED TIIEFTS FROM HAYDOCIC COLLIERIES.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Occasional Court, on Monday, before /Lijor Pickmere, John Ashall, of St. Helens, was charged with stealing two picks, value 23. Bd. the pro- perty of John Brown, employed at Haydock Collieries, and a coal spade value 2s. 6d. the property of Messrs. Richard Evans and Co.—John Brown, collier, Haydock, identified the picks as his property by the marks "J. 0. B." punched in them, and James Coombs, 9, Haydock- lane, Haydock, who had employed the prisoner for a short time, said since he dismissed the prisoner he had missed six spades. The spade produced was one of theta—lsaac Heaton, broker, 12, College-st., St. Helens, said he bought the spade and picks from the prisoner. He gave him a snilling for the two picks and a shilling for the spade.. His suspicions were aroused previous to buying the spade, mad he had informed the police as the prisoner had been before, and he had bought about fifteen other picks from him. He gave the name of John Harrison, 118 Lyon-st., St. Helens, and said he was sell- ing his tools because they were not coaling but on day wage.—P.C. Addison stationed at Messrs. Richard Evans' Collieries, Haydock, having given evidence, the prisoner was asked if he had anything to say. He replied that he took the picks to work with, and had them in the house three days before he sold them. He thought of taking them back but was afraid to do so. He was committed to the Sessions for trial. DISGRACEFUL CONDUCT TO A SISTFR.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Court, on Monday, Daniel O'Loughlin, was charged with committing an aggravated assault upon his sister Bridget O'Loughlin residing at 20 Portland-st., at 6 30 on Sunday morning.—The Chief Constable said that Bridget O'Loughlin was 21 years of ago and prisoner 26 years. On Sunday the 25th inst., prisoner assaulted his sister indecently, in the front kitchen. On her screaming, he decamped. On Satur- day night last, the father, mother and sister of com- plainant had gone to bed, and complainant waited up for prisoner to get his supper ready fmr him. When he came home, he again attempted to assault his sister in the same way, but she screamed and the rest of the family came downstairs and he desisted. Bridget then went to skep with her mother and sister and prisoner with his father. At about 6.30 next morning, Bridget was awakened by the bed clothes being pulled down, and prisoner indecently handling her. She screamed out and prisoner ran out of the room. Bridget then told her father and when he mentioned the matter to pri- soner, prisoner struck him. P.S. Taylor and P.C. Lewis were then sent for and they took prisoner to the police 3tation.—Bridget and Margaret O'Loughlin corroborated the Chief Constable's statement, the latter also saying that priioner attempted to treat her in the same manner twelve months ago.—The magistrates considered the case to be a very gross and scandalous one, and sentenced prisoner to one. month's bard labour. SHOCKING CRUELTY 70 A n'ORSE.</p>
        <p>Before the Warrington magistrates, on Monday, Wen. Phaeton, broker, Howard-st., was charged with cruelly ill-treating a horse on the,22nd ult., by working it while in an unfit state, and also with being drunk while in charge of the horse.—lnspector Dowtv, R.S.P.C.A., said that from information received he examined the animal the day after the alleged offence. Both legs were com- pletely skinned from the hocks to the fetlocks. On the back there were wounds 14 inches long, and there were marks on the head. The horse was in an unfit state for work, and could scarcely stand in the stable. Defend- ant told him that the horse WAS a kicker, and that it received the injuries by kicking the cart. Defendant had, however, a rope tied to thc shifts behind the horse, and it was impossible for the horse to kick.—John Smith said that he saw defendant coming along Liver- pool-road on the 22nd inst., with the horse and cart. He was drunk and unfit to take charge of the horse, and was jerki9g the reins. He stopped near the end of Howard's-lane, aad witness examined the horse. It was bleeding terribly from wounds on the legs. On re- starting., the horse ran against a gas lamp and fell over completely exhausted, and lay for some time apparently dead. After some considerable trouble the horse was again put into the shafts and defendant went on. Wit- ness then gave information to P.C. Turner who went with witness to the house of defendant where Turner examined the horse, and then reported him.—William Lazenby and P.C. Turner supported the last witness's statement.,—Richard Hinds, Mrs. Bennett and Mr. Shawcross, were each called by prisoner to prove that no cruelty had taken place, but the magistrates considered the case amply proved against defendant and fined him 19s, and costs in each case or in default 14 days im- prisonment, the total amount of the fine and costs being £29s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>GOING TO SELL THE PIGS.</head>
        <p>GOING TO SELL THE PIGS.</p>
        <p>At the Occasional Court, Mr. John White, Peter Iforridge, Padgate, was charged on a warrant with assaulting his wife. It appeared that there had been a quarrel between the parties, and de- fendant threatened on Friday night to sell the pigs, and pull dAwn the sign over the door. Ile struck complain- ant and her daughter, and on Saturday ho threatened to knock out her brains with a chair.—Defendant was bound owr to keep the peace for six months, and or- siered to ray the costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>MOICDAY. —Before Alderman Harrison (chairman), and Messrs. Wm. Pierpoint and S. Wright. DRUNEL AND DISORDERLY.—CatheIiIIO Brown, no address, one month bard labour; Patrick Burke Rose- 'yard, 10s. and costs or 14 da.-a ; for being drunk, Thos.-1 Roberts, Friar's Green, 55. an.. ,osts, or seven days. ICSUNNRY ON FIRE.—For this offence Patrick Mockin,, 13, New-road, was tined is. ASSAULT ON A TICkET COLLECTOR.—John Duggan, Friar's Green, was claarged with being drunk and dis- orderly on Saturday night, and assaulting ticket collec- tor Holland, at Bank Quay Station. Holland stated that prisoner wished to go on to the station to go to Crewe, but witness declined to allow him as he was drunk. The booking clerk had previously refused to give him a ticket, but one of his companions had got one for him. When witness stopped him, prisoner becaene violent and gave him two blows on the chest. Detective Ede and P.S. Rowbottom then took him into custody, and his conduct was so had that the snaps had to be put on him. The magistrates sent prisoner to gaol for 14 days. A. SLEEPY COACTIMAN.—Robert once, was charged with frequenting the enclosed premises of the Patten Artns at 3.30 on Monday morning. P.C. Bell proved the case. Prisoner said said that he had been working at the Patten Arms during the past week, but had been discharged. On Saturday afternoon he went into the yard at five o'clock and fell asleep is one of the coaches and did not awake until midnight, when it was too late to go to his lodgings. Prisoner was ordered to pay the lock up fees is. ASSAULT ON A MOTHER.—Wm. Curley, 4, Jolley's-yard was summoned for assaulting his mother, Bridget Cur- on the 23rd ult. Complainant did not press the case, and prisoner was ordered to pay costs or seven days. IT WAS NOT A CAR—Patrick Clarke, 12, Berry-st., was charged with being drunk while in charge of a cab on the 23rd June. Defendant pleaded not guilty and said that he did not drive a cab, he drove a hansom. P.C. Barlow proved the case, and defendant was fined n the costs. TUESDAY.—Before Mr. R. Garnett (chairman), and Dr.</p>
        <p>Starkey Smith. sesturr OW A WlFF.—Charlos Daniels was r•h-trged with assaulting hi a wife on tiunday. Mrs. Daniels :aid</p>
        <p>that her husband had thrashed her on Thursday and Friday, and on Sunday he again beat her and wade her arms black and blue. He was not sober at the time. Prisoner was bound over to keep the peace for six months in one surety of .£5 and pay the DOA&amp; DETERMINED TO CtET AWAY.—Catherine Irlamscliffe was charged with being drunk and disorderly in River road on the previous night. P.C. Hollingsworth said that prisoner was very disorderly in company with a man in River-road on the previous night. When the man saw witness, he sprang int., the river fully dressed and swam across. Witness brought the pi liner to the police station. Prisoner was fined ss. and costs. ASSAULT ON A FATIIER.—JOhD Conway was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Dial Court on the previous evening and with assaulting his father Frank Conway. Prisoner had been drinking, and on coming home ho struck his father who was obliged to.call in the police, and prisoner was locked up by P.C. Delahay. Prisoner was fined ss. and costs or seven days for being drunk and disorderly, and was committed to 14 days' hard labour VII* assaulting his father. WEDNESDAY.—Beforo Councillor Fleming (in the chair), and Councillor James Faircleugli. . . .</p>
        <p>DRUNX AND DISORDEULY.—WiIIiam Allen, hawker, was fined 51. and costs for being drunk in Hall-street.—Mary Jane Butterworth, Northwich, for being drunk and dis- orderly in Bridge-street, was dismissed on condition she left the town.—Charles Jones, a married man and Edith Jane Gleave, a single woman were both charged with being drunk in a closet in Refuge-square. P.C. Turner said the woman's conduct was disgraceful. They were each fined ss. and COAL ASSAULTING HIS Morß.—Thomas Burke, living with his mother in Wainwright's-yard, off Buttermarket- street, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Buttermarket-street, and also with assaulting his mother Mary Burke, at the same time. Sergeant Smith said at 11.20 he received a complaint from Mrs. Burke that she was in dread of her life. He went to the house and Burke came out of the house, and used very •bad language and attempted to again strike his mother. Mrs. Burke who appeared with a black eye, said her son had had some drink, and come home and hit her in the face, giving her a black eye. " She was in fear of her life." Re had previously given her a black eye. Pris- oner was sent to gaol for one month's imprisonment with bard labour. . _</p>
        <p>A SUSPICIOUS CITAUACTER.—JoIin Smith, an old I offender, was charged with being a convicted thief, and with being on the premises of Charles Stewart, marine store dealer, with intent to commit a felony. P.C. Turner said at 3.30 that morning he went to examine the premises of Mr. C. Stewart's, and found the prisoner in one of the sheds. He was trying to cover his face with bags. He was sent to prison for 14 days' hard! labour. ASSAULT ON A WOMAN.—Thomas Benson, sergeant in , the Liverpool Militia, stationed at Orford Barracks, was charged with unlawfully assaulting Ellen Metham, wife of Mr. John Metham, timekeeper on the Ship Canal, , residing at 13, Rodney-street. Prisoner pleaded not ' guilty. From the evidence of the Chief Constable it appears the prisoner entered the complainant's house, ' and after a conversation with her, he commenced "sky- larking." He afterwards dragged her on the floor in the, kitchen, and also on the stairs. Her screams were heard by a young girl named Sarah Ann Stone who came in the house, and the prisoner hastened out by the back door. Information was conveyed to the police and he was arrested at the Barracks by Inspector Spinks, and charged with the offence to which he made no reply. After evidence by Ellen Metham, Sarah Ann Stone, and James Gleeson, and Inspector Spinks, prisoner was fined £1 and costs or one month's imprisonment.</p>
        <p>THIMISDAY.—Before Alderman Sutton (chairman), Cap- tain Ridgway and Councillor James Fairclough.</p>
        <p>AN OLD WA.III2ANT.—Mary Brown was charged on a warrant with assaulting Annie Cunningham on April 30, 1892. Complainant said that her sister lodged at Messrs Brown's house at the time of the dispute, and she was on a visit to her sister when Mrs. Brown struck her down with a teapot. A witness was called by prisoner to prove that complainant was drunk at the time. Asked why she did not appear to her summons, prisoner said that she did not like to come to court as she had never been there before. The magistrates fined prisoner 10s. and costs or 14 days' imprisonment. . . .</p>
        <p>_ DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—For being drunk and dis- orderly, on Wednesday night, in Buttermarket-st., John Moran was dismissed on promising not to repeat the offence. _ .</p>
        <p>VAGABOND.—Ann Moran was charged with being a rogue and a vagabond, sleeping in a closet in Turner- st., early that morning having no visible means of sub- sistence. P.C. Wood proved the case and prisoner was ordered to leave the town.</p>
        <p>THE CURLEY'S AGAlN.—Martin Curley was charged with being a highly disorderly person found wandering abroad in Arpley-road, at 2.30 that morning and having no visible means of subsistence.—P.C. Bell said that he found prisoner wandering in front of Arpley Station. He appeared to have been in the " wars." Being a person who scarcely ever worked he brought him to the police station.—The Chief Constable said that scarcely a week passed without having a man of the name of Curley in the court. On Tuesday prisoner's brother had been before the magistrates for assaulti,', his brother. The cnagistrates sent prisoner for a monts imprison- ment with hard labour. FRlDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (chairman), Dr. Gornall, Mr. William Pierpoint, and Councillor Roberts.</p>
        <p>PCMER.—John Barnes, 2, Talbot-street, was charged with assaulting and beating his wife Annie Barnes on the 4th inst. The wife stated that her husband came home drunk on the above date. Ete.ran at her and knocked her over, and afterwards broke a chair on her back. A witness called by defendant said that the wife was srriking at her husband with a poker.—P.S. Chant- ler said that the parties led a very unhappy life, and one was as bad as the other.—Defendant was bound over to keep the peace for six months and ordered to pay the costs.</p>
        <p>ASSAULT WITH A BllCKET.—Charles Stewart and Mary Stewart, 3, Buckley's Court, were each charged with assaulting and beating Mary Jane Owens, 7, Buckley- street on July Ist. Complainant said that her children had " fallen out " with Stewart's children about some blacking brushes, when Mrs. Stewart came into the house and gave her a blow on the head with a wooden bucket. Mr. Steward also kicked her. A large number of witr.esses were called by both complainant and defen- dants and it was shown that there was a fight between Mrs. Stewart and Mrs. Owen.—The magistrates dis- missed each case, ordering each party to pay their own costs. _ _ _</p>
        <p>QUARREL AT Towns Exp.—Thomas Taylor, 17,Turner- street, was charged with assaulting Thomas Mannion, 2, Pinshop-yard, on July Ist. Mr. Longland appeared for the defence. Complainant stated that he awed some money to defendant, and on Saturday night, defendant's wife asked him for the money. He could not pay it and told her so. Upon this defendant jumped out of the house and struck him, knocking him on the floor.—Mr. Longland brought witnesses to prove that complainant was drunk, and had struck defendant's wife before defendant struck him back.—The magistrates dismissed the case, each side to pay costs. UNPROVOKED ASSAM:V.—Jane Taylor, 1, Rodney-st., was charged with assaulting Margaret Fogg, 3, Rodney- st., on the 3rd inst. Mrs. Fogg said that she was talk- ing to a neighbour when defendant came across and gave her a blow in the face. She also called her foul</p>
        <p>names' and was always remindinng`her of being a cou- sin of Helen Heesom, and called her a murderess. P.C. Hollingsworth said that he was called to defendant twice Secretaries of Cricket Clubs are requested to note thief on the day in question, and defendant expressed her reports of matches played on SaturdAy must be sent to the satisfaction of giving complainant a good " hammer- I head office. Beeesey-street. Warrington, not later than ten leg." Defendant was bound over to keep the peace for o'clock on Wednesday morning. six months and to pay the costs. STONE THEOW INO.—Samuel Clarey, Edwin Howard, and John Holloway, all of Appleton-st., were charged with doing damage to glass windows of the Longford Wire, Iron and Steel Works,to the extent of 10s. on June 26th. P.S. Taylor said that he was sent to the works by the Chief Constable, in consequence of complaints. He made a house to house visitation in Appleton-st., and found the three defendants, the latter two of whom accused Clarey, and Clarey accused the other two. A clerk at Longford, who would have been a witness was on his holidays, and was, therefore, no evidence against defendants who were accordingly discharged.</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEnsEsDAY..—Before Messrs. G. Comber (chairman),</p>
        <p>and W. Mortimer, and Major Pickmere. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—For this offence Michael Harringtoon, Orford, was fined ss. and costs, or seven days' imprisonment, and William Atherton, Hixton, ss. including costs,and for being drunk in the Joiner's Arms, Croft,on the 28th ult., Joseph Stringer was fined ss.and costs.</p>
        <p>FURIOUS DRIVING.—James Unsworth, of Leigh, and Thomas Mather were each charged with furiously driv- ing a horse and trap in Croft on the nth ult. P.C. Cal- laghan said that he saw the two defendants lashing their horse in a violent manner in Smithy-lane, Croft. The horse was going at a great rate, and when witness stopped it, it was sweating all over. P.S. Barnes and anothcr constable corroborated last witness's statement and the magistrates find Unsworth 10s. and costs, and Mather ss. and costs.</p>
        <p>CRICKETERS. Lawn Tennleers, and Golfers are reminded that GEO. G. BUSSEY Lt Co., manufacture high-elms requisites. Their new catalogue is obtainable from Local Dealers, or the Sorts Manufactory, Peckham Rye, S.E. Trade 4•CC,,,;(,.. Mark.</p>
        <p>Wannniormi V. BROUGHTON. —This match was played at Arpley on Saturday, the teams meeting for the first time during the season. Broughton took their innings first and scored for 8 wickets. Massrs. S. M. Cros- field and Griflin opened Warrington's essay, and runs came rapidly in the outset. Crosfield was in rare form, and after the first few ewers the scoring rate rose at a good percentage. The half century was passed after 30 minutes play, but at 59. Crosfield returned'one to Brad- dock, who had relieved Roberts, his 47 having been made without a chance. Sugg opened well with a couple of boundaries, but after putting together 14 in as men minutes he was taken at slip. At 90 Griffin was caught at the wicket, his 19 being made up by good cricket. With Smith and Clarke associated, the onlookers were treated to some capital hitting,the batsmen treating the several bowlers tried with no respect. Fourteen runs were made by Clarke off one over of Braddock's, whilst a capital drive by Smith landed in the pavilion. On the stroke of seven Smith was bowled, and the close of the day's play saw Warrington's aggregate 145 for four 1 wickets. Score end bowling analysis :</p>
