      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Mr.C.Reynolds and Major frazlehurst. ASSAULT.—A married woman, named Mary Jane Var- ney, was summoned by her sister. Emily Wood. for having assaulted her by striking her in the face and cal- ling her bad names.—Defendant was bound over to keep the peace for three months. WITHOIIT A NAME—James Littler, fanner, Sutton.was summoned for not having his name on his cart as re- quired by law.—Fined ss. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>GREAT STRIKE OF AMERICAN IRONWORKERS.</head>
        <p>GREAT STRIKE OF AMERICAN IRON- WORKERS.</p>
        <p>35,000 MEN THROWN OUT OF WORK.</p>
        <p>NEW YORK, SATURDAY.—The " World" publishes a despatch from Pittsburg stating that all the Union iron and steel mills in the country, with the exception of the sheet and tin plate plants, will ho closed down at mid- night to-day, owing to the failure of the proprietors to agree upon a scale of wages.' The manufacturers wish to make an all round reduction of 10 to 15 per cent. of the wages, but the employees decline to accept it. 35,000 men will be thrown out of employment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>MESSRS. M'CORQUODALE AND THE UNION PRINTERS.</head>
        <p>MESSRS. M'CORQUODALE AND THE UNION PRINTERS.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons, on Monday, Mr. Woods asked the Secretary to the Treasury whether he was aware that between the 21st March and 6th of April, 1993, ten of the most prominent workmen were dis- charged by Col. Wood, the Manager of the firm of Messrs. M*Corquodale and Co., printers, at Newton and elsewhere, who do part of the printing for Her Majesty's Glvernment, because they had joined the typographical trade union, and that a numb4r of others received their notice, which was withdrawn only when the men signed a compulsory agreement to sever their connection with the above society ; whether he was aware that at the other branches of the same firm, in London, Leeds, Birmingham, and Manchester, the men were allowed to join their trade union, and in consequence they worked shorter hours and earned higher wages than at Newton; and whether it was the intention of the Government to take such steps as would give these men fult liberty to join their trade union, or in the alternative would they cease to employ this firm ic the future to do their work. Sir J. Hibbert, who was heard very imperfectly, was understood to say that the Treasury had no authority to interfere with the selection by manufacturers and others of particalar persons whom they might choose to employ. On Thursday evening, Mr. Woods again, in the House of Commons,asked the Secretary to the Treasury whether Her Majesty's Government were aware that printing or other work was being done for the Government by A firm at Newton-le-Willows, Lancashire, which did not allow its emplo%iis to join the trade union belonging to their trade; and if so whether lit could undertake or arrange on behalf of the Government that in the future Government work should not be placed with firms or employers who would not allow their workpeople to belong to the unions of their respective trades.—Sir J. T. Hibbert was unable to add anything to the answer he gave on Tuesday to the effect that the resolution of the 13th February, Mt, did not authorise the Treasury to inquire whether contrac- tors did or did not employ trade union labour.—Mr. Woods : In consequence of the unsatisfactory answer I shall be obliged to call further attention to the question. (Hear, hear.) Mr. J. K. Hardis : Does not the resolu- tion of the6th March of this year cover a ease cf this kind 7—Sir J. T. Ilibbert asked for notice of this ques- tion.</p>
      </div>
