      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <p>k• ' ). •</p>
        <p>STREET ROBBERY AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>A SITSPECTm.D SHARPER.</p>
        <p>At the Town Hell, Newton, on Tuesday morning, before Mr. T. J. Gillespie, a man named John Mc Carthy was brought up on a charge of stealing a purse and 7s. 3d., from a- boy named John Wilson, in Earlestown, on Monday afternoon. The boy had been sent to the pawnshop with some articles of clothing, and pledged them for 7s. Si. When he tame out of the pawnshop, the prisoner was stand- ing °amide. He told the boy he wented him ad -another lad to take some checks for bin ion 'the market, and said he would give him 4.1. He also said if he bad anything in his pockets, he was to take it out, and roll it in apiece of paper, which he produced. The lad took the purse, and a lead pen- cil from his pockets and gave them to the prisoner, who pretended to wrap them in the paper. Me then put the paper under the lad's waistcoat, and , told him to go to the beck door of the pawnshop and wait there until he brolght the checks. The 'lad did so, but after waiting some time, and the ' man not patting in on appearance, he went to !look fora policeman. Meeting P.C. Burns he told I him what had taken place, and together they -searched the various public houses in the town. At the Viaduct hotel, they found the loan, who on seeing the police made of, but he was emartly caught by Burns, and on being searched, the purse, 6s. 11d., and the lead pencil were fount) upon him. He was remanded to Warrington Petty Seseions. At the. Warrington County Sessions on Wednes- day before Major Pickniere,Xotin McCarthy, a res- pectably dressed man, was °hinged with stealing 7s. 6.1. in money, ond a pencil case, the property of John Wilson, on July 3rd, in Newton. John Wilson, a boy aged 12 years, said that his mother had sent him tosehopan Eariestown on the above date. On I coming out of thaskop,he saw prisoner standing in the street close to. Prisoner came to witness and said that if he would take some checks to the market•he would give him fourpence. Prisoner then took witness to the back of-Jones' Buildings and told him to empty his Pockets, so that he could wrap the things in a newspaper, in, order to find room for the checks. Witness took out his purse which cmtained 7. 64.,and'a blacklead pencilend a piece of indiarubber, and put them into the newspaper. Be pre- ' tended to wrap the things in the paper and then put the parcel in witness's bag. Prisoner then. told witness to stop at a back door until he went mend the house to fetch the checks. He then left,,ind witness waited for him almost half. an hour,. after which time witness opened the parcel and only found the indiarubber. Wit- ness then by the advice of a man, informed F.C. Burns, who -went with him to various publie-howies in Hades- town, and on entering-the Viaduct Hotel, they found prisoner. Prisoner otiseeino them immediately bolted out of another door,but P.C.'llurns followed and caught him in Eirle-st.—P.C. Burns confinned the boy's state- ment, and said that when he searched prisoner at the police station, he found the money and laRO the missing pencil case. When charged with the ogence prisoner pleaded guilty. The prisoner was committed to the Liverpool Quarter Sessions.</p>
        <p>The prisoner resembled the description of the man who has been in Warrington recently, stealing money from boys in the streets, and as the robbery VIRG also com- mitted in a similar manner, prisoner was placed among four other persons in the police station after the above case had been tried. Two boys who had been robbed were brought in, one at a time. The first one after sin:- ' veying the others fixed .his eyes upon prisoner bat failed to identify him. The second boy, however; at once pato:4&gt;d to the prisoner saying that helves the man who bad !rob- bed • him..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>RATIVG 01? SALT MINES AN D</head>
        <p>THE RATIVG 01? SALT MINES AN D</p>
        <p>SIIAFTS.</p>
        <p>UNITED' ALKALI; COMPANY v. GABSTAISCrU NION. This appeal respited from the April Sessiote vs: is tried at Proton Quarter Sessions.. before H. Wereley -Taylor, Esq., (te., and other Justices. The eaSP WWI of great Importance and • raised MA ny interesting: and obscure questions as to the correct method,of ratieg brine shafts and beds-of salt. Me. Edward Boyle of the. 8 Rah Eas- tern Circuit, specially retake (II and Mr. Smith appeared for the appellants, and Mr. D'Arey foe the. re Apoodents. From Ceunsel'S atatemen ti t a ppeared thattto appellants held leasee of a very large area of ee.lt in the Fylde dis- trict which they.are in process of developing The pre- sent make of salt-at their Fieetwood weeks to which the ,brine from the shaft is conveyed is abet* 4IC ',OW tons per annum, but it is their intention, to very' brgely extend their works and increase this output. The. t wine shaft and salt beds were assessed at -P,V2.111 gross,. s; rid £2,638 10'. rateable values.—Mr.. Flumphreya-F)asitei , surveyor and valuer of London and Manchester.. gave., videnee in sup- port of the Anpeal, and placed the' gee is value of the property at2.6lßiand the rateable 1r744 aat £412. and exptained theprinciplae whieh•in, his eg wen were ap- plicable to the retina at such. properer. , and the system adopted in the Cheshire sah district: it r the assessment of salt property., and his opinion, was confirmed by Mr. Oakes, valuer to the towneibito of Vire/ ford.—Mr. Cross, valuer, of Manchester,.gtere evidenes in support of the respondents' case, and placed the teable value of the property. at £9.1.11i and viae-suppohltel in his views by Mt. NeWtori.whose valuation.ammud en to £948 15s. The trial of the ease ocoupied the greaar r part of two days and aftersome considerationdha arned Chairman de- livered the judgment of. the auto; to the effect that the gross value of the property ahoniti be reduced to £639. and the rateable value to-25711-Utlli .• the appellants to , , have their. costs.</p>
        <p>Al P.</p>
