      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>PITIFUL CASE.</head>
        <p>A PITIFUL CASE.</p>
        <p>VAGARIES OF THE POOR-LAW.</p>
        <p>At the Prescott Petty &amp;maims on Tuesday, a tall, well made, and healthy-looking man wished to make applica- tion under the following circumstances. He, his wife, and two children were destitute. He had had no regular work since October last year. On Saturday night they obtained shelter in the vagrant ward at Present Work- house, but on Monday they were turned out and refused relief. His eldest girl had whooping cough, and his wife bad an abdisss on her face. He was told that the only way was for him to desert his family so that their wawa could be attended to, but he would not do that. He wanted work, and showed papers setting forth that he had been over nine years in one employ. Pie had since tramped the country through, and was 350 miles from home (Devonshire).—The Bench sent for the medical echoer, and Dr. Cousins, his deputy, attended and found that the facts as stated were true, also that the baby had a cold, and that they ought to have proper shelter, but he understood that the guardians refused to admit any able-bodied person.— Ihe Bench requested Superintend- ent Baxendalo to see tile relieving °Ulcer in regard to the matter.</p>
      </div>
