      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>TRIPS FROM WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE TRIPS FROM WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>TO BLACKPOOL. As is the custom in W arriugton the different works in the town trad their annual excursions to the seaside, the day following Walking Day. Hun- dreds of people might have been seen bustling to the Bank Quay Railway Station, before six o'clock on Satutday morning, with bag and buggage,intent on majojing their holiday. The weather, as had been the case the previous day was beautifully fine ; not a threatening cloud was to be seen in the skies. The place of destination for the majority of the works trips , this year was Blackpool, which has been designated the " Brighton of the North." Tne first excursion to leave the platform was that of the employ&amp; of Messrs. Rich- mond and Co., Ltd., gas engineers, with 700 trippers. The train left the station a few minutes fter six o'clock and maned its destination, after a quick journey before half-past seven. Quickly to tollsw it were the excursions ot Messrs. Bylands, Bros. Ltd.„ with 800 excursionists. The Whitecross wire and iron Co. Ltd., with a like nun.- ber, and Messrs. W. D. Houghton's Sankey Wire Mills with six hundred. It will be seen that nearly 3,000 Warrinetoniaus went to Blackpool, but these only formed a tithe ot the visitors, for that morning over thirty trains of excursionists arrived from all parts of toe country. On arrival, and after a little refreshment had been partaken of, the shore is made for and before long the bathing machiue owners are having . a busy time. Other trippers ate pacing the promenade, a walk of °vsr two milessor pa tronisiug the eiectric cars. or arranging for a drive. Dssvn on the sands below are the donkeys driven along by screaming girls and risen, with an occasional tumble perhaps, while men dressed as sailors are soliciting' customers tor a rowing excursion. Yonder is the oyster man with his customers, who are satisfying their cannibal propensities by eating " natives" whilst further on are some youngsters dispensing with their esppers on ice cream. The " pro- fessor" also is now prepared to read your bumps either publicly or privately, whilst the ventriloquist is "just agoin' to commence." Near to is the blind man, with his inharmonious organ and singing, sometimes with an occasional stoppage to say " Thank ye', sir." Others are marching down the piers where the whistling steamers are ready to take the excursionists on hourly trips on to other wiseriug-places in the vicinity. Pre- sently the tide comes in and the promenade becomes crowded. The most striking landmark of Blackpool in the near future will be the Eiffel Tower, now in course ot erection. The tower is now nearing completion, and attracts considerable .attention from visitors. In the the afternoon the palace and the Winter Gardens are well patronised, to which the excursionists are admitted at reduced prices. After tea, a look rotind the town is male to buy romething for " our Billy and Polly." Another visit to the sands is madetand then the trippers, well-tired out, wend their way to the station, and after inquiring fortheir trains from those ever-Sivil porters, they entrain, and depart punctually half-an-hour later than the advertised time. Happily, the day was spent without any serious accidents to Warringtonians., al- though one excursion train, which followed a Warring- ton train, met with an unfortunate accident at Poulton, which delayed the excursion train of Messrs.Richinonces employes, they arriving in Warrington early next morn- ing. WINDERMERE. • The employes at Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co.'s Iron Works, held their annual trip on Saturday, the place of resort being Windermere. There were about 500 tickets sold. 'The excursion left Warrington at 6.45, and arrived at Windermere ais 11.45, the country from Lan- caster to Windermere being much admired. The wants of the" inner man" were well catered for, there being numerous places of refreshment near the station. After partaking of refreshments, the trippers at once pro- ceeded to examine the beautiful' scenery with which this part of England abounds. A few went by coach from the station to Ambleside, but the majority of the visi- tors went down to time lake, which is about it mile and a half from the station. The lake which is 13 miles long and about a mile wide, is a splendid piece of water. From Bow ness, steamers go to mind fro at frequent inter- vals to Ambleside aud also to the gout h end of the lake. A large number of the excursionists; however, preferred to go by boat, as by that moans they could visit the dZeretit points of interest as well as Am t leside. AintSe- side village, which iS about half a inile from the lake is a very pretty village, and neir by is the Stock Ghyll Force, a waterfall, a view of which is in itself, warth the journey to Ambleside. From here there were numerous drives to Grasmere,Hydall Hall,which was the residence of the poet Wordsworth, and other places of interest. The rsturnjournerjeoinmenced at 7.45 p.m „Warrington being reached without any notable incident at 11 p.m. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>HDYL AND LLANDUDNO.</head>
        <p>HDYL AND LLANDUDNO.</p>
        <p>_ Between seven and eight hundred persons, the greater number being employes of Messrs. J. Crosfield and Sons' Soap Works, Bank Quay, journeyed to Shy! and Ltan- , dudno on Saturday morning. The choirs of St. Peter'sl Church, Oughtrington, and St. Mary's Church, Bank Quay, Warrington, also accompanied the excursion. It should be mentioned that the whole of the workmen at Messrs. Crosfields' were each preseuted with an excur- sion ticket by the firm. The excursionists departed from Bank Quay station at 7.30. Some of them alighted at Hhyl, but the majority went t) Llandudno, and arrived there a little before ten o'clock. The principal places of inteiest at this queen of watering-places were visited. Many took advantage of the sail to Bangor and Menai Bridge, whilst others gathered in the Happy Valley on the Ureat _Oruro Bead, where the minstrels gave one of their drollish performances. After a most enjoyable day's outing the return journey Was commenced, and the party safely arrived bank st 10.15.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>NEWTON RACES.</head>
        <p>NEWTON RACES.</p>
        <p>As in former years, the days on which the New- ton race meeting is held, was observed as a general holiday in the district, with the exception of the printing works of Messrs. McCorquodale and Co., at Newton, which continued at work as usual. The weather was of a beautiful description, and pre- sented a pleasant contrast to the three previous years, when the elements have been decidedly unfavourable. Consequently the influx of visitors was phenomenally large, more especially on Saturday, the cup day, when the crowd was greater than had been known for many years past. There was tho usual contingent of the rough element, and from what is said the pick pockets were extraordinarily lively, several persons complaining of the loss of their watches. One local gentleman in a good position, was relieved of a leather wallet, containing aitout £BO. A good many people belonging to Earles- town left by train on Friday and Saturday' for various places, but a larger number remained at home. The following are some of the polico results of the races : Fill DAY.</p>
        <p>A special court was held at the Earlestown police station on Friday night, the magistrates present being Mr. T. H. Pearson, and Colonel J. L. Wood. RICOVERY OF A Warm—Four individuals named Horace Beaumont, Win. Roberts, John Brown, and Wm. Pierce, were arraigned on a charge of stealing a watch and chain of the value of £1.5, the property of George Beardsley. A plain clothes officer said he saw the pris- oners on the race course, acting in a suspicious manner, and attempting to pick the pockets of several people. , With the assistance of other constables he arrested them and ou the way to the police station, one of the pris- oners put Ilk hand into his pocket, with the evident int..utarin of passing something. The officer seized his hand, and found the watch and chain referred to in his possession. The 10AS was afterwards reported by the owner, to the police. The prisoners made various excuses but they were all remanded in custody until Newton Petty sessions, to-day (Saturday). • Joseph Coulson was cliarged with playing the three card trick at Newton. A plain clothe officer said he saw the prisoner playing • the three cant trick: He arrested him, and when searched he had 28a.. in his pos- • session. Fined 20s. including costs. George Reade for the same offence was mulcted in the same sum. He had only 43. in .his possession, and he asked the magistrates to be as lenient as they could with SATURDAY. • .</p>
        <p>William Johnson was tined 203. for the three card trick I betore Mr. T. J. Gillespie and Col. .1. L. Wood.—Joseph Coulson who was fined the previous day, again fell into the hands of the police for the same offence. and was ordered to pay a line of 20s.— John Gavin, for the same offence, said he had not been '.playing, he had only been betting. A plain clothes offi- cer said he saw the man playing what W as known as the three card trick, . He gut hold •f him in the act of throwing the cards,and on searching him found 15.4.10 d. in his possession. Fined 20s.—George Heade, who had been fined the previous day, was again brought up for for the Mille offence. A plain clothes officer said he saw him playing the three card trick. He took him in custody, but his mates got the money. Fined 20s. William Worsdale,eharged with having played a game called box and numbers, was fined 20s. In default ot payment he was sent to gaol for 14 day s.—Thomas McGowan, for playing a game with box and dice, was fined 204., and in default. was sent to gaol for 14 days. Hugh Cuiloglman for playing • with a spinning wheel, being a game of chance, was fined 20.5., and in default was committed to gaol for 14 days. He said he was very hard up. —Walter Middleton, for the same offence, had to pay a similar bum. James Vernon was charged with being drunk whilst in charge of a horse and wagonette, and further with cruelly ill-treating the horse at the canto time and place. For being drunk he was lined 53. and costa. Lie pleaded not guilty to the .rher charge. Sergeant BirneA, of Widnes, said about three o'clock that afternoon he saw the defendant in charge of a one-horse wagonette in front of time grand stand. lie was drank ar.d thrash- ing the horse with a whip, about the need and ears, arid galloping into the crowd. Witness seized the horse's brad and directed two constables to get him off the bex,- and they had considerable .difilculry in doing so, and when got olf lie resisted ;ill the way to the Police tita- tiOn, lie treated the horse brutally. Inspector Thom. H.S.P.C.A., tVimman, said he was at the entrance t.ci the racecourse, ancr saw the defendant driving up the road. He was thrashing the horse with a drop thong whip about the head. Witness examined tne horse and found it covered with weals on the off side. P.C.'s Bamforth and Cummings, of Widnes, corroborated the evidence of the previous witnesses,and the defendant was fined 20s. and costs.</p>
      </div>
