      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Mao to the Vidor.</head>
        <p>Mao to the Vidor.</p>
        <p>ME EDUCATION QUESTION AT EMILE*</p>
        <p>TOWN.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln your issue of the 30th ult., you published a letter written to Mr. Edwards, of 9, Priticess-st., Ea ries- town, relating to an application for tree schooling. With regard to the case, will you allow me to state, that the children of Mr, Edwards were admittal free t the upper department of the Wesleyan day school, on the 12tb. of December, 1892, and from that time to tart pre- isent, they have attended without payment of fee.— Yours truly,</p>
        <p>GEORGE BURGESS.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Day School, Earlestown, sth July, 1,893.</p>
        <p>THEOCRACY, HIEROCRACY OR DEMOCRACY? Sir, —Whilst sympathising with your correspondent John Jones, in his tirade against Kingcraft, I cannot see the farce of the reasons he gives for his contention. Like many others I see the ludicrous absurdiiy of a people, who claim to be self-ruled, paying enormous sums to gild a dummy figure-head bowing down to it and calling it " Majesty." We have not got to the supreme height of calling it " sacred majesty" yet--but this may come with a slight increase of national sycophancy. With Mr. Jones I think this to be a wicked waste of public money. But what wind he have? What alternative scheme has he to pr pose ? Would tie substitute a theocracy for our prese•it sham monarchy ? By-the-way, the verdancy of the man is very refreshing who can believe that there ever was such an institution as a theocracy ! The theocracy he talks of would be better described as Priestcraft—it was neither more nor less than a Hierocracy—i e. Priest-rule. Now would he have us revert ta this; and hare his Grace of Canterbury, assisted by " General " Booth, as interpreters of the " Divine Will " ? Bah ! How much removed from a Demonocraey this would be I leave your readers to imagine. Mr. Jones' idea of the origin of the kingly institution will, I think, not meet with very general acceptance with students of history. Rather will they regard kings as a phase of national evolution. The greatest warrier —and biggest robber—becomes the head cf the ttioe and eventually the kingships, if not the kingly qualities, becomes hereditary. So Kingcraft in certain stages of national existence is justified by its necessity and advantages. I feel sorry that your correspondent can- not treat this subj.:ct without dealing in " clap-trap." Take that int about the ownership of land. ' Surely he does not believe the " Torniny-r-t " I that those born upon the land have thereby acquired any title to it ? " Men,fleets,and armies have to be main- tained that the glory of Royalty may stand high at home and abroad." Now, so far as Britain is concerned, this is pure puerility—simply " bosh." The declaration of war or the maintenance offered is now in the hands of the electors of England. and it lies with them without reference to their limited Ruler. Again, whilst deplor- ing with Mr. Jones the undoubted evils from which our people are suffering I think the remedy is largely in their own hands by the exercise of their legal rights. I dislike the political Micawberism that is always "ex- pecting something to turu up "—somebody to " (10 something for us." If the people of England will only be self-reliant, learn to rule themselves individeally, learn to mind their own business more thoroughly-, be just and do justice, they may then dispense with kings and kingscraft. At present we are in the curiously anomalous posi- tion of being a democratic people who are afraid of Democracy. We cannot trust ourselves. It may be self-knowledge that creates this distrust. Anyway it wilt be well to await the time when our Democratic rulers—i.e. ,the people,acquire the elements ot a political education, and can rule themselves. When this politi- cal millenium does arrive, we in England have alreaev in active operation, the legislative machinery to act in place of the expensive sham now disgracing the realm. I use the word "disgrace" advisedly. They who are busy- ing themselves to celebrate Royal hapments are, in effect, celebrating the fact that Englishmen are not yet sufficiently civilised to govern themselves ; or if they are so, are sufficiently foolish to pay extravagantly for another to pretend to do it. Viewed in this way 1 cer- tainty think with with Mr. Jones that "it would be neither wise nor patriotic for the working c!aises or the country to make jubilation over the Royal weddine— I am, ke., _ _ _</p>
        <p>JOFIN PEERS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>"BETTERMENT'</head>
        <p>"BETTERMENT'</p>
        <p>sir.--l--The London County Council is to he congratu- lated upon the perseverance it has shown in insisting that the principle of " Betterment " shall be admitted in any public improvements they make with the tax- payer's money. That principle as put in their re-eint Improvement Bill is as follows :—" Where it is clearly shown that property adjacent to orin the neighbourhood of a public improvement, carried out at the public ex- pense, is substantially and permanently increased in value by such improvement, it is just that the proportionate contribution towards such costs ehould be equitably levied on the property so improved." ' The only objection that was urged against this in Parliament was that it was impessible to assess the value cf any pablic improvement to any particular pro- perty, but those who argued thus must have Nem well aware that a private owner would have no difficulty in so doing and raising the rent accordingly ; and what can be done by a private owner can surely be done by an official assessor especially when we remember that if anyone improves his own property, that assessor very soon notes the improvement. Mr. Kiinber in opposing the motion said that" It was the first step towards the Henry George principle that all increment should be ap- propriated to the services of the This does not affect the justice of the proposal, though ir may affard proof Of the soundness of Mr. George's plan. ft must he remembered however that Mr. George tines not advocate the appropriation cf" all incremsnt" but merely of the increment in value of land caused by the community, and he maintains the sacredness of private ownership of improvement maie by the owner himself. This is the difference between the followers of Henry ileerge and the Socialists, and Mr. Kimber was treading on very dangerous ground when he endeavoured to make out that there was no difference between lard and</p>
        <p>_ --capital. It ought not to he neces;ary f.)r any local body to have to apply to Parliament for permission to apply this principle of " Betterment." M was pointed out the debate this is a part of the larger question of the taxa- tion of land values—apart from buildings and improve- ments, Had the various localities been allciwed to tax land values they would have found themselves in a much more satisfactory financial position, while the people would have housed in a very much better condition thaia now.</p>
        <p>Looking at it from the financial peint of .view and • dealing with urban land only, we fiod that while tho value of that land has increased enormously during the past twenty years,--owing in a grest measure ;.. the money expended by the ratepayers in publie imerove- a:Lents—yet, since the ratepayers have not been allowed to tax land values, though the rates oe purely urban :Paled have increased nearly eleven millions since 1866, the local debt has increased from £92.820,100 in 1874,t0 £201.215,458 in 1391, and of this latter amount £183,915,189 is on purely urban Lind. The onlv. so- called " relief " that has been given to the ratepayers has been by the system of grants-in-aid from the lrepe- rial Exchequer. But this does oat even mean the traits- erring of a portion of the burden of local taxation from the individual as ratepayer to the individual as tax- payer ; it is practically a gift to the ground landowner or lantkowners. This has been admitted in regard to rural lands, and the same holds good as regards urban hind also. A striking example is afforded bYthe United States in relation to the district of Coluinbia; in which is situated Wasnington,where Corigrese is held. The Fede- ral Government makes grants- -aid of the loc:i.1 taxa- tion el the district, and recently a .committee of the House of Representatives inquired into tWttxation of the district and the effect of the grants-in-aid mien by Congress. After a careful examination of all classes of witnesses, including the representatives of the owners of house property there, the commirt, f‘ says :—" If the na- tional Government were to assume the entire cost of the district Government, no one would he beneetted except the landowners. They could and would stiil demand from tenants the full rental of land reeardless of the remissions in the assessment en it. and the effealtof thie net increase in the profits of larelowning would bets raise the selling value of land and to greatly stimulate land speculation. Thus the effect of enehlitterality to- wards the Federal district on the part of Congress would ultimately be only to increase enormoeslv a few large fortunes and to drive a greater number of citizens into narrower quarters and make it More difficult for them to live. And if while refraining from, assessing land values, still more lavish approDriatiens were made from the proceeds of general taxation for time pury,ese of jai- provingand embellishing the national Capital, tL effect would simply be to increase a few great fortunes and to hasten the crowding of the batty of its population into tl,ts and tenement houses, and behind idately avenues, lined with palaces, to raise noisome alums." The Financial Reform Association sent a copy or thia report to each member of Parliament, and no doubt, it did good work in assisting the adoption of the Bill. A few copies are still on hand and ear, be had upon applica- tion. The Committee further declared that " a tax on lend values was the only just tax," though I would point out that, as was clearly put by the "Manchester Guardian" in its leader on" Betterment," that this is really not a tax at all, being simply the taking back br the community of a value achled to land hy the (par munity. Now as to the value of this 'tax ' la regard to the Lousing of the people. It is a startling fact that if tin</p>
        <p>• -</p>
        <p>owner of land in or near a town refuses to let the whole of his land be put to use, he can frequently get as much for the cse of a portion as for the whole. The people can afford only a certain amount fur rent, and their comfort depends on the amount of land they get in exchange for this. If there is plenty then for a certain sum each family will be able to get a house and garden but if the owner withholds a portion of his land from use, with a view to increased future value, then for the same sum. each family will only get the house without the garden. If still more be kept out of use then for the same :Inn only one or two rooms in a house can be had. If howelror land be taxed upon its value, then it will be out of any owner's interest to keep land which is wanted out of use, since he will in that case have to pay taxation on that which is bringing him in no rent. At present we tax the man who puts his land to use and allow the man who keeps his land idle to escape. The man who builds a house or a mill is a public benefactor, and should not be taxed for so doing, while the man who witholds land from use is, to that extent, injuring the commuKity and should be taxed for so doing. Every worker from the biggest manufacturer down to the poorest man or woman living in our slums would be benefitted by the taxation of land values, and I hope that the different municipalities will bring such pressure to bear on the Government, that it will be forced to give the various localities power to avail them- selves of this form of raising a revenue.—Yours faith- fully. EDMUND E. MUSPRATT, president. Financial Roform Association, flackins Hey, LiverptiA, July 3rd, 1893.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>NOT FS ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOT FS ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>AfHLETICki.</p>
        <p>CRICXF.T.</p>
        <p>Cricket maLtiea were about as scarce on Saturday, as showers&amp; ruin have been recently. This no doubt was the result of the "trips," or as our Cockney friends would call them, " bean feasts" ; for cricketers, like other mortals, oft-times require to have their vital mechanicisql oiled by a change of air aud surroundings.</p>
        <p>By the way, some cricketers should have been made fiction writers. It seems on Monday evening, about 6 30,a heavy tall of rain occurred in the vicinity of Man- chester Road, while there were occasional flashes of lightning and rumblings of thunder. There was no rain falling in other parts of the town. Well, a certain mem- ber—perhaps not a player—of the Warrington Cricket Club, told a number of friends that he saw the rain. He was driving to Manchester in a dog cart. The shower came from the direction of Warrington, and kept 20 yards behind him all the way to Manchester. fie wouldn't have got wet the least bit Only his dog, which was in the shower behind, showed signs of drowning, and he had to stop to pick it up. But he got ahead of the shower again presently. Well now, yes can believe that if you like, but when it comes to swallowiug any- thing big,l prefer laudanum or prussic acid,or something more palatable than that wheeze.</p>
        <p>The game ola3-ed at Arpley on Saturday between our first eleven and Broughton, was an enjoyable one. its chief feature being good batting. For five wickets, War- rington compiled 145 runs. Is that not a treat after some scoring that has been exhibited of late! S. M. Crosfield was in excellent form. His score amounted to 47. Clarke ran 28 and Smith 25. Griffin and Sugg :nade up the balance. For eight wickets and two players not out. Broughton compiled 172. Practically speaking, of course, they were beaten—at least to speak in political parlance, it was a "moral victory" for Warrington,</p>
        <p>ilia batting by Warrington constituted a great con- trast with their play last week,for it will be remembered t)Lat instead of players going in for double figures, they were chiefly content with duck's eggs. This only goes to prove the blessed uncertainty of things in general.</p>
        <p>By the way, I see there has teen a considerable row raised amongst the admirErs of Cambridge and Oxford, in consequence of one of the latter team indulging in " mean " play, or what the " Daily News " is pleased to call casuistry. Cambridge had made 182; just before the time for drawing wickets, Oxford had one wicket to fall, and was within four or five runs of the 80 needed ta follow on. It was policy for Oxford to follow on. The two batsmen who were in were seen to exchange re- marks, when Wells, the bowler, perhaps, as the "Daily News" said : " lead by the devil and the Laird of Lee," went in and bowled such dreadfully furious wides that a tumult of disapprobation arose. Oxford did not follow on, and the wicket was drawn. There is an awful fuss going on about this, and probably some rule will come into vogue dealing with this not illegitimate, but de- cidedly discourtious play.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>CYCLING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING.</p>
        <p>Warrintr,n cyclists can be fittingly desct:hed " scattered " this week. Like the-innocent butterfly, they have been flitting hither and thither, bathing in seas of ectacising pleasures, forgetful of the troubles of work-a-day life, and drinking copiously of enjoyment and—and--Oh, its all right, never mind what they drink.</p>
        <p>The two detachments of the W.C.C., who left last week for North Wales and the Wye Valley, are expected to arrive home on Saturday. Eight el them went to ihe Wye Valley,and ten to North Wales. I hope the rumour, about one of them endeavouring to scale Snowdon on hiS machine is not true. It would fatigue the csmber if be did it.</p>
        <p>Last Saturday- thoso riders who were not away from home went to Cr:maga again. Think of it : Went to Cranage again. By the way, I would just like to ask them if they encountered that frail young man whom they met last year, enquiring : "Oh ! Is this Kwanage Row bewetly aw-ful." Anyhow, if they did not see him they would see the ;MIMI trees and, well, similar teas,and Lbove all, the same entertainers.</p>
        <p>In August, the Secretary, and probably several ethers are going down into the Midland Counties for a ten day's tour. This will be nice, but I would remind them that when going through Stratford-on-Avon they must be careful and sing no comic songs, save Shake- spere's, talk about nothing but the oh i Bard, and treat nothing which has been his with undue levity. Or, beware ! for there will be terrible consequences to reckon with :</p>
        <p>T. A. Edge, of Manchester, had a rough time of it whilst endeavouring to beat the 1,000 miles record. The record stood at five day a eleven hours. Edge beat this time by five hours. The ride was done under great difficulties : on the latter half of the day rain fell heavily for several hours. which caused the rider to contract an internal chill, which affected tt* stomach so much that it refused to retain and digest any food which was given to it. in this state Edge had or. the second, thir; and fourth days to battle against adverse winds. The wcnder is that the rider did not give up the ride, but, once started, Edge would not relinvish the attempt. During over five day's ride helimul but six and a half hours sleep. At titniw in the ride. he became so sleepy that be rode on to the grass at. the side of the road and fell off his machine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>NtiseELLAszeca.</head>
        <p>NtiseELLAszeca.</p>
        <p>MECTIt.O OF Sr. HELENS FOOTBALL CLUB..</p>
        <p>dimastk</p>
        <p>the eighth annual festival in connection with the i Police Athletic Society held on Saturday at Fairfield, there were from 108000; to 124000 spectators. For the first time the Liverpool police have lost the Poole Chal- lenge shield, the points boing London, a • Liverpool,? ; Satiord, 6 ; Warrington, 5.; Dublin. 2; and Manchester, ,2. Constable Robinson, of the Warrington Borough Police Force, was very successful. In the 100 yards ! flat race scratch (shield) he won the first pri while in the 440 yards Ist race scratch (shield) he came in second. He has, there secured two. valuable timepieces, , which have been since exhibited in the spectators' lake at the Police Station,. Constathe Robinson, who is lan excellent runner, has been warmly complimented by his brother cfßcers on his success.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of th 2 newly formed swimming club on Wedneay, the Mayor was made preeident of the club, and as t predicted last week, "Fair" Turner was mad -Caption, with a capita.' sub-captain in Mr. T. G. Grounds.</p>
        <p>I was sorry to heat,. however, that there is a litt frictkri grin., on between the club and the Baths Com. rnittee. Eliti hope it will soon be settled satisfactoriAy. It seems the Club want to have practices from seven. 'until eight on Thursday, but the Committee I under-- stand have rejected this proporition, and are will :lig Cut the meeting alvaild start at 7.30. Well, I hope they will 2k•ili the error 0 this, and do all they can to het ip a club which will he able to make or mar, to a peat extent, the public interest in swimming in Warringt, oa.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of the St. Helens Football) Club la.#.t week a resolution prohibiting the entrance oE cabs tv the would was carried, and this was considered by Mr. F. J. laennett, the hon, corresponding secretary, to be a want ot confidence, and he, therefore, sent in his resignation. An idea prevailed that when cabs entered the ground, persons got in without raying, and the finances suffered in consequence, but we are informed that the club has not lost.tialf-a-crown during, the season by this means. It is probable that. at the general meet- ing on Friday might the misunderstanding will be smoothed over, and that every person connected with the club will do his atmost to ensure suLcess for the com- ing season. Mr. J. Tyrer, financial secretary, has resigned on account of pressure of business and private reasons, but expresses his willingness to assist the club all he possibly ten.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>EXAMINER SATURDAY, J tiLl B, 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURDAY, J tiLl B, 1893.</p>
        <p>AMERICAN NOTE 6.</p>
        <p>(BY MR. 11. IV HITHAM.)</p>
        <p>The United States cover a tolerably fair patch of ground, and one is strongly reminded of this when sitting down to write about them. There are Englishmen and women who can form no concep- tion of the distances, when reference is made to , American cities. Would-be visitors to the World's fair actually write to secure quiet lodgings at places nearly a thousand miles away. The fresh water lakes occupy a lager area than Great Britain and Ire!and, and are said to contain nearly one half of all the fresh water on the globe.</p>
        <p>Speaking of the Big Fair, let it be as big a capital B as you may have at disposal, Mr. Editor, or any chance American reader will Le disgruntled. I think, judging from what I read, that visitors may venture to come now. All is peace. A few weeks ago this hardly seemed likely ever to be possible. Strikes amongst the waiters. Discord rampant in the committees, and as dead set towards the bottom of visitors purses were all one could read about. The note of alarm that was struck in the press has helped to preserve the life of the goose, so that the golden eggs may be gathered, alter all, by the Chicagoans.</p>
        <p>Just now the New England States are much exer- cised over two phases of civilised life—murder and heresy. I suppose murder is not at all uncommon in uncivilized lite, only it goes by another name, and the murderer is usually spoken of as a "brave." The civilised murderer is usually a coward, and is known as such. The murderers we have hal in Slassuchassets during the past twelve months have been cowardly and horrible beyond all, belief. .11eresy hunting seems to be getting as common as murder. I suppose there is a likeness between the two. If anything, I should almost say that mare der ot the intellect should call down as complete, and strong condemnation as mere murder et the body. The Presbyterians of the States seem to haTe a strong leaven of Calvinism iu. their composition. The way in which they have hounded Dr. Briggs out of their body will certainly do 'much towar4s bringing this kind of paltry persecation to an end. Dogma and bigotry seem to be making a desperate struggle just now, trying to use the mop against ' the rising tide of thought. It seems the doctor was unable to reconcile the 250 years old Westmin- ster confession with present day knowledge. He had also made the awful mistake of daring to read their first five bool.s of the Bible with a pair of Colenso spectacles. I think he will be able to bear up under his troubles.</p>
        <p>Plutocracy in the States is leading right up to ! what it always has done since the world has stood. I noticed during the presidential election, that quite a number of colege professors were go- ing for Cleveland. This was a scholars protest !against the dishonest tariff system of the Republi- can party. Professor Brown, of Boston, has writ- ten the following,—" In a very important sense the respectable class is the dangerous class in the community. By its examples it degrades the social conception of the meaning of life, and thus materia- lises, vulgarises, brutalises the public thoughts. Also by its indifference to public duties, it con- stitutes itself the guilty accomplice of all the enemies of society. Professor Hyslop of N.J., thus writes : "The great clamour of to-day is against the unduly large wages of the wealthier class. As a class they can hardly be surpassed for selfishness, lying, intemperate use of their money in self- indulgence, and indifference to the world's suffer- ing." Such utterances are cettainly to be taken as typical of the present tendency of thought towards the much scoffed at platform of (lie socialist party.</p>
        <p>I have before rue something that is very interest- ing, although not an American note. It is a list of wills proved since Christmas last, I think. One, Dame ninon, of London, died, and had the misfor- tune to leave behind her £1,617,170. Another pock' fellow, pretty well known in Warrington, was com- pelled to leave behind him £2,800,000, and 150 public-houses in Liverpool. if some people's theo- logy is correct S. A. B. W., would do with a few of these taps, just to moisten his tongue a little. I think I remember something about a W. H. Smith, a great monopolist news agent,ad leader of a party claiming to be scholars and gentlemen,leaving some millions of pounds behind him some time ago. Can anyone tell me how much he left for charitable pur- poses?</p>
        <p>There is great rejoicing just now in Boston. A home for aged couples has been, opened. A few right-minded people put their heads together and determined so far as they could,to put a stop to the cruel and outrageous system 0.t6 separating aged poor married people. It has always seemed to me a surprising thing that the people did not make this a burning question. It may be that the occu- paves of workhouses have been gaity of drunken- ness, have been thriftless, and so on, bat there still remains no reason worth listening zo for separating aged maples when the black day comes for them to seek help in their declining- yeass.</p>
        <p>I noticed some months ago, in the "Examiner," a note by" Merlin," I think, on the American prac- tice of ejecting tobacco juice. lam reminded of this by seeing an editorial in a Liverpool paper on the sante unsavoury subject. "Merlin," if I temeta- beraright,cast some doubt on the matter. Well,let us put it in this way. America is a, big place, and there itt ft big lot of juice ejected, het it is not the native Acterieen only who does the ejecting. My experience has been that wherever there is a crowd of workmen there is a crowd of chenverkr, And where 'there is chiming there is ejecting. Rs it not the every-day experience of all that at every corner and store where men and boys meet and stand, it takes a lady all her time to avoid acting as sea- ?' en American, works near me,all Irish !and Fins;"et G cannot take a tool up-fironti the floor without, unless careful, dabbling in OM filthy stuff. My son holdeliis present situation be,Nmuse the boas wanted a boy who was not always. covering the tools ith lirice_ A man works near me at a vice who does not even tern his head, het sends it all over the vice. A determined stand should be made by. workmem on this question of cleanAness.</p>
        <p>We- are Ihving at a time when. great social changesure. =ming about. I have- Jug 4 received tile report of the A.M.C. of the T.0.0.F. held, at Southampton, in which, 6 and that it is deeidiid to admit ladies as members. It is not clear-tome whether separate-lodges are to . be ohartere4l;,or whether the two 807-100- age to meet in, the same lodge roams. lithe latter iss to be the casejpredict utter and complete failure unless there is a great reform brought abol-sti kr the matter drink, smoke,, and talk. With us- in the states these matte are dune in much better style than I ever saw in•Fingland. No drinkiog plane is ever connected with the meeting halls. Itis most of the lodge have 'visited even smoking is prohibited, ' emcept on special occasions. The 'foresters have ladies courts.. The two different rout te have power to exchangs- visits on stated oz-,easions. All the lamer orderahave.Ladies Lodges„.artel have never heard of them taking back seatsnolitrits manage- ; nient is concerned. In principle •Canes in favour of the two sexes meeting in the saale room to discuss the businean which is certainly of entrench interest :to the woman, as to the man. li have never since came to America seen drink in either trades union' :meeting, ov in an Oddlellcws lodge,. I rememhta ?that some yeara ago, a few oft uaOddtellows di4 'Some little ta)k about buildirK4aliall in Warringten where aole Friendly societies,m-etl4 meet, matt ao be clear of the various unplsaimat incidents (-tin- nected with the public bw,use. Now you are thinkieg. ot replacing the old Pbliblie Hall with a new one, it seems to me that the temperance ;tarty and !he new Social Questions Vision might make enquiry amongst the leader&amp; of the trades =lions, and Fliendly societies, and see how far all the forces could he combined for ebbs furtherancesof this object. No party need mount its hobby home, but all strive for one object.4hat is, to make it possible for the workingman andwoman to hold their meet- ings in places where they tan, with a clar con- science. take their sons and daughtcvs. Most certainly were tin Warrington now,. I should hesitate before allowing my bon to enter an Odd- fellows Lodge through the public-house door, and I think most members of Friendly Sogiasties think . too mnch of their wives to allow them tasit through ' the usual two hou,rs of smoke, and drink of the lodge room.</p>
        <p>The Americon newspaper ileservPq special article all to itself. The way it talks disvra to, and panders to the lowest and most morl id feature in poor human. nature is pitiful in the extreme. I speak not of pa:+ers of the polio?, news type, bin of those that have the largest circulation, and deem themselves highly respectable. A prizefight will any time fill column+ of slugging slang, while a lecture can scarcely claim half a column. The way they go into details of private life, resurrecting all the misdeeds of a :ilea') past life, would ia England</p>
        <p>be sufficient to dam the paper at once. In the report of a murder trial now going on every detail is given for dramatic effect. Sensational headings and illustratiorrs that are simply disgusting appear each day. Ridiculous descriptions of the judges and counsel lre given such as would not be tolerated for a moment with you. The clothes they wear, with the shape and colour of their neckties. I should certainly give over having n paper if Border , trials were on often. I was amused at reading the report of a recent wedding at New York, in which the following details of the bride's trousseau were given. "The three under petticoats of white silk were trimmed to the waist with small frills with pinked edges, and the dainty flannels were trimmed with duchess lace. The stockings were of white silk and duchess lace, fastened with garters of silver and a diamond clasp. The garters were im- ported from Normandy. The linen was supported from the shoulders by silver chains."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL SANITARY AUIIIORITY. PENKETH WATER :RIPPLY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL SANITARY AUIIIORITY. PENKETH WATER :RIPPLY.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Rural Sanitary Authority was held at the iVorkhouse, yesterday. There were then present : Rev. G. F. Roberts (in the chair), and Messrs. A. Jolley, J. Houghton, Mather, Dignum, W. Houghton, Long, and Swinton, also Dr. Sephton (medical Aker), Mr. Owen (urveyor), and Mr. S. Sutton (clerk). Ur. R. Garnett attended as a representative of the Panketh ratepayers to give information respecting the Penketh Water supply. A DILEAMA.</p>
        <p>Mr. OWEN (surveyor), stated that on June 13th the Improvement Committee visited Cuerdley, and were met by the Overseers of Penketh and Cuerdley. It was decided that the Surveyor should write to the Corporation of Liverpool and also to the Corporation of Widnes, ask- ing each Corporation on what terms water would be supplied to the townships of Cuerdley and Penketh. The Surveyor then read a quantity of correspondence which had passed on the matter. The first was a letter to the Corporation of Liverpool asking on what terms water would be supplied from the main in Cuerdley,and also suggesting that a six inch main through Cuerdley and Penketh would be sufficient and that a three inch subsidiary branch from the main be required. The Surveyor next alluded to the communication he had sent to the Corporation of Widnes, asking for terms. In reply the Liverpool Corporation inquired whether the townships were within the district of supply of any Waterworks Company or Corporation. Mr. Owen had replied to this that Penketh was in the district of War- rington and Cuerdlvy in that of Widnes, but the reason for the application. was that the Authority were of the opinion that they might secure more advan- tageous terms from the Corporation which could best serve the township, notwithstanding that the towships were under the Water Act included in the district of supply of a particular Corporation. The Town Clerk of Liverpool replied that having regard to the provision of Section 16 of the Liverpool Waterworks Improvement Act, 1887, which provided that the Corporation should not supply water within the disZricts unless both Cuerdley and Penketh obtained provisional orders from the Local Government Board. Bolero that provisional order could be obtained the coy/sent of the Wiclues and Warrington Corporations would have to ho obtained.—Mr. ATKINSON (clerk to Liverpool Corporation) also suggested that as Warring- ton Corporation appeared to have had the matter under , consideration, there might be some difficulty in obtain- ' ing the consent, and he was not aware of any case which went the length of relieving a Waterworks undertaking from the restrictions placed upon them for the protec- tion of other authorities throrgh whose districts their works passed. In conclusion Mr. OWEN said that Widnes Corporation had not yet replied. He also said that the Town Clerk of Warrington had explained to him that the Corporation was desirous of serving Penketh and sug- gested that the two Committees should meet, and he (Mn. Owen) suggested that that Authority meet the Warrington Water Committee on July 26.—A short discussion took place tspon the subject and it I was suggested that counsel be consulted upon the , vnatter.—Mr. J. C. Surrms suggested that this would be .simply a needless expense. It was absolutely necessary that a water supply should he obtained, and if they could nvt obtain the permission of the two Corporations, it would be advisable for them to consult the Local Govern- ment Board and it was probaibits they would do some- thing in the matter, especially in face of the provisions ()Utile Parish Councils Bill, which dealt comprehensively with water supply.—Mr. Owwts read a letter from Mr. James B. Aitkin cif Penketh, in, which was enclosed a resolution, passed by a meeting of inhabitants stating that urgent steps should be takem to procure a water supply and that the 3upply be not obtained from War- ringtere Waterworks.</p>
        <p>It was agreed to act upon Mr. tton's suggestion.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF COMMISSIONERS.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting was held at the Town Hall on Wednesday. Mr. S. Taylor presided, and there were present Messrs. P. Speakman, J.P., C.C., F. Wiswall, W. Fisher, H. J. Once, W. Ilandley, J. Pritchard, J. Meadowcroft, R. Abel, D. Bisbrown W. Humphreys ; F. A. Lake (law clerk), E. Mardian, (secretary), M. BarLer (surveyor). ADIEU TO SMALLPDX.—RHATY MORTALITY FROX DIARRERZA. The number of deaths registered during the past month amounted to 71. Three were inquest cases, and of the remaining 68, thirty were due to diarrlicra, I to varinla (smallpox), 2 to enteric fever, 1 to puerperal fever, 4 to mesenteric disease, 4 to phthisis, 2 to conges- tion of the lungs. 4 to inflaannatien of the lining mem- brane of the brain, 3 to debility, 2 to heart disease, 3 to convulsions and one each to arterial degeneration, blood disease, laryngitis, scarlet, fever, bronchitis, paralysis, congestion of the brain, malformation, senile decay, gangrene, cancer, and disease of uncertain origin. The seventy-one deaths showed an aveeage death rate of 42.6 for the twelve months and exclusive of the inquest cases of 40.8. The authority, added the Medical Officer, will be glad to know that the town is and has been free from smallpox for over three weeks, and that the two patients remaining in quarantine in the Isolation Hospital will be discharged within the next few days.</p>
        <p>LOCALISE DISEASE. The Chairman said this was highly satisfactory.—Mr. Bisbrown : flow does the town stand with regard to ether diseasea.—Dr. McDougall : Very well.—Mr. Zis- brown: The death rate was excessively high.—The Surveyor said in the corresponding month last year the death rate was 31.—Mr. Handley suggested that it would be an advantage if the doctor could see his way Ito divide the town into mortality districts, so that they might have the death rates from these districts. They would then be able to see where there was insanitary property that led to zymotic disease.—The medical officer said this would be a matter for the registrar ; it was not for him. The deaths were not confined to one place. The general death rate was about the average ;. the largo death rate was entirely due to diarrhcea, and the deaths were distributed all over the place.—Mr. Ilvmplireys said he took it that Mr. Handley did not refer to that report but to future reports, so that epi- demics might be localised.—Dr. McDougall said that would be dune in the annual repot presented that day. —Mr. Humphreys said the annual report came late in the day and asked could it not be in the hands of the Commissionees earlier.—Dr.McDougall said the prepara- don of the report involved great trouble, and it was got out as soon as possible. Such a report was never con- templated when he was appointed.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED CLOSING OF SCB-POST OITICE ON BAEZ EGLI- . ' DAYS.</p>
        <p>The General Purposes -C-o-tn.inittee reported a letter having been received from Mr. Orre%,, pestinaster, ask- ing the opinion of the Board as the governing body of the town, as to whether it would be considered a- public inconvenience if one or more of tke town sub-post offices were closed for the sale of stamps, &amp;c.,,0n Bank Holidays (collection of letters as at present), it is recom- mended that the Board advise the cloeing of such sub. offices on Bank Holidays.—Mr. Hankinson moved and Mr. Bisbrovro seconded the adoption of the minute which was referred back for further information as to whether such a course would be a public inconvenience or not.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>WIDNES TOWN 00UNCIL.</head>
        <p>WIDNES TOWN 00UNCIL.</p>
        <p>PIVZSENTATION MA...ORAL CHAIN.</p>
        <p>A special weetinw of the Widnes Town Council was held'at the Widnes Town Hall, on Thursday afternnon ; the Mayor (Aldermen. F. H. Homage), presiding. There was a full attendance of alder- men and councillore.</p>
        <p>The Mhyor said the Council had • been called together that day sea special meeting for '..special purpose, and he was sure it gave him very great pleasure in present- ing that day to the Council and bnrough of Widnes chain of Alai*" It, the Use of the Alayors- of Widnes: They (the Council) had been good enough ) examine the designs-of the chain, and he we very fortunate in obtainintheir at-quiescence to as. present design. He had also been very fortunate—through his daughter he must confess—in being able to cor.suinnome of the high- est art which cosid be bought through South Kensington and through ,the School of Art and Crafts in Bedford Park, Chi•wick.. London. He mightiadd that there had been gre:tt interest taken im the artistic vrorld of London in the making of the chain and the jewel which had not yet, been clinpbeedi hot which he heped. wouiti be ready in a fortnight.— Aldermen V,Aareivw moved the following resolution- -" 'flat this Council have very !mat pleasere in ac- cepting the gold- chain of office ang'e pendant which have this (laa been presented to the Cot-prat:on by the Hight Worshipful the Mayor (Alderman I. H. f36ssop, for the useof the. mayor for the time being of this borough, and hveby express theia- sincere thanks to his Wore l,ip or this-generous gift, and at the same time they deeire to, and do hereby, place upon 'record their appreliathin of the great care wlia;d, he- has. exercised in selecting the appreiate devices ;,-rriincrisiaig the staple industry to whish the borough owes its present posi- tion, which have seen approved and' enthedied by the authoritleaof Herald's College in ?nog:rent alarms made to the borough,. " —Alderman Ssdler seconded the resolntiom. which was, supported by CounaillOrs I.F. Neil, and 'levies, and carried Threl. hearty cheers were girsn.for the Mayor and Mayoress, and t'oetere the mernberia. resumed their seats they joined in singing " For lie's,a jpliy good fellow."— The Mayos• its reply said he had bestowed deal lot bihmar in choosing it. It haat! been a lahoer of love, but he did not think he had aoen lessehan 200 chains of oars. The chain had beene:Yecuted at the Chiswick &amp;lion! of Acts and Crafts', at Ledford Perk South Kensingl,on., and this would shoal theirs the artiatin work which those wham they termsd tholovrtir clamea could turn out.</p>
        <p>haG-.</p>
