      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>TO. DAISY ON HER, 1-.I4iST BIRTHDaI.</head>
        <p>TO. DAISY ON HER, 1-.I4iST BIRTHDaI.</p>
        <p>BY MELBOBS.</p>
        <p>Thou ramping, rosy, cbustuby elf, Thou tiny, mocking impudence ; Owning no mistress tuas thyself, Thy height in coppeas. six in pence ! Thou nilest with an ii,en rod, And I must act at Sity dictation ; Bowing submission to.thy nod, AS mighty empress.of a nation! Crowing and smilinzirkilly glee, To pull itiv hair aad slap my face ; Al SO it must be Sul to me, Though thus &amp;ended in disgrace. And every toy and, every doll Thrown at me with a roguish leer. net I may lilt the Mas they fall, And yield them up in very fear ; Thou little tyraut, thou woulci'st have, Despotic power o'er all the rest ; For each must Ls thy humble slaw And each must try to serve thee best. Why is it that we yield to Thee Respect and homage thus above All else and give thee fealty.— It is tor thee the dearest love! ily the hours go fleeting by , For thee and others by thy side, Hope one year old, will linger nigh, • And constant love will be thy guide. "happy returns," thou little one, We pray for every Natal day, That health and happiness alone ' May brighten all thy future way Warrington, 1893.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
