      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>TI1E: 'MATER WORKS STAFF.</head>
        <p>TI1E: 'MATER WORKS STAFF.</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee further submitted the-report of the sub-conunittee appointed to consider the organiz- ation of the Water Works Department and recommended its adoption. Office-staff :—the Committee findthat the following persons with salaries appended compose the office staff now in the employ of the Water Vi'orks Com- pany. Mr. Salkeld, secretary, £l5O ; Mr. Walter Jones, Clerb, £93 ; Mr. James L. Patin, collector £7a,• :Mr. J. Niffi.office youth, £2;94.. per annam, total, 'rhe duties appertaining to the staff of the Commis- I sinners are now performed by the following : Mr. E. Marshall, accountant and secretary, Entap, Mr. 1 J. D. Jones, colloctor £B5 ; office boy, £15,12. per ' annum; total, £301;125. After giving the question mature consideration the sub-committee have-come- to the conclusion that the duties connected with the-above departments if they were brought together and centred under one head would, be more efficiently and economi- cally performed thsu if kept separate, and the Com- inittea,recommend that Mr. Marshall be given, charge of the whole. The Committee believe that the following staff will be necessany and adequate for the performance of the 'whole of the Moles, and recommend the following appointments at the salaries named, such solarise to be subsequently apportioned to the various departments Mr. Marshall, accountant and secretarv/(increase £25 per armain), £225;: office assistant, £96 ; collector of waterand district rams and other accounts, En ; office youth. £3O; office troy per week, £1.Ti3.2.4. per annum, total, £4S3as, The above officials to ,devote the whole of their time to the duties of their respective office*. The salaries-oar paid to (tie two departments amount in the agne,r•nte to £648 123.. and the auove !arrangements will shoW a saving of £192 per annum.</p>
        <p>airticid &amp;qr.—The Gammittee recommend that the I present outdoor star:die taken over for the time being ; say engine d:iyerstat Runcorn well ; two stokers! at Ituncorn well ; onfeengine driver at Halton :and in spare time as litter ;one general fitter. The admanittee further recommend that the surveyor take charge of 1 this staff,and also the oukide management o2the depart- merit. Ctiie Aecommotittea.—With the increase-the staff it 111 he necessary to proyide at the Town Hall further office accommodation and the Committec-recommend . that the rooms now,occupied by the Overseers be utilised for the purpose and fe,as. information received, the Cmissuilttee have rastem to believe the Overseers would 1 not be unwilling to• vacate the same at an, early, date ; and the Committe.checapowered to take The necessary steps • to • carry out the recoinutendatiegs. , (Signed)i 11411kun Fkher :ehairunti), E. Wynne Huniplireys Jobe Meadovra.-oft,. and James Pr:Se/lard, . Fisher moved the arkytiou of the. ;sinutes. Mr. I ;Apeakman seconded, the motion and complimented the Committee upoiso their arduous labours. -44 r.. Handley ?feinted out tha.t aoceatuts amounting,to. £B4B ls. at I were recommetteled, tar payment, and there were no Pitrticulars-given, of these accounts, H, streested that I a list of the Ne.Z.AtuttNshoilld be published in the minutes, :as weed to se the custimo.—The Cliairnato said lie did not remember suclai: custom. They hajnothing to hiisa.l from tim.ratepayers hat he thought a detailed statement of the ace/mots-would occupy much waluable time. —10..1, Fisher concllreth —Mr. Handley said no more time would be occ.upica t ban at present, but each account would he- recorded wad reference could be made to it.—Xlr..l Speakmamsaid:the particulars of t'ee accounts were in, the hooks Handley sur,gesteckthat the Comrailtee should take back the repc.rt with regard to the water- works stealer' the members would like more time so coo- eider a report/ of such a—reachileg character. If they passed the report now they woald be tied as to the nun- of offitri,as.t,o. be appointed an their salaries., 1,0. one case additional work was cast r.fon an officer without an increase ossalary.—Mr. Fisher said after thz labeur the Committee had hail lutbe nialter he could net entertain Mr. Headley's proposal—Mr.. &amp;rice sod Mr. Humphreys sympathisea with Mr. Handley'a desire for further informattion.-11r. Handley proposed . ihat the report he referred taa Committee of the whole Baard.—Mr. Once seconded,. remarking that he should like the matter dis- missed, not necessarily for criticbm or faultfinding.— Me. Meadowcroft objected. Let them discuss it publicly.—Mr. Handley said his past life showed that he had never desired to laeep anything back from the ratepayers.—The Chairman was pleased with the report. He believed it would be beneficial to the ratepayers.— Mr. 1 iandley's arnendnier,t was I..)st by b votes to three. —The minutes were then adopted.</p>
