      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>PROPOSED PLEASI7RE tiROCNDS.</head>
        <p>PROPOSED PLEASI7RE tiROCNDS.</p>
        <p>The Secretary submitted a letter from Mr. George Marshall offering to give land at " Beaconsfield " for a pleasund ground on condition that the Commissioners pay the annual chief rent of £BB 14s. sd.—Mr. Meadow. croft inquired how many acreft.—Mr. Bisbrown : Mr. Pritchard said there were Iwo acres.—Mr. Humph- reys moved that the letter be referred to the Pleasure Grounds Coinmittee.—Mr. Hankinson seconded.—Mr. Bisbrown said it was an opportune time to deal with the subject. —Mr. Wiswell suggested that the committee consider the advisability a renting the field adjoining the cemetery for a recreation ground.—Mr. Pritchard said the committee haring charge of this matter had not made a Dublic appearance for some time.. (Laugh- ' ter.) The committee had made considerable progress in their efforts to obtain control of the cotnmon lands, but they could not proceed forthrr without trepense, and as the rate had gone sip to Zs. 10d. in the £ they had al- lowed the matter to rest.—The Chairman approved of obtaining the cemetery field —Mr. Wiswall said :f bad had a conversation with: a. gentleman who promised, If the field were obtained to contribute towards the cost.-I\llr. Meadoweroft sure-, ported the acquirement of the common lands, and said It was well to have a variety e( 34s.—The tlOtlcsaiwaa</p>
        <p>carried and it was understood that the Committee would deal with the whole question.</p>
        <p>A RESIGNATION. The Secretary read a letter from Mr. P. Smith resign- ing his seat on the Board, and on the motion ot Mr. Fisher seconded by Mr. Bisbrown, the resigns.tion was accepted. The Chairman instructed that Mr. Sinith would have come out in September, and it was decided not to fill the vacancy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>FATAL ACCIDENT ON THE 'SHIP CANAL,</head>
        <p>FATAL ACCIDENT ON THE 'SHIP CANAL,</p>
        <p>A peculiar accident. occurred at the Ship. Canal, at, Torn Paine's Bridge, Stockton Heath, on Sanday. Henry SneTwood, aged 16 years, of 17, Cheater-at., was going along the canal, and when he arrived at the syphon at Tom Pine' s Bridge, he appears to have crawled along it. The syphon, which is four feet in diameter, makes a sudden turn downwards after going about 50 feet, and goes under the bottom of the canal. When Sherwood had crawled to the turn,he fell to the bottoin,a distance of about 40 feet. A boy, who saw what had happened, at once gave the alarm, and a man was lowered down the ayphon by a rope, and the boy was pulled out. His injuries were so serious that he was at one. taken to the Warrington Infirmary, where it was discovered that he was suffering from a fractured arm and injuries tc the head. lie died on Wednes- day morning. INQVEST.-1, wrirNEsi COMPLIJIENTED.</p>
        <p>1, The inquest on deceased was held by Mr. S. Brighouse, at the White Halt Inn on Priday afternoon. Mr. Arthur I Newman, solicitor, Manchester, and Mr. Birch, resident engineer, represented the Manchester Ship, Canal Co., and Mr. A. Jolley was foreman of the jUry.-.--Henrc Sherwood raid that his son left home on Sunday after- noon with Abraham Stout. Ile next saw him at Tom Paine's Bridge, before he was taken to the Thflrmary. Deceased never told him how the accident occurred as .herwas unconscious almost to the time of his death. When witness examined his son's pockets he found a pair of bathing drawers in. them.—Abraham Abut de- posed that he went a wali . with deceased ana three ,other boys intending to go round • Walton Neches and then past Wilderspool Brewery. When they got to the ;branch of the Old Qttay Canal near the Arches,they had • a bathe,after which they wentalon,gthe Ship Canal. They then came near to the syphon pipe which goes, under the -canal. Deceased walked' in, only, slightly bending his head. When fie had gone about two yards he. cried "foul air,' and soon afterwards witness heard deceased make a noise against the pipe. Thinking something was wrong, witness went for help, and the man was lowered downrand pulled deceased out.—The engineer explained' that the pipe was 47: inches wide. mad the boy weulil have to walk al'yards before the pitse turned downwards. The depth he would then fall would be 59 feet 9 inches: tehn,NeSeall said that -he was informed t by-the last witnesa Of the" accident.., and went on, an ;engine to the pipe. He walked about 20 yarda-along ithe pipeandthen shouted. His heard a-faint mean. A trope was then obtained and witness fastened it toned , him, and'three menlowered him down the pipe. When Ihe got to the bottom; he fond that deceased had shot about six feet in the level. Witness examined hien, but found no bruises,thoughonturning himovtrthebegarato Eib'waaliaufed up and appeared to be in gteas pain during the operation. I% knees Stiund very Inti foul air in the pipe, but had stirred' a suit o clothes by going down.*Dr. Bowdem, house surgeon, at thrlnfirmary said the symtoms led hiss to believe that he had died ftom fracture of the skull. His hada brokru forearm, and both longs were bruised—The coroner did not think that anyone was responsible fertile accident. as the place where the pipes were was not a public walk —The jury after a short consultation rettrned a verdi c- of "accidental death,"aind thought great ztraise was due to the witness Newell for going down by the rope. They thought it was very creditable to him as ass Englishman. —The coronet' on behalf of the jury conveyed their admiration to Newall for his courageous act, 44 nd hoped that the Manchester Ship Canal Company would show their approval in a substantial manner.</p>
      </div>
