      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-07-08_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>M. HELENS. TOWN COT.FAIL.</head>
        <p>M. HELENS. TOWN COT.FAIL.</p>
        <p>INCREASE. IN THE RATES.</p>
        <p>At the monthly tweeting of the St. Helens Town Council On Wednesdny, the Mayor (hir. A. Sinclair) presiding, Alderman..l. C 'Oamble (chairman of the Finance Committee) proposed that a highway late of 9.1. in the pound, and a borough rate of 2s. 6d. in the pound be levied for the year esiding 31st March' 1854. The last borough levy was at the rate of 2s. Sd. for seven months, and the highway rate only 4d. for seven months. Less than a year ago (Alderman Gamble said), when he moved the laying of the rates, he stated that they had gone up, mad would continue to go up. There was rather a sad smile on the faces of members at that time, but he thought his prediction, had been verified, and he thought he might again say they would go oobincleasing, and would be more Lira% ear than this ye,tr.—alderman Bishop (chairman et tile Highways Committee) said the bulk of the highways expenditure was for permanent uner.iweinetits in the laying of granite paveinents.—The Mayor said be did not think suldimient stress had been •laid upon the very large proportion of expenditure which wa* not formerly levied by rates ti the Town Council. Twenty years ago they hada rate of 2s. now it was 3s. 3d. but in the Larger amount they had 3d. in the poutuu for county charges and 3jl. for police, both of which used to tie in the poor-rates. Then they had expenditure for which ' they ifet full value—namely, 3J. on the parks, D. on. libraries, and id. on the baths. These practically amounted to is in the pound of the as rates.—The minutes were confirmed._ .</p>
        <p>The proposed inereasein the solartes of the gas man- ager. (Mr. Glover) from £BOO to VOA 'tad of the techni- cal organiser (Mr. ?drumhead), from £lBO to £2OO, will come up at the next quarterly inviting. Coune.allor Hatton said £172 bad been expended on a trioto London. It was quite time they were looking into those " eteoteras," for het:amid not tell wheal they ' were going to, (Laughter.) It was quite time the rate- payers had a putllie ineetiug about spen4ing the rate- payers money like that. He thought d a shrine tn. spend tueuey as they were doing.—ALlermari J. C. Gamble said if hey 'WO 4 bill in Parliament they rouxt pay for it.—The Town-cierk said ic as netual •xperidit tyro for the members aval. officials, kin b.ad to pN, to Lon. ei.ol‘ to give evidenco,</p>
